// Rep Max questions: Physics (AQA 8463), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gph-001","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A car of mass 800 kg moves at 15 m/s. What is its kinetic energy?","o":["90 000 J","6000 J","180 000 J","12 000 J"],"a":0,"x":"Kinetic energy = ½ × m × v². So ½ × 800 × 15² = ½ × 800 × 225 = 90 000 J. Forgetting to square v, or forgetting the ½, gives the wrong options.","w":["","Left out the square: ½ × 800 × 15 = 6000, but v must be squared first.","Forgot the ½: 800 × 15² = 180 000 J, but kinetic energy is ½mv².","Just multiplied mass by speed (800 × 15), missing both the ½ and the square."]}
{"id":"gph-002","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A motor takes in 250 J of electrical energy. It wastes 60 J as thermal and sound energy. What is its efficiency?","o":["24%","32%","76%","132%"],"a":2,"x":"Useful output = 250 − 60 = 190 J. Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input = 190 ÷ 250 = 0.76, which is 76%. 24% is the wasted fraction.","w":["That is the wasted fraction (60 ÷ 250), not the useful fraction.","Divided wasted energy by useful energy (60 ÷ 190) instead of useful by total input.","","Turned it upside down (250 ÷ 190); efficiency can never be over 100%."]}
{"id":"gph-003","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these energy resources is renewable?","o":["Natural gas","Tidal power","Coal","Uranium for nuclear power"],"a":1,"x":"A renewable resource is replaced as fast as it is used. Tides keep coming. Coal, gas and uranium will run out because they cannot be replaced in a human lifetime.","w":["Natural gas is a fossil fuel, so it will run out and is not renewable.","","Coal is a fossil fuel and cannot be replaced in a human lifetime.","Uranium is a finite fuel, so it is non-renewable even though it is not burnt."]}
{"id":"gph-004","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A resistor has a potential difference of 9.0 V across it and a current of 0.30 A through it. What is its resistance?","o":["2.7 Ω","0.033 Ω","9.3 Ω","30 Ω"],"a":3,"x":"Resistance = potential difference ÷ current (R = V ÷ I). So R = 9.0 ÷ 0.30 = 30 Ω.","w":["Multiplied V × I (9.0 × 0.30) instead of dividing V by I.","Divided the wrong way round (I ÷ V) instead of V ÷ I.","Added the numbers instead of dividing.",""]}
{"id":"gph-005","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A 20 V battery is connected across a 4 Ω resistor and a 6 Ω resistor in series. What is the current in the circuit?","o":["2.0 A","5.0 A","3.3 A","8.3 A"],"a":0,"x":"In series, resistances add: 4 + 6 = 10 Ω. Current = V ÷ R = 20 ÷ 10 = 2.0 A. Using only one resistor gives 5.0 A or 3.3 A.","w":["","Used only the 4 Ω resistor (20 ÷ 4); both resistors are in the circuit.","Used only the 6 Ω resistor (20 ÷ 6); series resistances must be added.","Used the parallel combination (2.4 Ω), but these resistors are in series."]}
{"id":"gph-006","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A 12 V cell is connected across a 6 Ω resistor and a 3 Ω resistor in parallel. What current does the cell supply?","o":["1.3 A","4.0 A","6.0 A","2.0 A"],"a":2,"x":"Each branch has the full 12 V. Branch currents: 12 ÷ 6 = 2.0 A and 12 ÷ 3 = 4.0 A. In parallel the branch currents add to the total, 6.0 A. 1.3 A comes from wrongly adding the resistors.","w":["Added the resistors (12 ÷ 9) as if in series; parallel branch currents add instead.","Only counted the 3 Ω branch and forgot the 6 Ω branch current.","","Only counted the 6 Ω branch and forgot the 3 Ω branch current."]}
{"id":"gph-007","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"In a UK three-pin plug, which wire is correctly matched with its colour?","o":["Live wire: blue","Neutral wire: blue","Earth wire: brown","Neutral wire: green and yellow"],"a":1,"x":"The live wire is brown, the neutral wire is blue and the earth wire is green and yellow striped.","w":["Blue is the neutral wire; the live wire is brown.","","Earth is green and yellow striped; brown is the live wire.","Green and yellow is the earth wire; the neutral wire is blue."]}
{"id":"gph-008","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A kettle runs from the 230 V mains and has a current of 8.0 A. What is its power?","o":["29 W","238 W","14 720 W","1840 W"],"a":3,"x":"Power = potential difference × current (P = V × I). So P = 230 × 8.0 = 1840 W. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 29 W.","w":["Divided V by I (230 ÷ 8) instead of multiplying them.","Added the numbers (230 + 8) instead of multiplying.","Multiplied by the current twice; P = V × I only uses I once.",""]}
{"id":"gph-009","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A plastic rod becomes negatively charged when it is rubbed with a cloth. What has happened?","o":["Electrons moved from the cloth to the rod","Protons moved from the cloth to the rod","Electrons moved from the rod to the cloth","Protons moved from the rod to the cloth"],"a":0,"x":"Only electrons can move in friction charging. The rod gains electrons, so it becomes negative, and the cloth loses them and becomes positive. Protons stay fixed in the nuclei.","w":["","Protons are fixed in the nucleus and cannot move during rubbing.","Electrons leaving the rod would make it positive, not negative.","Protons are fixed in the nucleus; only electrons move when things are rubbed."]}
{"id":"gph-010","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A block has a mass of 0.60 kg and a volume of 0.00020 m³. What is its density?","o":["300 kg/m³","0.00033 kg/m³","3000 kg/m³","3.0 kg/m³"],"a":2,"x":"Density = mass ÷ volume. So 0.60 ÷ 0.00020 = 3000 kg/m³. Dividing the other way round gives 0.00033.","w":["Power of ten slip: 0.60 ÷ 0.00020 is 3000, not 300.","Divided volume by mass instead of mass by volume.","","Ignored the powers of ten in 0.00020; the answer is far bigger than 3.0."]}
{"id":"gph-011","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"How much energy is needed to heat 2.0 kg of water from 20 °C to 70 °C? The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J/kg °C.","o":["8400 J","420 000 J","588 000 J","210 000 J"],"a":1,"x":"Change in thermal energy = m × c × Δθ = 2.0 × 4200 × (70 − 20) = 420 000 J. The temperature change is 50 °C, not 70 °C.","w":["Forgot the temperature change: 2.0 × 4200 only uses mass and c.","","Used 70 °C as the temperature change instead of 70 − 20 = 50 °C.","Forgot to multiply by the mass: 4200 × 50 leaves out the 2.0 kg."]}
{"id":"gph-012","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A sealed container of gas is heated and its volume stays the same. Why does the gas pressure rise?","o":["There are more particles in the container","The particles get bigger and take up more room","The particles lose energy and stick to the walls","The particles move faster and hit the walls harder and more often"],"a":3,"x":"Heating raises the average kinetic energy of the particles. They hit the walls more often and with more force, so the pressure rises. The number of particles has not changed.","w":["The container is sealed, so the number of particles cannot change.","Particles do not grow when heated; they just move faster.","Heating gives particles more energy, not less, and they do not stick to walls.",""]}
{"id":"gph-013","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A bicycle pump is pushed in quickly and the air inside gets warmer. Which statement explains this?","o":["Work done on the gas transfers energy to the kinetic energy of its particles","The gas particles gain mass as they are squashed together","Energy is created because the pressure of the gas rises","The particles stop moving and release stored thermal energy"],"a":0,"x":"Pushing the piston does work on the gas. This energy increases the internal energy, mainly the kinetic energy of the particles, so the temperature rises. Energy is not created.","w":["","Particles don't gain mass; squashing only changes their spacing.","Energy is never created; work done on the gas transfers it.","Particles never stop; they move faster when the gas is compressed."]}
{"id":"gph-014","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":1,"q":"How does an atom of carbon-14 differ from an atom of carbon-12?","o":["It has two more protons","It has two more electrons","It has two more neutrons","It has two more protons and two more electrons"],"a":2,"x":"Both are carbon, so both have 6 protons. Isotopes have different numbers of neutrons: carbon-14 has 8 neutrons and carbon-12 has 6.","w":["Protons define the element; both isotopes have 6 protons.","Neutral atoms have 6 electrons in both; isotopes differ in neutrons.","","Changing protons changes the element; only the neutron number differs."]}
{"id":"gph-015","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A source emits radiation that passes through paper but is stopped by thin aluminium. What is the radiation?","o":["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","Neutron"],"a":1,"x":"Alpha is stopped by paper. Beta passes through paper but is stopped by a few mm of aluminium. Gamma and neutron radiation are much more penetrating.","w":["Alpha is stopped by paper, so it can't pass through it.","","Gamma passes through thin aluminium; it needs thick lead or concrete.","Neutrons are very penetrating and would not be stopped by thin aluminium."]}
{"id":"gph-016","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A nucleus of mass number 238 and atomic number 92 emits one alpha particle, then two beta particles. What are the final mass number and atomic number?","o":["234 and 90","230 and 92","238 and 94","234 and 92"],"a":3,"x":"Alpha: mass number −4, atomic number −2 gives 234 and 90. Each beta: mass number unchanged, atomic number +1, so two betas give 234 and 92.","w":["Stopped after the alpha; the two betas then add 2 to the atomic number.","Took mass off for the betas; beta decay leaves the mass number at 234.","Ignored the alpha, which removes 4 from mass and 2 from atomic number.",""]}
{"id":"gph-017","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A radioactive source has a half-life of 6 hours. Its count rate is 800 counts per minute at the start. What is it after 18 hours?","o":["100 counts per minute","200 counts per minute","133 counts per minute","400 counts per minute"],"a":0,"x":"18 hours is 3 half-lives. The count rate halves each time: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 counts per minute.","w":["","Only two half-lives used; 18 hours is three.","Divided 800 by 6; it should be halved once per half-life.","Only one half-life used; 18 hours is three."]}
{"id":"gph-018","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Which example is irradiation, not contamination?","o":["A worker spills a radioactive liquid on their skin","A person breathes in radioactive dust that stays in the lungs","Food is exposed to gamma rays to kill bacteria","Radioactive atoms get into a river and stay in the water"],"a":2,"x":"Irradiation is exposure to radiation from a source outside the object, and the object does not become radioactive. Contamination means radioactive atoms get onto or into something.","w":["Radioactive liquid on skin is contamination, not irradiation.","Dust staying in the lungs is contamination: radioactive atoms are inside you.","","Radioactive atoms in the water is contamination, not irradiation."]}
{"id":"gph-019","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a natural source of background radiation?","o":["Nuclear weapons testing","Cosmic rays from space","Medical X-rays","Waste from nuclear power stations"],"a":1,"x":"Cosmic rays, radon gas and rocks are natural sources. Weapons testing, medical X-rays and nuclear waste are human-made sources.","w":["Weapons testing is human-made, not natural.","","Medical X-rays are human-made, not natural.","Nuclear waste is human-made, not natural."]}
{"id":"gph-020","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes nuclear fusion?","o":["A large nucleus splits into two smaller nuclei","A nucleus emits an alpha particle","A neutron in a nucleus changes into a proton","Two light nuclei join to form a heavier nucleus"],"a":3,"x":"Fusion is the joining of light nuclei, such as hydrogen, to make a heavier nucleus and release energy. Splitting a large nucleus is fission.","w":["Splitting a large nucleus is fission, not fusion.","Emitting an alpha particle is alpha decay, not fusion.","A neutron turning into a proton is beta decay, not fusion.",""]}
{"id":"gph-021","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"An astronaut has a mass of 70 kg. The gravitational field strength on the Moon is 1.6 N/kg. What is the astronaut's weight on the Moon?","o":["112 N","686 N","70 N","44 N"],"a":0,"x":"Weight = mass × gravitational field strength = 70 × 1.6 = 112 N. 686 N is the weight on Earth (70 × 9.8). Mass stays the same everywhere.","w":["","That is the weight on Earth (70 × 9.8), not the Moon.","Mixed up mass with weight; weight is mass × field strength.","Divided 70 by 1.6 instead of multiplying."]}
{"id":"gph-022","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A box is pushed to the right with a force of 40 N. Friction of 15 N acts to the left. What is the resultant force?","o":["55 N to the right","25 N to the left","25 N to the right","15 N to the left"],"a":2,"x":"The forces act in opposite directions, so subtract: 40 − 15 = 25 N. The larger force is to the right, so the resultant is to the right.","w":["Added the forces; opposite directions must be subtracted.","Right size but wrong direction; the larger force is to the right.","","Gave the friction force; the resultant is the difference, to the right."]}
{"id":"gph-023","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A person pushes a crate 6.0 m along the floor with a constant force of 80 N. How much work is done?","o":["13 J","480 J","86 J","0.075 J"],"a":1,"x":"Work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force = 80 × 6.0 = 480 J.","w":["Divided 80 by 6; work is force $\\times$ distance.","","Added force and distance instead of multiplying.","Divided distance by force; work is force $\\times$ distance."]}
{"id":"gph-024","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":3,"q":"A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m. It is compressed by 12 cm, within its limit of proportionality. How much energy is stored?","o":["14 400 J","12 J","2.88 J","1.44 J"],"a":3,"x":"Convert 12 cm to 0.12 m. Elastic potential energy = ½ × k × e² = ½ × 200 × 0.12² = 1.44 J. Leaving out the conversion gives 14 400.","w":["Forgot to convert cm to m, so 12 was squared instead of 0.12.","Used $\\frac{1}{2}ke$ and not $e^2$; the extension must be squared.","Forgot the $\\frac{1}{2}$ in $\\frac{1}{2}ke^2$.",""]}
{"id":"gph-025","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A seesaw is balanced. A 300 N force acts 1.5 m to the left of the pivot. What force acts 2.0 m to the right of the pivot?","o":["225 N","400 N","600 N","150 N"],"a":0,"x":"At balance the clockwise moment equals the anticlockwise moment. 300 × 1.5 = 450 N m, so the force on the right is 450 ÷ 2.0 = 225 N.","w":["","Inverted the distance ratio (×2.0÷1.5); the larger force is on the shorter side.","Multiplied 300 by 2.0 and ignored the 1.5 m, so no moments were used.","Divided 300 by 2.0 and ignored the 1.5 m; moments need force × distance."]}
{"id":"gph-026","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":1,"q":"A force of 600 N acts at right angles on an area of 0.030 m². What is the pressure?","o":["18 Pa","2000 Pa","20 000 Pa","0.000 05 Pa"],"a":2,"x":"Pressure = force ÷ area = 600 ÷ 0.030 = 20 000 Pa (N/m²). Multiplying instead of dividing gives 18.","w":["Multiplied force by area instead of dividing.","Decimal slip: 600 ÷ 0.30 gives 2000, but the area is 0.030.","","Divided area by force, the wrong way round (pressure = force ÷ area)."]}
{"id":"gph-027","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A diver is 15 m below the surface of fresh water of density 1000 kg/m³. What is the pressure due to the water? (g = 9.8 N/kg)","o":["15 000 Pa","147 000 Pa","1470 Pa","14 700 Pa"],"a":1,"x":"Pressure = height × density × g = 15 × 1000 × 9.8 = 147 000 Pa. Leaving out g gives 15 000.","w":["Left out g; pressure = height × density × g.","","Slipped by two powers of ten, giving a hundredth of the true value.","Slipped by a power of ten, giving a tenth of the true value."]}
{"id":"gph-028","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":1,"q":"A runner covers 400 m in 50 s at a constant speed. What is the speed?","o":["0.125 m/s","350 m/s","20 000 m/s","8.0 m/s"],"a":3,"x":"Speed = distance ÷ time = 400 ÷ 50 = 8.0 m/s.","w":["Divided time by distance, the wrong way round.","Subtracted 50 from 400 instead of dividing.","Multiplied distance by time instead of dividing.",""]}
{"id":"gph-029","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A car speeds up from 4.0 m/s to 16 m/s in 3.0 s. What is its acceleration?","o":["4.0 m/s²","5.3 m/s²","0.25 m/s²","12 m/s²"],"a":0,"x":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = (16 − 4.0) ÷ 3.0 = 4.0 m/s². 5.3 comes from forgetting the starting speed.","w":["","Divided 16 by 3.0 and forgot to subtract the starting speed of 4.0 m/s.","Divided time by velocity change, the wrong way round.","Found the change in speed but forgot to divide by the time."]}
{"id":"gph-030","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A car moving at 8.0 m/s accelerates uniformly at 2.0 m/s² over a distance of 60 m. What is its final speed?","o":["304 m/s","15 m/s","17 m/s","11 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Use v² − u² = 2as. v² = 8.0² + 2 × 2.0 × 60 = 64 + 240 = 304, so v = √304 ≈ 17 m/s. Forgetting the square root gives 304; forgetting u² gives 15.","w":["Forgot to take the square root, so this is v² not v.","Left out u² (8.0² = 64), so only the 2as part was square-rooted.","","Used v² = as, missing both the 2 and the u² term."]}
{"id":"gph-031","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A 1200 kg car has a resultant force of 3000 N acting on it. What is its acceleration?","o":["0.4 m/s²","2.5 m/s²","3.6 m/s²","4200 m/s²"],"a":1,"x":"Force = mass × acceleration, so acceleration = 3000 ÷ 1200 = 2.5 m/s². Dividing mass by force gives 0.4.","w":["Divided mass by force, the wrong way round.","","Multiplied mass by force instead of dividing, then slipped on powers of ten.","Added mass and force; use a = F ÷ m."]}
{"id":"gph-032","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A book rests on a table. Which force forms a Newton's third law pair with the weight of the book (the Earth's pull on the book)?","o":["The upward contact force of the table on the book","The downward force of the book on the table","The friction between the book and the table","The book's gravitational pull on the Earth"],"a":3,"x":"A third law pair acts on two different objects, is the same type of force and is equal and opposite. Earth pulls the book down, so the book pulls the Earth up.","w":["Equal and opposite but a different type of force, and it acts on the same object.","Pairs with the table's upward push, not with the Earth's pull on the book.","Friction is not involved; a pair must be the same type of force.",""]}
{"id":"gph-033","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":1,"q":"Which factor increases a driver's thinking distance?","o":["The driver is tired","The road is wet","The tyres are worn","The car has a larger mass"],"a":0,"x":"Thinking distance depends on reaction time, which is longer when a driver is tired or has taken alcohol or drugs. Wet roads, worn tyres and a heavier car increase braking distance.","w":["","A wet road affects braking distance, not thinking distance.","Worn tyres affect braking distance, not reaction time.","Mass affects braking distance, but not how fast the driver reacts."]}
{"id":"gph-034","s":"physics","t":"4.5.7","lv":3,"q":"A 1000 kg car moving at 12 m/s hits a stationary 500 kg car. They stick together. What is their speed just after the collision?","o":["12 m/s","6.0 m/s","8.0 m/s","4.0 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Momentum is conserved. Before: 1000 × 12 = 12 000 kg m/s. After: (1000 + 500) × v = 12 000, so v = 8.0 m/s.","w":["Ignored the extra mass; the combined mass is bigger so the speed drops.","Divided by 2000 as if the cars were 1000 kg each; the total mass is 1500 kg.","","Divided the speed by 3, not using momentum before = after."]}
{"id":"gph-035","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A water wave has a period of 0.50 s and a wavelength of 0.80 m. What is its wave speed?","o":["0.40 m/s","1.6 m/s","0.63 m/s","2.0 m/s"],"a":1,"x":"Frequency = 1 ÷ period = 1 ÷ 0.50 = 2.0 Hz. Wave speed = frequency × wavelength = 2.0 × 0.80 = 1.6 m/s. 2.0 is only the frequency.","w":["Multiplied wavelength by period; speed needs frequency, which is 1 ÷ period.","","Divided period by wavelength instead of using frequency × wavelength.","Stopped at the frequency (2.0 Hz) and forgot to multiply by the wavelength."]}
{"id":"gph-036","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A ray of light passes from air into a glass block at an angle to the normal. What happens to the ray?","o":["It speeds up and bends towards the normal","It slows down and bends away from the normal","It speeds up and bends away from the normal","It slows down and bends towards the normal"],"a":3,"x":"Light travels more slowly in glass than in air, so it bends towards the normal as it enters the glass. Explaining refraction by the change in speed is Higher tier.","w":["Light slows down in glass, not speeds up.","Right that it slows, but it bends towards the normal, not away.","Light slows down in glass, and bends towards the normal, not away.",""]}
{"id":"gph-037","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A boat sends an ultrasound pulse down. The echo from the sea bed returns after 0.40 s. Ultrasound travels at 1500 m/s in sea water. How deep is the sea bed?","o":["300 m","600 m","150 m","3750 m"],"a":0,"x":"The pulse travels to the sea bed and back, so total distance = 1500 × 0.40 = 600 m. The depth is half of this, 300 m.","w":["","Forgot the pulse goes there and back, so the depth is half of this.","Halved twice; only one halving is needed for the round trip.","Divided speed by time instead of multiplying them."]}
{"id":"gph-038","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list shows electromagnetic waves in order of increasing wavelength?","o":["Visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays","Gamma rays, ultraviolet, X-rays, visible light","Gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible light","X-rays, gamma rays, visible light, ultraviolet"],"a":2,"x":"The spectrum runs: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma, from longest to shortest wavelength. Reading it backwards gives increasing wavelength.","w":["That is increasing frequency, so the wavelength gets shorter along it.","Swapped UV and X-rays; X-rays have the shorter wavelength.","","Jumbled order; gamma rays have the shortest wavelength of all, not X-rays."]}
{"id":"gph-039","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":3,"q":"What happens when radio waves are absorbed by the aerial of a radio receiver?","o":["A direct current is produced","An alternating current is produced with the same frequency as the waves","An alternating current is produced with a lower frequency","The aerial emits radio waves of a different frequency"],"a":1,"x":"When radio waves are absorbed by a conductor they create an alternating current in it with the same frequency as the radio waves, which the receiver turns into sound.","w":["Radio waves are alternating, so they make an alternating current, not a direct one.","","The frequency of the current matches the waves; it is not lowered.","Confused absorbing with emitting; a receiver aerial absorbs the waves."]}
{"id":"gph-040","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of electromagnetic waves are ionising and can cause gene mutations and cancer?","o":["Radio waves and microwaves","Infrared and visible light","Microwaves and infrared","X-rays and gamma rays"],"a":3,"x":"X-rays and gamma rays carry enough energy to ionise atoms and damage DNA. Radio waves, microwaves, infrared and visible light do not ionise.","w":["Both are non-ionising; they lack the energy to knock electrons off atoms.","Both are non-ionising; visible light does not damage DNA this way.","Both are non-ionising; infrared mainly causes heating.",""]}
{"id":"gph-041","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A red T-shirt is looked at in pure green light. What colour does it appear?","o":["Black","Red","Green","White"],"a":0,"x":"A red object reflects red light and absorbs other colours. There is no red light in pure green light, so the T-shirt absorbs the green and reflects nothing: it looks black.","w":["","Thought a red shirt always looks red; it can only reflect red light that is there.","Green light is absorbed by a red object, not reflected.","White would need all colours reflected; a red shirt absorbs green."]}
{"id":"gph-042","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":1,"q":"Which surface is the best emitter of infrared radiation at the same temperature?","o":["Shiny white","Shiny black","Matt black","Matt white"],"a":2,"x":"Matt black surfaces are the best absorbers and the best emitters of infrared radiation. Shiny and light-coloured surfaces are poor emitters.","w":["Shiny and white surfaces are both poor emitters, so this is the worst choice.","Black is good, but shiny finishes emit poorly; matt is better.","","Matt helps, but white surfaces emit less than black ones."]}
{"id":"gph-043","s":"physics","t":"4.7.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the magnetic field of a bar magnet is correct?","o":["Field lines point from the south pole to the north pole outside the magnet","Field lines point from the north pole to the south pole outside the magnet","Field lines are furthest apart at the poles","Field lines cross each other near the centre"],"a":1,"x":"Field lines leave the north pole and enter the south pole. They are closest together, and so the field is strongest, at the poles. They never cross.","w":["Direction reversed; field lines go from north to south outside the magnet.","","Lines are closest together at the poles, which is why the field is strongest there.","Field lines never cross, as the field has only one direction at any point."]}
{"id":"gph-044","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which change makes the magnetic field of a solenoid stronger?","o":["Reducing the current","Taking some turns off the coil","Reversing the direction of the current","Putting an iron core inside the coil"],"a":3,"x":"A solenoid is stronger with more current, more turns, or a soft iron core. Reversing the current only reverses the direction of the field.","w":["Less current weakens the field, it does not strengthen it.","Fewer turns weaken the field.","Reversing the current only flips the field direction, not its strength.",""]}
{"id":"gph-045","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A wire of length 0.15 m carries a current of 3.0 A at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.20 T. What force acts on the wire?","o":["0.090 N","4.0 N","0.90 N","0.010 N"],"a":0,"x":"Force = magnetic flux density × current × length (F = BIl) = 0.20 × 3.0 × 0.15 = 0.090 N.","w":["","Divided by the length instead of multiplying: B × I ÷ l.","Decimal slip; the answer is ten times too big.","Divided by the current instead of multiplying; F = BIl needs all three multiplied."]}
{"id":"gph-046","s":"physics","t":"4.7.3","lv":3,"q":"An ideal step-down transformer has 230 V across its primary coil and 23 V across its secondary coil. The secondary current is 4.0 A. What is the primary current?","o":["40 A","4.0 A","0.40 A","0.040 A"],"a":2,"x":"For an ideal transformer, Vp × Ip = Vs × Is. So 230 × Ip = 23 × 4.0 = 92, and Ip = 0.40 A. The step-down transformer has a smaller current in the primary.","w":["Thought step-down means a bigger primary current; it is the smaller one.","Assumed the current stays the same; only the power is equal, not the current.","","Power-of-ten slip; Ip = 92 ÷ 230 is 0.40, not 0.040."]}
{"id":"gph-047","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What was our Solar System formed from?","o":["Material thrown out of the Sun after it formed","A cloud of dust and gas pulled together by gravity","A collision between two galaxies","A black hole that cooled and broke up"],"a":1,"x":"The Sun and planets formed together from a huge cloud of dust and gas (a nebula) that was pulled together by gravity. The planets did not come from the Sun.","w":["The planets did not come from the Sun; both formed from the same cloud.","","Galaxy collisions are not how the Solar System formed.","Black holes do not break up into stars and planets."]}
{"id":"gph-048","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which sequence is correct for a star the same size as our Sun?","o":["Protostar, red giant, main sequence, white dwarf","Protostar, main sequence, red supergiant, neutron star","Main sequence, protostar, red giant, white dwarf","Protostar, main sequence, red giant, white dwarf"],"a":3,"x":"A star like the Sun forms from a cloud as a protostar, spends most of its life on the main sequence, swells into a red giant, then shrinks to a white dwarf.","w":["The red giant stage comes after the main sequence, not before it.","Red supergiant and neutron star are for much bigger stars, not Sun-sized ones.","A star cannot be main sequence before the protostar stage.",""]}
{"id":"gph-049","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A satellite is moved into a smaller circular orbit around the Earth. Which statement is correct?","o":["Its orbital speed must increase","Its orbital speed must decrease","Its orbital speed stays the same","It no longer needs a gravitational force"],"a":0,"x":"Gravity is stronger closer to the Earth, so the satellite must move faster to stay in a smaller stable orbit. Its direction keeps changing, so its velocity changes even at constant speed.","w":["","Thinks a smaller orbit means slower; gravity is stronger closer in, so speed rises.","Confuses constant speed within one orbit with speed across different orbits.","Thinks orbiting objects escape gravity; gravity provides the centripetal force."]}
{"id":"gph-050","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Light from distant galaxies is red-shifted, and the shift is larger for galaxies that are further away. What does this suggest?","o":["Distant galaxies are moving towards us faster","All the galaxies are the same distance away","Distant galaxies are moving away faster, so the Universe is expanding","The Universe is getting smaller"],"a":2,"x":"Red-shift means the galaxies are moving away. A larger red-shift for more distant galaxies shows they are receding faster, which is evidence that the Universe is expanding.","w":["Mixes up red-shift with blue-shift; red-shift means moving away, not towards us.","Ignores that the shift changes with distance, so the distances clearly differ.","","Reverses the conclusion; galaxies moving apart means the Universe is expanding."]}
{"id":"gph-051","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A kettle transfers 120 000 J of energy in 60 s. What is the power of the kettle?","o":["0.0005 W","2000 W","7 200 000 W","120 060 W"],"a":1,"x":"Power = energy transferred ÷ time. So 120 000 ÷ 60 = 2000 W. The trap is multiplying instead of dividing, or turning the equation upside down.","w":["Divided time by energy (60 ÷ 120 000), so the equation is upside down.","","Multiplied energy by time instead of dividing, so the power is far too big.","Added the energy and the time instead of dividing."]}
{"id":"gph-052","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A stretched elastic band is let go and shoots across a room. Which energy transfer happens as it is released?","o":["Gravitational potential store to kinetic store","Chemical store to thermal store","Kinetic store to elastic potential store","Elastic potential store to kinetic store"],"a":3,"x":"A stretched band has energy in its elastic potential store. When released, this energy is transferred to the kinetic store as the band moves. The trap is picking the transfer the wrong way round.","w":["The band starts stretched, not raised up, so its energy is in the elastic store, not the gravitational store.","No chemical reaction happens here, so this confuses stretching with a fuel being used.","This is the wrong way round, because on release the elastic store empties into the kinetic store.",""]}
{"id":"gph-053","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A 2.5 kg box is lifted steadily through 1.2 m. Gravitational field strength is 9.8 N/kg. How much energy is transferred to its gravitational potential store?","o":["29.4 J","3.0 J","0.31 J","24.5 J"],"a":0,"x":"Gravitational potential energy = m × g × h = 2.5 × 9.8 × 1.2 = 29.4 J. Leaving out one of the three quantities is the common slip.","w":["","Left out g: 2.5 × 1.2 = 3.0 J, but weight must be found using g.","Divided by g instead of multiplying: 2.5 ÷ 9.8 × 1.2 is wrong.","Left out the height: 2.5 × 9.8 only gives the weight in newtons, not energy."]}
{"id":"gph-054","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"2 kg of water is heated from 20 °C to 45 °C. The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J/kg °C. How much energy is transferred to the water?","o":["378 000 J","105 000 J","210 000 J","168 000 J"],"a":2,"x":"Change in thermal energy = m × c × temperature change. The temperature change is 45 − 20 = 25 °C. So 2 × 4200 × 25 = 210 000 J. The trap is using a temperature instead of the change.","w":["Used the final temperature 45 °C instead of the change in temperature, which is 25 °C.","Left out the mass: 4200 × 25 = 105 000 J, but the mass is 2 kg.","","Used the starting temperature 20 °C instead of the change in temperature, which is 25 °C."]}
{"id":"gph-055","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A motor lifts a 20 kg load steadily through 3 m in 4 s. Gravitational field strength is 9.8 N/kg. What is the useful power output of the motor?","o":["588 W","147 W","49 W","15 W"],"a":1,"x":"Energy gained = m × g × h = 20 × 9.8 × 3 = 588 J. Power = energy ÷ time = 588 ÷ 4 = 147 W. Stopping at 588 forgets to divide by time.","w":["This is the energy in joules (20 × 9.8 × 3), not yet divided by the time to give power.","","Left out the height: 20 × 9.8 ÷ 4 = 49 W, but the load is lifted 3 m.","Left out g: 20 × 3 ÷ 4 = 15, but weight needs 9.8 N/kg."]}
{"id":"gph-056","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about energy in a device is correct?","o":["Wasted energy is destroyed once it is no longer useful.","Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred, stored or dissipated.","Energy is used up when a device does its job.","An efficient device can give out more energy than it takes in."],"a":1,"x":"Energy is conserved, so total energy out always equals total energy in. Wasted energy is not destroyed. It spreads out (dissipates) to the surroundings. The trap is thinking that 'wasted' means 'gone'.","w":["Wasted energy is not destroyed but dissipated to the surroundings, so this confuses 'wasted' with 'gone'.","","Energy is transferred, not used up, so this confuses everyday 'using energy' with energy disappearing.","Output can never exceed input, so this breaks conservation of energy because no device is over 100% efficient."]}
{"id":"gph-057","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Oil is put on a bicycle chain. Why does this make the bike more efficient?","o":["It adds extra energy to the chain.","It makes the useful output energy bigger than the input energy.","It removes all friction, so the bike is 100% efficient.","It reduces friction, so less energy is dissipated as thermal energy."],"a":3,"x":"Lubricant reduces friction between moving parts. Less input energy is dissipated as thermal energy, so a bigger fraction is useful. Friction is reduced, not removed, so efficiency stays below 100%.","w":["Oil adds no energy because the energy comes from the rider, so this confuses reducing waste with creating energy.","Useful output can never exceed input because oil only cuts waste and cannot break conservation of energy.","Oil reduces friction but cannot remove it completely, so some energy is always dissipated and efficiency stays below 100%.",""]}
{"id":"gph-058","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A lamp has an efficiency of 0.35. It takes in 120 J of electrical energy. How much energy does it waste?","o":["78 J","42 J","85 J","343 J"],"a":0,"x":"Useful output = 0.35 × 120 = 42 J. Wasted energy = 120 − 42 = 78 J. The trap is stopping at the useful energy, or subtracting 35 straight from 120.","w":["","This is the useful energy (0.35 × 120), but the question asks for the wasted energy left over from 120 J.","Subtracted 35 from 120 as if efficiency were 35 J, but efficiency is a fraction of the input.","Divided 120 by 0.35, which reverses the efficiency equation and gives more energy than the input."]}
{"id":"gph-059","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A builder wants the slowest rate of energy transfer by conduction through a house wall. Which wall should they choose?","o":["Thin wall made of material with high thermal conductivity","Thin wall made of material with low thermal conductivity","Thick wall made of material with low thermal conductivity","Thick wall made of material with high thermal conductivity"],"a":2,"x":"Thermal energy transfers more slowly through thicker walls and through materials with lower thermal conductivity. Both together give the slowest rate. Changing only one factor, or the wrong one, gives a faster rate.","w":["A thin wall and high thermal conductivity both make energy transfer faster, so this gives the fastest rate.","Low conductivity helps, but a thin wall still lets energy through faster, so only one factor is improved.","","A thick wall helps, but high conductivity lets energy pass easily, so only one factor is improved."]}
{"id":"gph-060","s":"physics","t":"4.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A motor takes in 400 J of electrical energy to lift a 5.0 kg load through 4.0 m at constant speed. Gravitational field strength is 9.8 N/kg. What is its efficiency?","o":["5%","49%","51%","204%"],"a":1,"x":"Useful energy = gain in gravitational potential energy = mgh = 5.0 × 9.8 × 4.0 = 196 J. Efficiency = 196 ÷ 400 = 0.49 = 49%. The trap is using the wasted energy (204 J) or leaving out g.","w":["Used m × h = 20 J and left out g, but gravitational potential energy is mass × g × height.","","This is the wasted fraction (204 ÷ 400), not useful energy divided by total input.","Divided input by useful energy (400 ÷ 196), but efficiency can never exceed 100%."]}
{"id":"gph-061","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Why is solar power an unreliable way to generate electricity in the UK?","o":["Solar cells release CO₂ each time they work","The Sun will soon run out of energy to give","Output falls on cloudy days and is zero at night","Solar cells need fuel that must be delivered often"],"a":2,"x":"Solar output depends on sunshine, so it drops in cloud and stops at night. That makes it unreliable. Solar cells release no CO₂ when working and need no fuel. The Sun will not run out in our time.","w":["Solar cells release no CO₂ when working; this confuses solar with burning fossil fuels.","The Sun will keep shining for billions of years; the problem is daily and weather changes, not running out.","","Solar cells need no fuel, only light; this confuses solar with fuel-burning power stations."]}
{"id":"gph-062","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about nuclear power stations is correct?","o":["They release no CO₂ from the fuel, but they make radioactive waste","They release as much CO₂ as coal stations when they run","Their output stops when the wind drops or the Sun sets","They use a renewable fuel that is replaced quickly"],"a":0,"x":"Nuclear fission does not burn fuel, so no CO₂ comes from the fuel. The downside is radioactive waste that must be stored safely for a long time. Uranium is finite, so it is not renewable.","w":["","Fission is not burning, so no CO₂ comes from the fuel; this mixes it up with fossil fuels.","Nuclear output does not depend on weather or daylight; this confuses it with wind and solar.","Uranium is mined and will run out, so it is non-renewable; not burning does not make it renewable."]}
{"id":"gph-063","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is tidal power more predictable than wind power?","o":["Tides never stop, so the output stays constant all day","Tides are caused by the wind, which can be measured","Tidal barrages cause no harm to wildlife or habitats","Tide times are set by the Moon and Sun, so they can be forecast"],"a":3,"x":"The Moon and Sun cause tides, so high and low tide times can be worked out years ahead. Output still changes through the day, and barrages can harm habitats. Predictable does not mean constant or harmless.","w":["Tidal output rises and falls with the tide and is low at slack water; predictable does not mean constant.","Tides are caused by the Moon and Sun, not the wind; this is a misconception about their cause.","A barrage can change estuary habitats and affect wildlife; renewable does not mean no environmental harm.",""]}
{"id":"gph-064","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why do some people prefer biofuel to petrol for cars?","o":["Biofuel is a fossil fuel that will last longer","Plants take in CO₂ as they grow, so less extra CO₂ is added overall","Burning biofuel releases no CO₂ at all","Growing biofuel crops never affects land used for food"],"a":1,"x":"Biofuel crops absorb CO₂ while growing, which cancels out much of the CO₂ released when the fuel is burnt. It is not CO₂-free, and crops can take up land needed for food. Biofuel is renewable.","w":["Biofuel comes from plants or waste and is renewable, not a fossil fuel.","","Burning biofuel does release CO₂; the point is that growing the plants took CO₂ in.","Biofuel crops can use farmland that could grow food; this is a known environmental drawback."]}
{"id":"gph-065","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A country plans to get most of its electricity from wind. Why would it still need other power stations that can be switched on quickly?","o":["Wind is a finite resource that runs out as turbines use it","Wind turbines need fuel deliveries, which are often delayed","Wind speed cannot be controlled, so output may be low when demand is high","Wind turbines can only work in the daytime, when the air is warm"],"a":2,"x":"Wind speed cannot be controlled. On calm days output is low, but people still need electricity. Back-up stations that start quickly fill the gap. Wind is renewable, needs no fuel, and works at night too.","w":["Turbines do not use up the wind; it is renewable. The problem is that it varies, not that it runs out.","Wind turbines need no fuel; this confuses them with fossil fuel stations.","","Turbines work day and night whenever the wind blows; they do not need warm air or daylight."]}
{"id":"gph-066","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A student wants to measure the potential difference across a lamp. How should the voltmeter be connected?","o":["In series with the lamp, next to the ammeter","In parallel with the lamp","In series with the cell, before the switch","In parallel with the switch"],"a":1,"x":"A voltmeter must be connected in parallel with the component it measures, so it compares the two sides of the lamp. Ammeters go in series. Mixing up the two is the common trap.","w":["Ammeters go in series, not voltmeters; a voltmeter in series would also block most of the current.","","In series with the cell, the voltmeter is not across the lamp, so it cannot measure the lamp's potential difference.","A voltmeter across the switch measures the switch, not the lamp, and reads about zero when the switch is closed."]}
{"id":"gph-067","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about a diode is correct?","o":["Its resistance stays the same whatever the current","Its resistance goes up as it gets hotter","Its resistance goes down as the light gets brighter","It lets current flow in one direction only"],"a":3,"x":"A diode has a very high resistance in the reverse direction, so current flows in the forward direction only. The other statements describe a fixed resistor, a filament lamp and an LDR.","w":["This describes a fixed resistor at constant temperature, not a diode.","This describes a filament lamp, whose resistance rises as the filament heats up.","This describes an LDR, not a diode; the two components are confused.",""]}
{"id":"gph-068","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A current of 0.50 A flows through a lamp for 2.0 minutes. How much charge flows through the lamp?","o":["1.0 C","30 C","60 C","240 C"],"a":2,"x":"Charge = current × time (Q = It). Time must be in seconds: 2.0 minutes = 120 s. So Q = 0.50 × 120 = 60 C. Using 2.0 as the time is the trap.","w":["Used the time as 2.0 without changing minutes to seconds.","Used 60 s for the time instead of 120 s for 2.0 minutes.","","Divided time by current (120 ÷ 0.50) instead of multiplying."]}
{"id":"gph-069","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A current of 0.25 A flows through a 40 Ω resistor. What is the potential difference across the resistor?","o":["10 V","160 V","0.0063 V","40.25 V"],"a":0,"x":"Potential difference = current × resistance (V = IR). So V = 0.25 × 40 = 10 V. The trap is dividing instead of multiplying when rearranging the equation.","w":["","Divided resistance by current (40 ÷ 0.25) instead of multiplying them.","Divided current by resistance (0.25 ÷ 40) instead of multiplying them.","Added current and resistance instead of multiplying them."]}
{"id":"gph-070","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A 6.0 V cell, a 10 Ω fixed resistor and a thermistor are in series. A voltmeter is across the fixed resistor. The thermistor's resistance falls from 20 Ω to 10 Ω. What does the voltmeter now read?","o":["2.0 V","3.0 V","4.0 V","6.0 V"],"a":1,"x":"Total resistance falls from 30 Ω to 20 Ω, so current rises from 0.20 A to 0.30 A. The p.d. across the fixed resistor is V = IR = 0.30 × 10 = 3.0 V. It rises from 2.0 V.","w":["This is the reading before the change; the current was assumed not to change.","","This was the thermistor's p.d. before the change, not the fixed resistor's reading now.","This is the whole cell p.d.; the thermistor shares it, so the 10 Ω resistor does not get all of it."]}
{"id":"gph-071","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Two identical lamps are connected in series to a 6.0 V cell. What is the potential difference across one lamp?","o":["6.0 V","3.0 V","12 V","1.5 V"],"a":1,"x":"In series the cell's potential difference is shared between the components. Identical lamps share it equally: 6.0 ÷ 2 = 3.0 V. Each lamp getting the full 6.0 V is a parallel-circuit idea.","w":["Gave each lamp the full cell p.d.; that only happens in parallel, not series.","","Added the cell p.d. twice; the p.d. is shared, so it cannot be more than 6.0 V.","Halved the p.d. twice; it should be divided between two lamps only once."]}
{"id":"gph-072","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A 4 Ω resistor is connected to a cell. A second 4 Ω resistor is then added in parallel with it. What happens to the total resistance and the current from the cell?","o":["Resistance increases; current decreases","Resistance stays the same; current stays the same","Resistance decreases; current increases","Resistance decreases; current decreases"],"a":2,"x":"Adding a branch in parallel gives the current another path. Total resistance goes down (to 2 Ω here), so the cell supplies more current. The trap is treating it like adding a resistor in series.","w":["Treated it as a series addition; parallel branches lower the total resistance.","Thought the new branch changes nothing; it adds a path so the total current rises.","","Resistance does fall, but with the same p.d. a lower resistance means more current, not less."]}
{"id":"gph-073","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A 12 V cell is connected to a 10 Ω resistor and a resistor R in series. A voltmeter across the 10 Ω resistor reads 4.0 V. What is the resistance of R?","o":["20 Ω","30 Ω","10 Ω","8.0 Ω"],"a":0,"x":"Current = 4.0 ÷ 10 = 0.40 A, the same everywhere in series. The p.d. across R is 12 − 4.0 = 8.0 V. So R = 8.0 ÷ 0.40 = 20 Ω. 30 Ω is the total resistance, not R.","w":["","Found the total resistance (12 ÷ 0.40) but forgot to subtract the 10 Ω resistor.","Assumed the resistors share the p.d. equally; the p.d. values are 4.0 V and 8.0 V.","Used the p.d. across R (8.0 V) as its resistance; resistance is V ÷ I."]}
{"id":"gph-074","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A 6.0 V cell is connected across two resistors in parallel. One is 12 Ω. The ammeter next to the cell reads 1.5 A. What is the resistance of the other resistor?","o":["4.0 Ω","8.0 Ω","12 Ω","6.0 Ω"],"a":3,"x":"Each branch has 6.0 V. The 12 Ω branch carries 6.0 ÷ 12 = 0.50 A. The other branch carries 1.5 − 0.50 = 1.0 A. So R = 6.0 ÷ 1.0 = 6.0 Ω. 4.0 Ω is the total resistance.","w":["Found the total resistance (6.0 ÷ 1.5), not the resistance of the other resistor.","Subtracted 4.0 Ω from 12 Ω as if the resistors were in series; parallel currents are what add.","Used the 12 Ω branch current (0.50 A) for the other branch; that branch carries 1.0 A.",""]}
{"id":"gph-075","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which row correctly describes the UK mains electricity supply?","o":["Direct current, 50 V, 230 Hz","Direct current, 230 V, 50 Hz","Alternating current, 230 V, 50 Hz","Alternating current, 50 V, 230 Hz"],"a":2,"x":"UK mains is alternating current (the direction keeps reversing) with a potential difference of about 230 V and a frequency of 50 Hz. A common trap is swapping the volts and hertz values.","w":["Mains is not direct current, and the values for voltage and frequency are swapped.","Mains current keeps changing direction, so it is alternating, not direct.","","The type is right, but the voltage (230 V) and frequency (50 Hz) have been swapped."]}
{"id":"gph-076","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A kettle is wired with its switch in the neutral wire by mistake. The switch is turned off. Why is this dangerous?","o":["It stays connected to the live wire, so touching inside could still give a shock","The neutral wire would become live and the earth wire would stop working","It would draw a larger current because the neutral wire has a higher potential","The fuse would melt every time the switch is turned off"],"a":0,"x":"A switch must be in the live wire. In the neutral wire, 'off' still leaves the appliance connected to the live wire at about 230 V. Wrongly thinking a switch in any wire will do is the trap.","w":["","Switching the neutral wire does not change the earth wire; the danger is the appliance staying connected to live.","The neutral wire is at about 0 V, not a higher potential, and the problem is not about current size.","A fuse melts only when the current is too large; turning a switch off does not cause that."]}
{"id":"gph-077","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A toaster has a metal case connected to the earth wire. A loose live wire touches the case. What happens?","o":["The earth wire stops the live wire from ever touching the case","The neutral wire takes the current away, so the fuse does not melt","The earth wire carries a small current all the time, which warms the case","A large current flows to earth, the fuse melts and the live wire is cut off"],"a":3,"x":"The earth wire gives a low-resistance path to the ground. A large current flows, the fuse melts and the live wire is disconnected, so the case cannot stay at 230 V. The trap is thinking the earth wire carries current normally.","w":["The earth wire cannot stop a loose wire moving; it only gives a safe path if a fault happens.","The fault current goes through the earth wire, not the neutral wire, and the fuse does melt.","The earth wire carries no current in normal use; it only carries current when there is a fault.",""]}
{"id":"gph-078","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the neutral wire in a mains cable is correct?","o":["It is at about 230 V and carries current only if there is a fault","It completes the circuit and is at a potential of about 0 V","It is at about 230 V and is the wire the fuse is placed in","It is at about 0 V and carries current only if there is a fault"],"a":1,"x":"The neutral wire completes the circuit and is at about 0 V. The live wire is at about 230 V. Do not mix up neutral with earth, which only carries current if there is a fault.","w":["The neutral wire is at about 0 V and carries current in normal use.","","The neutral wire is at about 0 V, and the fuse goes in the live wire.","This describes the earth wire; the neutral wire carries current whenever the appliance is working."]}
{"id":"gph-079","s":"physics","t":"4.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A person standing on damp ground touches a bare live wire, but touches no other wire. Why could a dangerous current flow through their body?","o":["Their body is at 0 V, so there is a pd of about 230 V across them","Their body is at 230 V, so there is no pd across them at all","Current flows only if they also touch the neutral wire","Alternating current cannot pass through the human body"],"a":0,"x":"Our bodies are at earth potential, 0 V. Touching the live wire puts a large potential difference across the body, so current flows to earth. The trap is thinking you must also touch the neutral wire to complete a circuit.","w":["","The body is at earth potential (0 V), not 230 V, so a large pd exists across it.","The ground is at 0 V, so current can flow through the body to earth without touching neutral.","Alternating current can flow through the body and can cause a serious electric shock."]}
{"id":"gph-080","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Why does the National Grid send electricity along power lines at a very high potential difference?","o":["The current is larger, so more energy is delivered to homes","The current is smaller, so less energy is wasted as heat in the cables","The cable resistance is lower, so the cables do not heat up at all","The electrons move faster, so the energy is not lost on the way"],"a":1,"x":"For the same power, a higher potential difference means a smaller current (P = VI). Heating loss is I²R, so a smaller current wastes much less energy. The trap is thinking the voltage changes the cable's resistance.","w":["Higher voltage gives a smaller current, not a larger one, for the same power.","","High voltage does not change the cable's resistance, and some heating still happens.","Electrons do not move faster; the saving comes from a smaller current."]}
{"id":"gph-081","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A current of 3.0 A flows through a resistor of resistance 4.0 Ω. What is the power transferred in the resistor?","o":["12 W","144 W","2.25 W","36 W"],"a":3,"x":"Use P = I²R. So P = 3.0² × 4.0 = 9.0 × 4.0 = 36 W. The trap is forgetting to square the current, which gives 12 W.","w":["Forgot to square the current: 3.0 × 4.0 gives 12 W, not 36 W.","Squared the whole product (3 × 4)² instead of squaring only the current.","Divided the squared current by the resistance instead of multiplying.",""]}
{"id":"gph-082","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A hairdryer has a power of 1500 W. It is switched on for 2.0 minutes. How much energy does it transfer?","o":["3000 J","12.5 J","180 000 J","180 J"],"a":2,"x":"Energy = power × time (E = Pt), with time in seconds. 2.0 minutes = 120 s. So E = 1500 × 120 = 180 000 J. The trap is using minutes instead of seconds.","w":["Used the time as 2 minutes instead of converting to 120 seconds.","Divided power by time instead of multiplying them.","","Treated the power as 1.5 kW, which gives 180 kJ, then left off the 'kilo'."]}
{"id":"gph-083","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A lamp is connected to a 12 V supply. A current of 2.0 A flows for 20 s. How much energy is transferred by the lamp?","o":["480 J","24 J","52 J","3.3 J"],"a":0,"x":"Charge Q = It = 2.0 × 20 = 40 C. Energy E = QV = 40 × 12 = 480 J. The trap is stopping at P = VI = 24 W, which is power, not energy.","w":["","Multiplied V by I only. That gives power in watts, not energy in joules.","Added the charge (40 C) and the potential difference (12 V) instead of multiplying.","Divided charge by potential difference instead of multiplying them."]}
{"id":"gph-084","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A cable of resistance 10 Ω carries 2.0 MW at 20 000 V and wastes 100 000 W. The same power is sent at 200 000 V. How much power is now wasted in the cable?","o":["100 000 W","10 000 W","1000 W","100 W"],"a":2,"x":"Current = P ÷ V = 2 000 000 ÷ 200 000 = 10 A. Power wasted = I²R = 10² × 10 = 1000 W. The current falls by 10 times, so the loss falls by 100 times, not only 10.","w":["This is the loss at the old voltage; the higher voltage has not been used.","Assumed the loss falls 10 times. It depends on I², so it falls 100 times.","","Calculated I² = 100 but forgot to multiply by the resistance of 10 Ω."]}
{"id":"gph-085","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A student hangs a positively charged balloon on a thread. She brings a second positively charged balloon close, without touching it. What happens, and what type of force is it?","o":["They attract each other; a non-contact force","They repel each other; a non-contact force","They repel each other; a contact force","They attract each other; a contact force"],"a":1,"x":"Two objects with the same type of charge repel. The balloons do not touch, so this is a non-contact force. The trap is thinking that two positives attract.","w":["Same charges repel, not attract. Only unlike charges (one positive, one negative) attract.","","The balloons are not touching, so the force is a non-contact force, not a contact force.","Same charges repel, and the balloons are not touching, so it is a non-contact force. Both parts are wrong."]}
{"id":"gph-086","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A student holds a copper rod in her hand and rubs it with a cloth. The rod does not become charged. What is the best reason?","o":["Copper is too heavy to be charged by rubbing","The electrons in copper are held tightly and cannot move","Copper repels the electrons from the cloth","Copper is a conductor, so charge flows away through her body to earth"],"a":3,"x":"Copper is a conductor, so any charge gained leaks away through the hand and body to earth. Insulators keep their charge. The trap is thinking metals have fixed electrons.","w":["Mass has nothing to do with charging. Whether a material is a conductor or an insulator decides it.","Copper has free electrons that move easily. That is why it is a conductor, so this is the wrong way round.","Copper does not repel the cloth's electrons. The charge simply flows away through the hand.",""]}
{"id":"gph-087","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A negatively charged rod is brought near a small neutral piece of paper hanging on a thread. The paper is attracted to the rod. Which explanation is correct?","o":["Electrons in the paper are repelled, leaving the near side slightly positive","The paper must already be positively charged, so it is attracted","Protons in the paper move towards the rod, so it is attracted","Electrons jump from the rod onto the paper, making it negative"],"a":0,"x":"The rod repels electrons in the paper. The side nearest the rod becomes slightly positive. It is closer, so the attraction beats the repulsion. Protons cannot move, and a negative paper would be repelled.","w":["","The paper is neutral, not positive. It only has a slight separation of charge caused by the rod.","Protons are fixed in the nuclei and cannot move through the paper.","If the paper became negative it would be repelled, not attracted. The charge just shifts within the paper."]}
{"id":"gph-088","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A small charged ball is moved from 12 cm to 4 cm away from a charged sphere. What happens to the force on the ball, and why?","o":["It decreases, because the electric field is stronger further away","It stays the same, because the charge on the ball has not changed","It increases, because the electric field is stronger closer to the sphere","It increases, because the ball gains more charge as it gets closer"],"a":2,"x":"A charged object's electric field is strongest close to it and gets weaker with distance. The ball is in a stronger field at 4 cm, so the force on it is bigger. Its charge stays the same.","w":["The field gets weaker with distance, not stronger. Closer means a stronger field and a bigger force.","The ball's charge is fixed, but the force also depends on the field strength, which changes with distance.","","The force does increase, but the ball does not gain charge. The field it sits in is stronger."]}
{"id":"gph-089","s":"physics","t":"4.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A negatively charged rod hangs on a thread. An unknown object X is brought close and the rod is attracted to it. What can you conclude about X?","o":["X is negatively charged","X is positively charged or neutral","X is neutral only","X is positively charged only"],"a":1,"x":"Opposite charges attract, so X could be positive. A neutral object also attracts a charged rod because its charges shift. Only repulsion proves X is negative. The trap is treating attraction as proof of charge.","w":["Negative charges repel a negative rod, so X cannot be negative.","","X could be neutral, but a positive object would attract the rod too, so neutral only is too narrow.","A positive X would attract the rod, but so would a neutral X, so positive only misses one case."]}
{"id":"gph-090","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes the particles in a liquid?","o":["They are far apart and move quickly in random directions.","They are in fixed positions in a regular pattern and only vibrate.","They are close together, in no regular pattern, and can move past each other.","They are close together in a regular pattern and slide past each other."],"a":2,"x":"In a liquid the particles touch or nearly touch, but they are not in a fixed pattern. They can move past each other, so a liquid flows. The trap is giving a liquid a solid's regular pattern.","w":["This describes a gas, whose particles are far apart, so it mixes up liquids and gases.","This describes a solid, whose particles only vibrate about fixed positions, not a liquid.","","Particles in a regular pattern describe a solid, because a liquid has no regular arrangement."]}
{"id":"gph-092","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A stone has a mass of 150 g. It is lowered into a measuring cylinder. The water level rises from 40 cm³ to 100 cm³. What is the density of the stone?","o":["0.4 g/cm³","3.75 g/cm³","1.5 g/cm³","2.5 g/cm³"],"a":3,"x":"The stone's volume is the rise in water level: 100 − 40 = 60 cm³. Density = 150 ÷ 60 = 2.5 g/cm³. The trap is using one reading as the volume instead of the rise.","w":["Divided volume by mass (60 ÷ 150), but density is mass divided by volume.","Used 40 cm³, the starting water level, as the volume instead of the rise.","Used 100 cm³, the final reading, as the volume instead of the rise in water level.",""]}
{"id":"gph-093","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³. What is the mass of 0.025 m³ of this liquid?","o":["32 000 kg","20 kg","20 000 kg","0.000031 kg"],"a":1,"x":"Mass = density × volume. So 800 × 0.025 = 20 kg. The traps are dividing instead of multiplying and making a power of ten slip.","w":["Divided density by volume (800 ÷ 0.025), but mass is density multiplied by volume.","","Power of ten slip: used 25 instead of 0.025 when multiplying.","Divided volume by density (0.025 ÷ 800), rearranging the equation the wrong way round."]}
{"id":"gph-094","s":"physics","t":"4.3.1","lv":3,"q":"An empty measuring cylinder has a mass of 120 g. With 80 cm³ of a liquid in it, the mass is 184 g. What is the density of the liquid in kg/m³?","o":["0.8 kg/m³","2300 kg/m³","800 kg/m³","1250 kg/m³"],"a":2,"x":"Liquid mass = 184 − 120 = 64 g. Density = 64 ÷ 80 = 0.8 g/cm³. Multiply by 1000 to get 800 kg/m³. The traps are forgetting the cylinder's mass and the unit conversion.","w":["Left the answer in g/cm³ but wrote kg/m³, forgetting to multiply by 1000.","Used 184 g, forgetting to subtract the mass of the empty cylinder (184 ÷ 80 = 2.3 g/cm³).","","Divided volume by mass (80 ÷ 64) instead of mass by volume, then converted units."]}
{"id":"gph-095","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the internal energy of a substance?","o":["The kinetic energy of the whole substance as it moves","The total kinetic and potential energy of all its particles","The energy stored only in the bonds between its particles","The temperature of the substance measured in °C"],"a":1,"x":"Internal energy is the total kinetic energy plus potential energy of all the particles in the substance. It is not just kinetic energy, and it is not the same thing as temperature.","w":["This is the kinetic energy of the whole object, not the energy of its particles.","","This leaves out the kinetic energy of the moving particles. Internal energy has both kinetic and potential parts.","Temperature is not energy. It is linked to the average kinetic energy of the particles, but it is not the total."]}
{"id":"gph-096","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Ice at 0 °C is heated and melts. The temperature stays at 0 °C while it melts. What happens to the energy supplied?","o":["It increases the kinetic energy of the particles","It is lost to the surroundings as waste heat","It increases the mass of the particles","It increases the potential energy of the particles"],"a":3,"x":"During a change of state the energy breaks or weakens the bonds between particles. This raises their potential energy. Kinetic energy does not change, so the temperature stays constant.","w":["If kinetic energy increased, the temperature would rise. The temperature stays constant during melting.","The energy is not wasted. It is stored as potential energy as the bonds break.","Particles do not gain mass. Energy changes how the particles are arranged, not how heavy they are.",""]}
{"id":"gph-097","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"The specific latent heat of vaporisation of water is 2 260 000 J/kg. How much energy turns 0.50 kg of water at 100 °C into steam at 100 °C?","o":["1 130 000 J","2 260 000 J","4 520 000 J","1 130 000 000 J"],"a":0,"x":"Use E = m L = 0.50 × 2 260 000 = 1 130 000 J. The mass must be multiplied in, and 0.50 kg is already in kilograms.","w":["","This is the energy for 1 kg. It forgets to multiply by the mass of 0.50 kg.","This divides by 0.50 instead of multiplying, so the answer is double what it should be.","This uses 500, converting 0.50 kg to 500 g. The mass must stay in kilograms."]}
{"id":"gph-098","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A 0.40 kg metal block is heated and its temperature rises by 50 °C. It gains 36 000 J of energy. What is the specific heat capacity of the metal?","o":["720 J/kg °C","90 000 J/kg °C","1800 J/kg °C","720 000 J/kg °C"],"a":2,"x":"Rearrange ΔE = m c Δθ to get c = ΔE ÷ (m × Δθ) = 36 000 ÷ (0.40 × 50) = 1800 J/kg °C. Both the mass and the temperature change must be in the divisor.","w":["This divides by 50 only, 36 000 ÷ 50. It forgets to divide by the mass.","This divides by 0.40 only. It forgets to divide by the temperature change.","","This multiplies all three numbers together instead of rearranging the equation to divide."]}
{"id":"gph-099","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"0.20 kg of ice at 0 °C is melted and the water is then heated to 10 °C. Specific latent heat of fusion = 334 000 J/kg; c of water = 4200 J/kg °C. How much energy is needed?","o":["66 800 J","75 200 J","58 400 J","8400 J"],"a":1,"x":"There are two stages. Melting: 0.20 × 334 000 = 66 800 J. Heating: 0.20 × 4200 × 10 = 8400 J. Add them to get 75 200 J. Leaving out a stage is the trap.","w":["This is only the melting stage. It forgets to heat the water from 0 °C to 10 °C.","","This subtracts the heating energy from the melting energy. Both energies must be supplied, so they are added.","This is only the heating stage. It forgets the energy needed to melt the ice first."]}
{"id":"gph-100","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"A student says the temperature of a gas tells us something about its particles. What does the temperature of a gas show?","o":["How many particles are in the gas","The average kinetic energy of the particles","How big each particle is","The total mass of the gas"],"a":1,"x":"The temperature of a gas is linked to the average kinetic energy of its particles. Hotter gas means faster-moving particles on average. The trap is thinking temperature shows how many particles there are.","w":["Temperature does not count particles. A cold gas and a hot gas can have the same number of particles.","","Particles do not change size when the temperature changes. They just move faster or slower.","Mass depends on how many particles there are, not on how fast they move."]}
{"id":"gph-101","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"A fixed mass of gas in a syringe is kept at constant temperature. The plunger is pulled out so the volume gets bigger. Why does the pressure fall?","o":["The particles slow down and hit the walls less hard","Some of the particles leave the syringe","Each particle gets smaller as the gas expands","The particles are more spread out, so they hit the walls less often"],"a":3,"x":"The temperature is constant, so the particle speed does not change. With more room, the particles hit a given area of wall less often. Fewer collisions each second means lower pressure.","w":["Constant temperature means the average speed stays the same, so the particles do not slow down.","The mass of gas is fixed and the syringe is sealed, so no particles leave.","Particles do not change size when a gas expands. The gaps between them get bigger.",""]}
{"id":"gph-102","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":3,"q":"The outlet of a bicycle pump is blocked. The piston is pushed in quickly and the air inside gets warmer. What is the best explanation?","o":["The air particles get squashed and become hotter","The air particles gain mass when they are pushed closer","Work is done on the air, so its particles gain kinetic energy","Particles in the air stop moving and turn into thermal energy"],"a":2,"x":"Pushing the piston is a force moving through a distance, so work is done on the gas. This transfers energy to the particles. Their average kinetic energy rises, so the temperature rises.","w":["Particles are not squashed and do not become hotter in themselves. Energy is transferred to them by the work done.","Particles keep the same mass however close together they are.","","The particles do not stop. They move faster after work is done on them."]}
{"id":"gph-103","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A fixed mass of gas at constant temperature has a volume of 120 cm³ at a pressure of 100 kPa. It is compressed to 40 cm³. What is the new pressure?","o":["300 kPa","33 kPa","12 000 kPa","180 kPa"],"a":0,"x":"At constant temperature, pressure × volume is constant. 100 × 120 = p × 40, so p = 12 000 ÷ 40 = 300 kPa. The trap is using the ratio the wrong way up, which gives a smaller pressure.","w":["","This turns the ratio upside down. Squashing a gas increases its pressure, it does not reduce it.","This multiplies pressure by volume but does not divide by the new volume.","This adds the change in volume to the pressure, but pressure and volume are not linked by adding."]}
{"id":"gph-104","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A fixed mass of gas at constant temperature is at $2.0\\times10^{5}$ Pa in a volume of 300 cm³. The volume is then reduced by 100 cm³. What is the new pressure?","o":["$1.3\\times10^{5}$ Pa","$3.0\\times10^{5}$ Pa","$6.0\\times10^{5}$ Pa","$2.0\\times10^{5}$ Pa"],"a":1,"x":"The new volume is 300 − 100 = 200 cm³, not 100 cm³. Then p₁V₁ = p₂V₂ gives p₂ = (2.0×10⁵ × 300) ÷ 200 = 3.0×10⁵ Pa. Smaller volume means higher pressure.","w":["This turns the ratio upside down. A smaller volume must give a higher pressure, not a lower one.","","This uses 100 cm³ as the final volume. It is the amount removed, so the final volume is 200 cm³.","This ignores the change in volume. At constant temperature, pressure changes when volume changes."]}
{"id":"gph-105","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"A gas is trapped in an insulated syringe. The plunger is pushed in quickly and the gas gets hotter. Which statement explains why the pressure of the gas rises?","o":["Particles are closer and heavier, so they hit the walls with more force","Particles are closer, so they hit the walls more often, at the same speed","Particles are closer and faster, so they hit the walls more often and harder","Particles are faster, so they hit the walls harder, but no more often"],"a":2,"x":"Work done on the gas raises the particles' kinetic energy, so they move faster. They are also closer together. Both changes mean more frequent and harder collisions with the walls. Particle mass does not change.","w":["Particles do not gain mass when compressed; the harder collisions come from their higher speed, not extra mass.","The gas got hotter, so the particles do speed up; saying their speed is unchanged ignores the work done on the gas.","","Closer, faster particles also hit the walls more often, so saying the collisions are no more frequent is wrong."]}
{"id":"gph-106","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"Gas in a metal cylinder is compressed very slowly in a cool room. Its temperature stays the same. Which statement best explains this?","o":["Pushing slowly does no work on the gas, so its internal energy is unchanged","Work is done, but energy is lost to the room by heating as fast as it is added","The work only raises the particles' potential energy, not the temperature","Particles lose kinetic energy when pushed closer, cancelling the work done"],"a":1,"x":"Pushing the piston always does work on the gas. Done slowly in a metal cylinder, the energy passes to the room by heating as fast as it is added, so the temperature stays constant. Slow does not mean no work.","w":["Work is force times distance moved, so work is done however slowly you push; slow compression is not zero work.","","The temperature stays constant because energy leaves by heating, not because the work is stored only as potential energy.","Particles do not lose kinetic energy just by being closer; the temperature stays constant because energy leaves by heating."]}
{"id":"gph-107","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes the plum pudding model of the atom?","o":["A tiny positive nucleus with electrons orbiting far away from it","A nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons in energy levels","A ball of positive charge with tiny negative electrons spread through it","A solid ball of neutrons with protons stuck on the surface"],"a":2,"x":"The plum pudding model had a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it. There was no nucleus. The nuclear model replaced it after the alpha scattering experiment.","w":["This is the nuclear model, which came later and replaced the plum pudding model.","This is the later Bohr-style picture, not the plum pudding model, which had no nucleus.","","The plum pudding model had no neutrons or protons on a surface; neutrons were discovered much later."]}
{"id":"gph-108","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":1,"q":"An atom has a radius of about $1\\times10^{-10}$ m. Which is the best estimate for the radius of its nucleus?","o":["$1\\times10^{-15}$ m","$1\\times10^{-12}$ m","$1\\times10^{-8}$ m","$1\\times10^{-10}$ m"],"a":0,"x":"The nucleus has a radius of less than 1/10 000 of the atom's radius. That is smaller than $1\\times10^{-14}$ m, so $1\\times10^{-15}$ m fits. The trap is thinking the nucleus is a large part of the atom.","w":["","$1\\times10^{-12}$ m is only 1/100 of the atom's radius. The nucleus is far smaller than that.","$1\\times10^{-8}$ m is bigger than the whole atom, so it cannot be the nucleus.","$1\\times10^{-10}$ m is the radius of the whole atom, not the nucleus."]}
{"id":"gph-109","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A sodium atom has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. It loses one electron to form an Na⁺ ion. What does the Na⁺ ion contain?","o":["10 protons, 12 neutrons, 10 electrons","11 protons, 12 neutrons, 12 electrons","11 protons, 23 neutrons, 10 electrons","11 protons, 12 neutrons, 10 electrons"],"a":3,"x":"Atomic number 11 means 11 protons. Neutrons = 23 − 11 = 12. Losing one electron leaves 10 electrons, giving a +1 charge. Only electrons change when an ion forms.","w":["Losing an electron does not change the number of protons; the nucleus stays the same.","A positive ion has lost an electron, not gained one, so it has 10 electrons.","The mass number is protons plus neutrons, so neutrons are 23 − 11 = 12, not 23.",""]}
{"id":"gph-110","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":2,"q":"In the alpha scattering experiment, a few alpha particles were deflected by large angles or bounced back. What did these large deflections show?","o":["Electrons in atoms are heavier than alpha particles","The nucleus is tiny, positively charged and holds most of the mass","The positive charge is spread evenly through the whole atom","Most of an atom is empty space"],"a":1,"x":"Large deflections happen when a positive alpha particle meets a small, dense, positive nucleus. That is why the plum pudding model was replaced. Empty space is shown by most particles passing straight through.","w":["Electrons are very light. They cannot bounce back heavy alpha particles.","","Evenly spread positive charge would only cause small deflections, as in the plum pudding model.","This conclusion comes from most particles going straight through, not from the ones that bounced back."]}
{"id":"gph-111","s":"physics","t":"4.4.1","lv":3,"q":"P has 6 protons and 6 neutrons. Q has 7 protons and 7 neutrons. R has 6 protons and 8 neutrons. S has 8 protons and 6 neutrons. Which two are isotopes of the same element?","o":["Q and R","P and S","P and R","Q and S"],"a":2,"x":"Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. P and R both have 6 protons but have 6 and 8 neutrons. The traps are matching mass numbers or neutron numbers.","w":["Q and R both have mass number 14, but they have different numbers of protons, so they are different elements.","P and S both have 6 neutrons, but isotopes must have the same number of protons, not neutrons.","","Q and S both have mass number 14, but their proton numbers differ (7 and 8), so they are different elements."]}
{"id":"gph-112","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list puts alpha, beta and gamma radiation in order of how strongly they ionise, from highest to lowest?","o":["Gamma, beta, alpha","Beta, alpha, gamma","Alpha, beta, gamma","Alpha, gamma, beta"],"a":2,"x":"Alpha is the most ionising, then beta, then gamma, which is the least. Ionising power is the opposite of penetrating power. The trap is to think the most penetrating radiation must ionise most.","w":["This is the order of penetrating power, which is the opposite of ionising power.","Beta is less ionising than alpha, because alpha is bigger and has a greater charge.","","Gamma is the least ionising because it passes through matter easily, so it comes last, not second."]}
{"id":"gph-113","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is a beta particle?","o":["Two protons and two neutrons joined together","A fast-moving electron emitted from the nucleus","An electron knocked out of an outer shell of an atom","A wave of high-frequency electromagnetic radiation"],"a":1,"x":"A beta particle is a fast-moving electron that comes from the nucleus. The trap is to think it comes from the electron shells. Those electrons are involved in ionisation, not in radioactive decay.","w":["This describes an alpha particle, a helium nucleus, not a beta particle.","","A beta particle is made in and emitted from the nucleus, not knocked out of an electron shell.","This describes gamma radiation, which is an electromagnetic wave, not a particle."]}
{"id":"gph-114","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is swallowing an alpha source more dangerous than having the same source outside the body?","o":["Alpha is the most penetrating radiation, so it only reaches organs from inside","Alpha travels further in the body than in air, so it reaches more cells","Alpha only becomes ionising once it is inside living tissue","Skin stops it outside, but inside it strongly ionises nearby cells"],"a":3,"x":"Alpha is stopped by skin, so outside the body it is much less harmful. Inside, it is absorbed by nearby cells and is strongly ionising, so it can damage them. The trap is thinking alpha is penetrating.","w":["Alpha is the least penetrating radiation, stopped by paper or skin, so this mixes up penetration.","Alpha has a short range in any material and does not travel further in the body than in air.","Alpha is always ionising; what changes inside the body is that skin no longer blocks it.",""]}
{"id":"gph-115","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A source emits only one type of radiation. A detector 30 cm away in air reads well above background. Which radiation can be ruled out?","o":["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","Neutron"],"a":0,"x":"Alpha has a range of only a few centimetres in air, so it cannot reach a detector 30 cm away. Beta, gamma and neutron radiation all travel much further in air. The trap is ignoring range in air.","w":["","Beta has a range of around a metre or more in air, so it could easily reach 30 cm.","Gamma radiation travels very far in air, so it could reach 30 cm.","Neutron radiation is very penetrating and travels far in air, so it could reach the detector."]}
{"id":"gph-116","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Background is 10 counts/min. A source gives 400. With paper it gives 398. With 5 mm aluminium it gives 120. With thick lead it gives 10. What does it emit?","o":["Alpha and beta","Alpha and gamma","Beta and gamma","Gamma only"],"a":2,"x":"Paper makes almost no difference, so there is no alpha. Aluminium cuts the count a lot, so beta is present. Thick lead brings it to background, but 120 remained after aluminium, so gamma is also present.","w":["Paper would stop alpha and cut the count, but it barely changed, so alpha is not present.","Paper made no difference so there is no alpha, and the big drop with aluminium shows beta.","","Gamma alone would hardly be reduced by 5 mm of aluminium, so the large drop shows beta is present."]}
{"id":"gph-117","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Polonium-210 decays by alpha emission: $^{210}_{84}\\text{Po} \\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}\\text{Pb} + {}^{4}_{2}\\text{He}$. What are the values of A and Z for the lead nucleus?","o":["A = 214, Z = 86","A = 206, Z = 86","A = 206, Z = 82","A = 210, Z = 82"],"a":2,"x":"The numbers on each side must balance. Mass: 210 − 4 = 206. Atomic number: 84 − 2 = 82. The trap is adding the alpha particle's numbers or changing only one of the two.","w":["Added 4 to the mass number and 2 to the atomic number instead of subtracting them.","Took 4 off the mass number correctly but added 2 to the atomic number instead of subtracting.","","Left the mass number unchanged, forgetting that an alpha particle carries away 4 nucleons."]}
{"id":"gph-118","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Carbon-14 decays by beta emission: $^{14}_{6}\\text{C} \\rightarrow {}^{A}_{Z}\\text{N} + {}^{0}_{-1}\\text{e}$. What are A and Z for the nitrogen nucleus?","o":["A = 14, Z = 7","A = 14, Z = 5","A = 13, Z = 6","A = 10, Z = 4"],"a":0,"x":"A beta particle has mass number 0 and atomic number −1. Mass: 14 − 0 = 14. Atomic number: 6 − (−1) = 7. The trap is subtracting 1 from the atomic number.","w":["","Subtracted 1 from the atomic number; the beta particle's −1 means the nucleus gains 1.","Took 1 off the mass number, treating the electron as a nucleon; beta decay leaves mass number unchanged.","Used the alpha decay rule, taking 4 off the mass number and 2 off the atomic number."]}
{"id":"gph-119","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":1,"q":"In beta decay the atomic number of the nucleus goes up by 1. Which statement explains this?","o":["An electron is lost from the inner shell of the atom","A proton in the nucleus changes into a neutron","Two protons and two neutrons leave the nucleus","A neutron changes into a proton and a fast electron is emitted"],"a":3,"x":"In beta decay a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton and an electron. The electron is emitted as the beta particle. Protons increase by one, so the atomic number rises by 1. The electron does not come from the shells.","w":["Beta particles come from the nucleus, not the atom's shells; losing shell electrons does not change the atomic number.","This would lower the atomic number by 1, the opposite of beta decay.","This describes alpha decay, which lowers the atomic number by 2.",""]}
{"id":"gph-120","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Thorium-234 (atomic number 90) decays by beta emission to protactinium (atomic number 91). Which nuclear equation is correct?","o":["$^{234}_{90}\\text{Th} \\rightarrow {}^{234}_{91}\\text{Pa} + {}^{0}_{-1}\\text{e}$","$^{234}_{90}\\text{Th} \\rightarrow {}^{230}_{88}\\text{Ra} + {}^{4}_{2}\\text{He}$","$^{234}_{90}\\text{Th} \\rightarrow {}^{234}_{89}\\text{Ac} + {}^{0}_{-1}\\text{e}$","$^{234}_{90}\\text{Th} \\rightarrow {}^{230}_{91}\\text{Pa} + {}^{0}_{-1}\\text{e}$"],"a":0,"x":"Beta decay keeps the mass number at 234 and raises the atomic number by 1, to 91. Check the bottom numbers: 90 = 91 + (−1). The other options break one of the balances or show alpha decay.","w":["","This equation balances but shows alpha decay, not beta decay, and the product is not protactinium.","The atomic number goes down to 89; the −1 of the beta particle means the nucleus gains a proton.","The mass numbers do not balance: 230 + 0 is not 234."]}
{"id":"gph-121","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A nucleus emits two alpha particles, then one beta particle. It ends as bismuth-214 (mass number 214, atomic number 83). What were the mass number and atomic number of the starting nucleus?","o":["222 and 86","222 and 84","218 and 86","222 and 88"],"a":0,"x":"Work backwards. Undo the beta: atomic number 82, mass 214. Undo two alphas: add 8 to mass (222) and 4 to atomic number (86). The trap is applying the changes in the wrong direction.","w":["","Undid the beta correctly but added back only 2 protons for the two alpha particles.","Added back only one alpha's mass (4) instead of two alphas' mass (8).","Undid the beta the wrong way, adding 1 to the atomic number instead of subtracting it."]}
{"id":"gph-122","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a correct meaning of the half-life of a radioactive isotope?","o":["Half the time it takes for all the nuclei in a sample to decay","The time for half of the radiation from a source to be absorbed by air","The time for the number of nuclei of the isotope in a sample to halve","The time for a source to lose half of its mass as it decays"],"a":2,"x":"Half-life is the time for the number of undecayed nuclei to halve. The count rate or activity also halves in that time. It is not half the time for all nuclei to decay, since there is no fixed time when all have decayed.","w":["Wrong because there is no fixed time for all nuclei to decay, so this confuses half-life with half a total lifetime.","Wrong because half-life is about unstable nuclei decaying, not about radiation being absorbed by air.","","Wrong because the sample's mass barely changes, since decaying nuclei turn into other nuclei rather than disappearing."]}
{"id":"gph-123","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"An isotope has a half-life of 8 hours. What does this tell us about ONE nucleus of this isotope?","o":["It will decay at exactly 8 hours","It has a 50% chance of decaying within 8 hours","It will decay at exactly 4 hours","It cannot decay in less than 8 hours"],"a":1,"x":"Decay is random. We cannot say when one nucleus will decay. Half-life gives the chance: each nucleus has a 50% chance of decaying in one half-life. Large samples follow the pattern well.","w":["Wrong because the decay of one nucleus is random, so treating half-life as an exact timer is a mistake.","","Wrong because decay is random and nothing fixes a time of 4 hours; this wrongly halves the half-life.","Wrong because a nucleus can decay at any moment, even straight away, so half-life is not a minimum waiting time."]}
{"id":"gph-124","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The activity of a sample falls from 1200 Bq to 300 Bq in 3 hours. What is the half-life of the sample?","o":["3 hours","2 hours","0.75 hours","1.5 hours"],"a":3,"x":"1200 → 600 → 300 is two halvings, so 3 hours is two half-lives. One half-life is 3 ÷ 2 = 1.5 hours. The trap is treating the whole 3 hours as one half-life.","w":["Wrong because this treats the whole fall as one half-life, but the activity has halved twice.","Wrong because this gives the number of half-lives as the time, instead of dividing 3 hours by 2.","Wrong because this divides 3 hours by the overall drop factor of 4 instead of by the 2 halvings.",""]}
{"id":"gph-125","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Sample A and sample B each have the same number of radioactive nuclei. A has a half-life of 2 minutes. B has a half-life of 2 years. Which statement is correct at the start?","o":["A has the higher activity, because its nuclei decay more quickly","B has the higher activity, because it lasts for longer","They have the same activity, because they have the same number of nuclei","Nothing can be said, because each decay is random"],"a":0,"x":"A short half-life means a nucleus is more likely to decay in any given second. So A has more decays per second, which is higher activity. Randomness of single decays does not stop us comparing large samples.","w":["","Wrong because lasting longer means decays happen less often, so this confuses long life with high activity.","Wrong because activity depends on how likely each nucleus is to decay, not only on how many nuclei there are.","Wrong because large samples follow predictable patterns even though single decays are random."]}
{"id":"gph-126","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A detector reads 260 counts per minute with a source present. The background count is 20 counts per minute. The source has a half-life of 30 minutes. What will it read after 60 minutes?","o":["65 counts per minute","130 counts per minute","80 counts per minute","60 counts per minute"],"a":2,"x":"Remove background first: 260 − 20 = 240. Two half-lives: 240 → 120 → 60. Background does not decay, so add 20 back: 80 counts per minute. The traps are halving the background too, or forgetting to add it back.","w":["Wrong because this halves the whole reading twice, but the background count does not halve.","Wrong because this halves only once, but 60 minutes is two half-lives.","","Wrong because this forgets the detector still measures the background of 20 counts per minute."]}
{"id":"gph-127","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A sealed gamma source is used to irradiate a sample of food. What is true of the food afterwards?","o":["It is now radioactive for a short time","It has not become radioactive","It is contaminated with radioactive atoms","It becomes radioactive if it is eaten"],"a":1,"x":"Irradiation means exposure to radiation from a source outside the object. No radioactive atoms get onto or into the food, so it does not become radioactive. The trap is mixing this up with contamination.","w":["Irradiation never makes an object radioactive, even briefly; this mixes up irradiation with contamination.","","A sealed source transfers no radioactive atoms, so the food is not contaminated.","The food contains no radioactive atoms, so eating it cannot make it radioactive."]}
{"id":"gph-128","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Why is an alpha-emitting source usually more dangerous inside the body than outside it?","o":["Alpha particles are less ionising when inside the body","Alpha particles become more penetrating inside the body","Inside, they reach living cells; outside, skin and air absorb them","Alpha particles turn into gamma rays inside the body"],"a":2,"x":"Alpha is strongly ionising but weakly penetrating. Outside, air and dead skin absorb it. Inside, it is in contact with living cells and can damage them. The trap is thinking alpha is equally dangerous everywhere.","w":["Alpha is just as ionising inside the body; the difference is where it is absorbed.","Alpha does not change its penetrating power; it is always weakly penetrating.","","Alpha particles do not change into gamma rays anywhere, inside or outside the body."]}
{"id":"gph-129","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A technician handles a radioactive powder. Which precaution best protects against contamination by the powder?","o":["Wearing gloves and a protective suit","Standing behind a thick lead screen","Handling the powder for a shorter time","Wearing a badge that records the dose"],"a":0,"x":"Contamination happens when radioactive atoms get onto or into you. A suit and gloves stop the powder touching skin. Screens and shorter time mainly cut irradiation, and a badge only measures dose. The trap is picking those.","w":["","A lead screen cuts radiation reaching you but does not stop powder getting on you.","Less time reduces exposure to radiation, but powder can still get on you.","A dose badge only monitors exposure; it does nothing to stop powder reaching you."]}
{"id":"gph-130","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A sealed alpha source and a sealed gamma source are both held outside the body. Which is the greater irradiation hazard, and why?","o":["Alpha, because it is the most ionising radiation","Alpha, because it is the most penetrating radiation","Both are equal, because both are sealed sources","Gamma, because it passes through skin and reaches organs"],"a":3,"x":"Outside the body, penetration matters most. Gamma rays pass through skin and can reach organs. Alpha is stopped by a few cm of air and by skin. The trap is thinking the most ionising radiation is always the worst.","w":["Alpha is most ionising, but it is stopped before reaching cells inside the body.","Alpha is the least penetrating, not the most.","The two types of radiation behave very differently, so the hazard is not equal.",""]}
{"id":"gph-131","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":3,"q":"Worker A stands near a sealed gamma source. Worker B has gamma-emitting dust on their clothes. Both leave the room. Which statement is correct?","o":["Both are now safe, because leaving ends the exposure","Worker A is still radioactive and carries radiation with them","Worker B is still exposed until the dust is removed","Worker B is only a danger to others, not to themselves"],"a":2,"x":"Irradiation stops when the person leaves the source. Contamination goes with the person, because the radioactive atoms are on them, so exposure continues until the dust is removed. The trap is thinking leaving the room ends both.","w":["Leaving ends irradiation for A, but the dust on B still emits radiation.","Irradiation does not make a person radioactive, so A carries nothing.","","The dust on B also irradiates B, as well as people nearby."]}
{"id":"gph-132","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Airline pilots get a higher dose of background radiation than most people. Which statement explains this?","o":["Aircraft engines give out radiation all the time","There is less air to absorb cosmic rays at high altitude","Radon gas from rocks collects inside the aircraft cabin","Pilots have X-ray checks before every flight"],"a":1,"x":"Cosmic rays come from space. High up, there is less air above to absorb them, so the dose is larger. The trap is thinking the plane itself or the ground is the source.","w":["Aircraft engines are not a source of ionising radiation; the extra dose comes from space.","","Radon comes from rocks in the ground and is not a problem high in the air.","Flight checks do not involve X-rays; the extra dose is natural, from cosmic rays."]}
{"id":"gph-133","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":1,"q":"In a lab, a student gets radioactive dust on their hands. What does the word contamination mean here?","o":["Being exposed to radiation from a source outside the body","Radiation making a nearby object become radioactive","The nucleus of an atom giving out radiation","Radioactive atoms ending up on or inside an object or person"],"a":3,"x":"Contamination means unwanted radioactive atoms are on or inside something. The atoms keep decaying there. Irradiation is different: it is exposure to radiation from outside, and it does not leave radioactive atoms behind.","w":["This describes irradiation, not contamination; mixing up the two terms is the usual slip.","Irradiation does not make objects radioactive; contamination is about radioactive atoms themselves being present.","This describes radioactive decay, not contamination.",""]}
{"id":"gph-134","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A tracer injected into a patient has an activity of 80 MBq. Its half-life is 6 hours. What is its activity 18 hours later, if none leaves the body?","o":["10 MBq","20 MBq","27 MBq","40 MBq"],"a":0,"x":"18 hours is 3 half-lives. 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 MBq. The trap is dividing by 3 instead of halving three times, which gives 27 MBq.","w":["","This is the activity after only 2 half-lives (12 hours), so one halving was missed.","This comes from dividing 80 by 3, but activity halves each half-life; it does not drop in equal steps.","This is the activity after just 1 half-life (6 hours)."]}
{"id":"gph-135","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":2,"q":"Gamma sources P and Q have the same activity today. P has a half-life of 2 hours. Q has a half-life of 200 years. Both are stored for a week. Which is more hazardous after that week?","o":["Both are equally hazardous, as they started with the same activity","P, because a short half-life means it gives out more radiation","Q, because its activity has hardly fallen after a week","Q, because a long half-life means it gives out stronger radiation"],"a":2,"x":"After a week P has gone through about 84 half-lives, so its activity is almost zero. Q has barely changed. Half-life tells you how fast activity falls, not the strength of each emission.","w":["The activities were equal only at the start; P's activity falls far faster than Q's.","P's activity has dropped to almost nothing; a short half-life means it loses activity quickly.","","Half-life does not change how strong each emission is; Q is more hazardous because its activity stays high."]}
{"id":"gph-136","s":"physics","t":"4.4.3","lv":3,"q":"A hospital injects a tracer into the blood. A camera outside the body must detect it. Which radioactive source is best?","o":["A gamma emitter with a half-life of 6 hours","A gamma emitter with a half-life of 6 years","An alpha emitter with a half-life of 6 hours","A beta emitter with a half-life of 6 hours"],"a":0,"x":"Gamma rays pass out of the body to reach the camera. A short half-life means the activity soon falls, so the patient's dose is low. Alpha and most beta are absorbed inside the body, and a long half-life keeps the patient radioactive.","w":["","The gamma rays can be detected, but a 6-year half-life would leave the patient radioactive for years.","Alpha is absorbed by tissue within a very short distance, so the camera would not detect it.","Beta is mostly absorbed by body tissue, so little reaches the camera; gamma is needed."]}
{"id":"gph-138","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":2,"q":"Control rods are lowered further into a nuclear reactor. What is the effect on the chain reaction?","o":["They absorb more neutrons, so fewer fissions happen and the rate falls","They speed up the neutrons, so more fissions happen and the rate rises","They add more uranium nuclei, so the energy released increases","They stop neutrons being released, so no fission can ever occur"],"a":0,"x":"Control rods absorb neutrons. With fewer neutrons left to cause fission, the chain reaction slows. The rods do not stop neutrons being released; they absorb them after release.","w":["","Control rods do not speed neutrons up; they absorb them, which slows the reaction rather than raising it.","Rods are not fuel; they remove neutrons rather than adding uranium, so the energy released falls.","Neutrons are still released by each fission; the rods absorb them, so the reaction slows rather than stopping completely."]}
{"id":"gph-139","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":1,"q":"Why must hydrogen nuclei be at a very high temperature and pressure before they can undergo fusion?","o":["Hydrogen nuclei have no charge, so they need strong forces to pull them together","The gas must first be turned into a solid before nuclei can join","Neutrons in the nuclei slow the hydrogen nuclei down too much","Nuclei are positively charged and repel, so they need high speed to get close"],"a":3,"x":"Nuclei all carry positive charge, so they repel each other. Very high temperature gives them enough kinetic energy to get close enough to fuse. The trap is thinking nuclei are neutral.","w":["Nuclei are positively charged, not neutral; the real problem is repulsion between them.","Fusion happens in hot gas, not solids; this is invented and not linked to repulsion between nuclei.","Neutrons do not slow nuclei down in this way; the main problem is the repulsion between positive charges.",""]}
{"id":"gph-140","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":2,"q":"One fission releases 3 neutrons. Each neutron causes one new fission, also releasing 3 neutrons. None are lost. The first fission's neutrons cause round 1. How many fissions are in round 3?","o":["9","27","12","81"],"a":1,"x":"The number triples each round: 3 in round 1, 9 in round 2, then 27 in round 3. The trap is stopping a round early or adding 3 each time instead of multiplying by 3.","w":["9 is the number in round 2; this stops one round too early.","","12 comes from adding 3 each round; a chain reaction multiplies the number by 3 each time.","81 is round 4, 3 × 3 × 3 × 3; this goes one round too far."]}
{"id":"gph-141","s":"physics","t":"4.4.4","lv":3,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the chain reaction in a nuclear reactor with the one in a nuclear weapon?","o":["Both use fusion, but a reactor adds extra hydrogen to slow it down","A weapon uses no neutrons, but a reactor needs neutrons to work","Reactor: about one neutron per fission causes another; weapon: more than one","Reactor: no neutrons cause further fissions; weapon: every neutron does"],"a":2,"x":"A reactor's chain reaction is controlled, so about one neutron per fission causes another and energy is released steadily. A weapon's is uncontrolled, so fissions multiply rapidly. Trap: thinking a reactor has no chain reaction at all.","w":["Both use fission, not fusion; reactors are controlled by absorbing neutrons, not adding hydrogen.","Both depend on neutrons causing new fissions; a weapon is simply an uncontrolled chain reaction.","","A reactor still needs neutrons to cause new fissions or it would stop, and not every neutron causes fission in a weapon."]}
{"id":"gph-142","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which pair contains only vector quantities?","o":["Speed and distance","Force and displacement","Mass and weight","Velocity and energy"],"a":1,"x":"Vectors have size and direction. Force and displacement are both vectors. The trap is mass: it has size only, so it is a scalar, even though weight is a vector.","w":["Speed and distance have size only, so both are scalars; speed is not the same as velocity.","","Mass is a scalar with no direction; only weight is a vector. Mixing up mass and weight causes this slip.","Energy is a scalar with no direction. Only velocity in this pair is a vector."]}
{"id":"gph-143","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list contains only non-contact forces?","o":["Friction, gravitational and magnetic","Air resistance, tension and electrostatic","Normal contact, magnetic and weight","Gravitational, magnetic and electrostatic"],"a":3,"x":"Non-contact forces act without the objects touching: gravitational, magnetic and electrostatic. Friction, air resistance, tension and normal contact forces all need objects to touch.","w":["Friction needs surfaces to touch, so it is a contact force.","Air resistance and tension are contact forces, so this list has two wrong items.","Normal contact force needs touching surfaces. Weight and magnetic force are non-contact, so only one item is wrong.",""]}
{"id":"gph-144","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"An object has a weight of 49 N on Earth, where the gravitational field strength is 9.8 N/kg. What is the mass of the object?","o":["5 kg","49 kg","480.2 kg","0.2 kg"],"a":0,"x":"Mass = weight ÷ gravitational field strength = 49 ÷ 9.8 = 5 kg. The traps are multiplying instead of dividing, or treating weight in newtons as mass in kilograms.","w":["","Treated the weight in newtons as if it were the mass in kilograms.","Multiplied 49 by 9.8 instead of dividing; the rearrangement of W = mg was wrong.","Divided 9.8 by 49, which turns the division upside down."]}
{"id":"gph-145","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A student plots a graph of weight (N) against mass (kg) for objects on a planet. It is a straight line through the origin. What does the gradient show?","o":["The density of the objects","The mass of each object","The gravitational field strength","The total weight of the objects"],"a":2,"x":"Weight = mass × gravitational field strength, so weight ÷ mass is the gradient. That equals the gravitational field strength in N/kg. Weight and mass are directly proportional.","w":["Density is mass ÷ volume, and volume is not on this graph.","Mass is on the horizontal axis, not the gradient; the gradient is a ratio of weight to mass.","","Total weight is read from the vertical axis for a given mass, not from the gradient."]}
{"id":"gph-146","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1","lv":3,"q":"A probe has a mass of 250 kg. It weighs 1000 N on planet X. How much more would it weigh on Earth, where g = 9.8 N/kg?","o":["2450 N","1450 N","3450 N","5.8 N"],"a":1,"x":"Earth weight = 250 × 9.8 = 2450 N. The extra weight is 2450 − 1000 = 1450 N. The traps are giving the full Earth weight, or adding the two weights.","w":["That is the weight on Earth, not how much more it weighs; the question needs a difference.","","Added the two weights instead of finding the difference.","Subtracted the field strengths (9.8 − 4) and forgot to multiply by the mass."]}
{"id":"gph-147","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A book rests on a level table. Which description of the forces on the book, as shown in a free-body diagram, is correct?","o":["Weight of 5 N down only, because the table is not moving","Weight of 5 N down and a contact force of 5 N up from the table","Weight of 5 N down and a force of 5 N down from the book on the table","Weight of 5 N down and a contact force of 6 N up from the table"],"a":1,"x":"A free-body diagram shows only forces acting on the book. Weight acts down and the table's contact force acts up. The book is at rest, so the forces are equal and the resultant is zero.","w":["Ignores the table's contact force; a still object has balanced forces, not no forces.","","This is the force the book exerts on the table; free-body diagrams show only forces on the book.","Unequal forces would give a resultant, so the book would accelerate upwards instead of staying at rest."]}
{"id":"gph-148","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A car travels at a constant 20 m/s along a straight, level road. The driving force is 800 N. What is the resistive force on the car?","o":["0 N, because the car is not accelerating","40 N, found by dividing 800 N by 20 m/s","800 N in the same direction as the motion","800 N in the opposite direction to the motion"],"a":3,"x":"Constant speed in a straight line means the resultant force is zero. The resistive force must equal the driving force and act the opposite way. Trap: thinking no acceleration means no resistive force.","w":["Confuses zero resultant with zero resistance; the forces are there but they cancel.","Divides force by speed, which has no meaning here; balanced forces are equal in size.","Forces in the same direction would add up to a resultant and the car would accelerate.",""]}
{"id":"gph-149","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Three forces act along a straight line on a trolley: 30 N to the right, 10 N to the right and 50 N to the left. What is the resultant force?","o":["10 N to the left","10 N to the right","90 N to the left","30 N to the left"],"a":0,"x":"Add forces in the same direction: 30 + 10 = 40 N right. Subtract from 50 N left: 50 − 40 = 10 N. The larger total is to the left, so the resultant is 10 N left.","w":["","Right size but wrong direction; the left total is larger, so the resultant is left.","Added all three forces, ignoring that opposite directions must be subtracted.","Treated the 10 N force as acting left: 50 + 10 − 30 = 30 N."]}
{"id":"gph-150","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":3,"q":"Two forces of 6 N and 8 N act at right angles. A scale drawing uses 1 cm for 2 N. The lines are drawn nose to tail and the resultant measures 5 cm. What is the resultant force?","o":["14 N","5 N","10 N","7 N"],"a":2,"x":"The measured length of 5 cm must be converted using the scale: 5 × 2 N = 10 N. Trap: giving the length as the force without using the scale, or adding the forces.","w":["Added 6 N and 8 N; forces at right angles do not simply add.","Read 5 cm as 5 N without using the scale of 2 N per cm.","","Halved the total of the forces (14 ÷ 2), a mistake in using the scale."]}
{"id":"gph-151","s":"physics","t":"4.5.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A parachutist falling at high speed opens the parachute. Weight is 800 N. Air resistance suddenly becomes 1100 N. Which statement is correct just after opening?","o":["Resultant is 300 N down, so the parachutist speeds up","Resultant is 300 N up, so the parachutist slows down","Resultant is 1900 N up, so the parachutist slows down","Resultant is 300 N up, so the parachutist speeds up"],"a":1,"x":"Air resistance is larger than weight, so the resultant is 1100 − 800 = 300 N upwards. It acts against the downward motion, so the parachutist slows down. Trap: thinking an upward force must make them rise.","w":["The larger force is upwards, not downwards, so the direction is wrong.","","Added weight and air resistance; opposite forces must be subtracted.","Gets the resultant right but a force opposite to the motion slows the parachutist down."]}
{"id":"gph-152","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"Work done is measured in joules. Which of these is equal to one joule?","o":["1 N/m","1 N s","1 N m","1 N/s"],"a":2,"x":"Work done = force × distance, so 1 joule is 1 newton metre: the work done when a 1 N force moves an object 1 m in the direction of the force. Dividing or using time gives different quantities.","w":["Force divided by distance is not work; work is force multiplied by distance.","Newton seconds involve time; work uses distance, not time.","","Force divided by time is not work; work is force multiplied by distance."]}
{"id":"gph-153","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A cyclist brakes and the brake blocks get hot. Which energy transfer happens because of work done against friction?","o":["Kinetic energy of the bike to thermal energy","Kinetic energy of the bike to gravitational potential energy","Chemical energy of the brakes to kinetic energy","Thermal energy of the brakes to kinetic energy of the bike"],"a":0,"x":"Friction in the brakes does work as the bike slows. The bike's kinetic energy is transferred to thermal energy in the brakes and surroundings. The energy is not lost; it is dissipated to the surroundings.","w":["","Braking does not lift the bike; work against friction transfers kinetic energy to thermal energy, not gravitational potential energy.","The brakes have no chemical store being used; the bike loses kinetic energy as it slows.","This reverses the transfer; the bike slows down, so kinetic energy decreases and thermal energy increases."]}
{"id":"gph-154","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A girl pulls a box across a rough floor with a constant force of 25 N. The work done on the box is 450 J. How far does she move the box?","o":["11 250 m","0.056 m","475 m","18 m"],"a":3,"x":"Rearrange W = F s to get s = W ÷ F = 450 ÷ 25 = 18 m. Multiplying the numbers, or dividing force by work, gives the wrong quantity.","w":["Multiplied work by force; distance is work divided by force.","Divided force by work; the division is the wrong way round.","Added the force and the work instead of rearranging W = F s.",""]}
{"id":"gph-155","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A sledge is dragged 12 m at a constant speed along rough snow. The friction force on it is 30 N. How much energy is transferred to thermal energy?","o":["0 J","360 J","42 J","2.5 J"],"a":1,"x":"At constant speed the pulling force equals the friction force, 30 N. Work done against friction = 30 × 12 = 360 J, all transferred to thermal energy. Constant speed does not mean zero work.","w":["Thought no change in speed means no work; work is still done against friction and becomes thermal energy.","","Added force and distance instead of multiplying.","Divided force by distance; work is force multiplied by distance."]}
{"id":"gph-156","s":"physics","t":"4.5.2","lv":3,"q":"A car of mass 1600 kg is stopped by a constant braking force of 4000 N over 20 m. All its kinetic energy is transferred by the brakes. What was its initial speed?","o":["7.1 m/s","100 m/s","10 m/s","50 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Work done by the brakes = 4000 × 20 = 80 000 J = kinetic energy lost. Then ½ × 1600 × v² = 80 000, so v² = 100 and v = 10 m/s. Remember the ½ and the square root.","w":["Left out the ½ in kinetic energy, so v² came out as 50.","Found v² = 100 but forgot to take the square root.","","Divided work by mass only, forgetting the ½ and the square root."]}
{"id":"gph-157","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A metal spring is stretched by a force. It is then released and returns to its original length. Which statement correctly describes this change?","o":["Inelastic deformation, because the spring has changed shape","Elastic deformation, because it returns to its original shape","Elastic deformation, because force and extension are proportional","Inelastic deformation, because the force was removed"],"a":1,"x":"Elastic deformation means the object returns to its original shape when the force is removed. Proportionality between force and extension is a separate idea, so it is not the reason the change is elastic.","w":["Changing shape while the force acts does not make it inelastic; inelastic means it stays deformed afterwards.","","Mixes up elastic with proportional; elastic is about returning to shape, not about the straight-line graph.","Removing the force is what allows recovery; inelastic objects stay deformed after the force is removed."]}
{"id":"gph-158","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A force of 3.0 N stretches a spring by 5.0 cm. The spring is within its limit of proportionality. What is its spring constant?","o":["0.6 N/m","15 N/m","60 N/m","0.017 N/m"],"a":2,"x":"Use $F = ke$, so $k = F \\div e$. Convert 5.0 cm to 0.050 m. Then $k = 3.0 \\div 0.050 = 60$ N/m. Using 5.0 without converting gives 0.6, which is in N/cm, not N/m.","w":["Used 5.0 cm without converting to metres, so this is 0.6 N/cm, not N/m.","Multiplied force by extension in cm instead of dividing force by extension.","","Divided extension by force instead of force by extension."]}
{"id":"gph-159","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A spring has a natural length of 20 cm and a spring constant of 40 N/m. A weight of 2.0 N hangs from it. The spring is within its limit of proportionality. What is its new length?","o":["25 cm","5 cm","40 cm","20.05 cm"],"a":0,"x":"Extension $e = F \\div k = 2.0 \\div 40 = 0.05$ m, which is 5 cm. The new length is the natural length plus the extension: 20 + 5 = 25 cm. The trap is giving only the extension.","w":["","This is only the extension; the question asks for the total length of the spring.","Divided k by F (40 ÷ 2 = 20) and added it to 20 cm; the extension is F ÷ k.","Added 0.05 m to 20 cm without converting, so the units are mixed."]}
{"id":"gph-160","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A spring is stretched by 2 cm, then by 6 cm, staying within its limit of proportionality. By what factor does the elastic potential energy stored increase?","o":["3","4","36","9"],"a":3,"x":"Energy is $\\frac{1}{2}ke^2$, so it depends on the square of the extension. The extension is 3 times bigger, so the energy is $3^2 = 9$ times bigger. Saying 3 treats energy as proportional to extension.","w":["Treats energy as directly proportional to extension; the extension is squared in the equation.","Subtracted the extensions (6 − 2) instead of comparing them as a ratio.","Squared the new extension (6²) instead of squaring the ratio of the extensions.",""]}
{"id":"gph-161","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":3,"q":"A spring obeys $F = ke$ up to 12 N, where its extension is 8.0 cm. It is stretched to this point. How much energy is stored in the spring?","o":["0.96 J","0.48 J","6 J","48 J"],"a":1,"x":"Spring constant $k = 12 \\div 0.080 = 150$ N/m. Then $E = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 150 \\times 0.080^2 = 0.48$ J. Using $F \\times e$ forgets the half, because the force rises from 0 to 12 N.","w":["Used force × extension, which forgets that the force increases gradually from zero, so the half is missing.","","Used $\\frac{1}{2}ke$ without squaring the extension.","Used 8.0 cm without converting to metres, so the answer is 100 times too big."]}
{"id":"gph-162","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":1,"q":"A force of 40 N is applied at right angles to a spanner, 25 cm from the nut. What is the moment of the force about the nut?","o":["160 N m","65 N m","10 N m","1000 N m"],"a":2,"x":"Moment = force × distance. Change 25 cm to 0.25 m first. 40 × 0.25 = 10 N m. The trap is leaving the distance in centimetres, which gives 1000.","w":["Divided 40 by 0.25 instead of multiplying; a moment is force × distance.","Added 40 and 25 instead of multiplying them, so no moment was calculated.","","Did not convert 25 cm to metres, so the answer is 100 times too big for N m."]}
{"id":"gph-163","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":1,"q":"Gear A has 20 teeth and is turned clockwise. Its teeth mesh with gear B, which has 40 teeth. How does gear B turn?","o":["Clockwise, slower than A","Anticlockwise, slower than A","Anticlockwise, faster than A","Clockwise, faster than A"],"a":1,"x":"Meshed gears turn in opposite directions. The larger gear (40 teeth) has to turn more slowly than the smaller gear (20 teeth), because its teeth are passed along at the same rate.","w":["Thought meshed gears turn the same way; touching gears always turn in opposite directions.","","Got the direction right but thought the larger gear turns faster; the larger gear turns slower.","Got both wrong: meshed gears reverse direction, and the larger gear turns slower."]}
{"id":"gph-164","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A crowbar pivots on a block. A 600 N load acts 0.40 m from the pivot. The effort acts on the other side, 1.2 m from the pivot, at right angles to the bar. What effort force balances the load?","o":["300 N","1800 N","240 N","200 N"],"a":3,"x":"Load moment = 600 × 0.40 = 240 N m. At balance, effort × 1.2 = 240, so effort = 240 ÷ 1.2 = 200 N. A long lever arm means a small effort.","w":["Used 0.80 m (1.2 − 0.40) as the distance; both distances are measured from the pivot.","Swapped the distances (600 × 1.2 ÷ 0.40); the longer lever arm needs the smaller force.","Stopped at the load moment (240) and forgot to divide by the effort's distance.",""]}
{"id":"gph-165","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":2,"q":"Child X weighs 250 N and sits 2.0 m left of a seesaw pivot. Child Y weighs 360 N and sits 1.5 m right of the pivot. What is the resultant moment, and which way does it turn?","o":["40 N m clockwise","40 N m anticlockwise","110 N m clockwise","1040 N m clockwise"],"a":0,"x":"X gives 250 × 2.0 = 500 N m anticlockwise. Y gives 360 × 1.5 = 540 N m clockwise. The difference is 40 N m clockwise. Subtract moments, not forces, and do not add opposite moments.","w":["","Right size but wrong direction; the larger moment is the clockwise one from child Y.","Subtracted the forces (360 − 250); resultant moment must be found from force × distance.","Added the two moments, but they turn in opposite directions so they must be subtracted."]}
{"id":"gph-166","s":"physics","t":"4.5.4","lv":3,"q":"A light beam balances on a pivot. On the left a 90 N force acts 0.80 m from the pivot. On the right a 20 N force acts 1.0 m from it, and force F acts 2.0 m from it. What is F?","o":["52 N","36 N","26 N","24 N"],"a":2,"x":"Anticlockwise moment = 90 × 0.80 = 72 N m. Right side: 20 × 1.0 = 20 N m, so F × 2.0 = 72 − 20 = 52. F = 26 N. Remember to include every force on the right.","w":["Found the 52 N m needed but forgot to divide by F's distance of 2.0 m.","Ignored the 20 N force and used 72 ÷ 2.0; both forces on the right add to the moment.","","Divided 72 by the total distance (1.0 + 2.0); each force needs its own force × distance."]}
{"id":"gph-167","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":1,"q":"Why does air pressure get smaller as you go higher above the Earth's surface?","o":["Gravity switches off higher up, so the air no longer has any weight","There is less air above, so the weight of air pressing down is smaller","Air higher up is hotter, so its particles move apart and push less","There is more air above, so the particles are pushed upwards"],"a":1,"x":"Air pressure comes from the weight of the air above. Higher up, there is less air above you, so the pressure is lower. The air does not lose its weight, and gravity does not switch off.","w":["Gravity still acts higher up, so the air still has weight; gravity does not switch off.","","Air generally gets colder with height, and temperature is not why pressure falls.","This reverses the cause: there is less air above you higher up, not more."]}
{"id":"gph-168","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":2,"q":"A block of mass 12 kg rests on a table. The area touching the table is 0.040 m². What pressure does it exert? (g = 9.8 N/kg)","o":["2940 Pa","300 Pa","4.7 Pa","294 Pa"],"a":0,"x":"Force is the weight: 12 × 9.8 = 117.6 N. Pressure = 117.6 ÷ 0.040 = 2940 Pa. The trap is using the mass in kg as the force instead of the weight in N.","w":["","Used the mass 12 kg as the force instead of the weight in newtons.","Multiplied weight by area instead of dividing; pressure = force ÷ area.","Decimal slip: divided 117.6 by 0.40 instead of 0.040."]}
{"id":"gph-169","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":1,"q":"A gas is sealed in a syringe. How does the gas push on the walls of the syringe?","o":["Parallel to the wall, along its surface","Only downwards, because of gravity","Only upwards, because gas rises","At right angles to the wall, at every part of it"],"a":3,"x":"A fluid pushes at right angles (normal) to any surface it touches, on every wall of the container, not just the base. The trap is thinking pressure only acts downwards or upwards.","w":["Fluid pressure acts at right angles to a surface, not along it.","Gas pressure acts on every wall, not only downwards; gravity does not set its direction.","Gas pressure acts on every wall, not only upwards; gas rising is not how pressure acts.",""]}
{"id":"gph-170","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":3,"q":"A force of 60 N produces a pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. What is the area, in cm²? (1 m² = 10 000 cm²)","o":["0.03 cm²","0.3 cm²","3 cm²","30 cm²"],"a":2,"x":"Area = force ÷ pressure = 60 ÷ 200 000 = 0.0003 m². Multiply by 10 000 to get 3 cm². The trap is using the wrong number of cm² in 1 m².","w":["Used 100 cm² in 1 m² instead of 10 000, so the answer is too small.","Used 1000 cm² in 1 m² instead of 10 000.","","Used 100 000 cm² in 1 m², but 1 m² = 100 cm × 100 cm = 10 000 cm²."]}
{"id":"gph-171","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5","lv":2,"q":"A sealed bag of crisps is taken from sea level to the top of a high mountain. The bag swells up. What is the best explanation?","o":["The air inside the bag gets heavier, so it pushes outwards more","The air outside has lower pressure, so the pressure inside pushes the bag out","Gravity is weaker on the mountain, so the crisps weigh less","The air outside has more particles, so it pulls the bag outwards"],"a":1,"x":"Higher up there is less air above, so outside pressure is lower. The air sealed inside is still at the old pressure, so the push outwards is bigger than the push inwards and the bag swells.","w":["The sealed air keeps the same mass, so it does not get heavier.","","Gravity is only slightly weaker on a mountain, and the crisps' weight does not swell the bag.","There are fewer particles outside on a mountain, and air pressure pushes rather than pulls."]}
{"id":"gph-172","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A solid cube of side 0.10 m is fully under water (1000 kg/m³). Its top face is 0.20 m deep and its bottom face is 0.30 m deep. What is the upthrust on it? (g = 9.8 N/kg)","o":["1.0 N","29.4 N","9.8 N","98 N"],"a":2,"x":"Upthrust is the difference in force between the bottom and top faces. Pressure difference = 0.10 × 1000 × 9.8 = 980 Pa. Force = 980 × 0.010 m² = 9.8 N. Using only the bottom face is a trap.","w":["Left out g when finding the pressure difference; pressure = height × density × g.","Used only the bottom face (0.30 m deep) and ignored the downward force on the top face.","","Used 0.10 as the face area instead of 0.10 × 0.10 = 0.010 m², so the answer is ten times too big."]}
{"id":"gph-173","s":"physics","t":"4.5.5.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A block floats at rest in fresh water with part of it above the surface. It is moved to salt water, which is denser, and floats at rest again. Which statement is correct?","o":["The upthrust is bigger, so the block sits lower in the salt water.","Upthrust equals weight in both, so less of the block is under the surface.","The upthrust is the same, so the same volume stays under the surface.","The upthrust is bigger than the weight, so the block keeps rising."],"a":1,"x":"A floating object has upthrust equal to its weight, so upthrust is unchanged. Denser liquid gives more upthrust per unit volume submerged, so less volume needs to be under the surface. Thinking upthrust grows is the trap.","w":["Upthrust cannot grow while the weight stays the same; a denser liquid makes the block sit higher, not lower.","","Upthrust is the same, but a denser liquid gives more upthrust per unit volume, so the submerged volume must fall.","If upthrust stayed bigger than weight the block would accelerate upwards; at rest, forces are balanced."]}
{"id":"gph-175","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a sensible typical speed for a person walking?","o":["1.5 m/s","15 m/s","0.15 m/s","150 m/s"],"a":0,"x":"A typical walking speed is about 1.5 m/s. Running is about 3 m/s and cycling about 6 m/s. 15 m/s would be a fast car in town, not walking.","w":["","15 m/s is about 54 km/h, far too fast for walking. This is a factor-of-ten slip.","0.15 m/s is a very slow shuffle, ten times too small. Walking is about 1.5 m/s.","150 m/s is faster than most cars and is nowhere near walking speed."]}
{"id":"gph-176","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A toy car's distance-time graph is a straight line from (0 s, 0 m) to (20 s, 60 m), flat until 50 s, then a straight line to (70 s, 120 m). What is its speed in the last section?","o":["1.2 m/s","1.7 m/s","3.0 m/s","6.0 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Speed is the gradient. In the last section the car goes from 60 m to 120 m in 20 s (50 s to 70 s). Speed = 60 ÷ 20 = 3.0 m/s. Use changes, not the end values.","w":["This is 60 m ÷ 50 s. It uses the start values, not the changes in distance and time.","This is 120 m ÷ 70 s, the average speed overall. It does not give the speed in the last section.","","This is 120 m ÷ 20 s. It mixes the end distance with the time interval instead of the distance change."]}
{"id":"gph-177","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":2,"q":"You see lightning and hear the thunder 6 s later. The speed of sound in air is 330 m/s. Light arrives almost instantly. How far away is the lightning?","o":["55 m","336 m","198 m","1980 m"],"a":3,"x":"Use distance = speed × time = 330 × 6 = 1980 m. The light takes almost no time, so the 6 s is the travel time of the sound. Do not divide the speed by the time.","w":["This is 330 ÷ 6. Dividing speed by time does not give distance. Distance = speed × time.","This is 330 + 6. Adding a speed and a time makes no sense. Multiply them.","This has a place-value slip. 330 × 6 is 1980, not 198.",""]}
{"id":"gph-178","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":3,"q":"A car's distance-time graph is a curve that gets steeper. A tangent at 4 s passes through (2 s, 4 m) and (6 s, 28 m). What is the car's speed at 4 s?","o":["2.0 m/s","6.0 m/s","4.7 m/s","7.0 m/s"],"a":1,"x":"The speed at a moment is the gradient of the tangent. Gradient = (28 − 4) ÷ (6 − 2) = 24 ÷ 4 = 6.0 m/s. Using one point on the tangent instead of the change is the trap.","w":["This is 4 m ÷ 2 s, using only the first point of the tangent. Use the change between both points.","","This is 28 m ÷ 6 s, using only the second point. Speed needs the change in distance over the change in time.","This is 28 m ÷ 4 s. It uses the end distance with the time interval instead of the distance change."]}
{"id":"gph-179","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":1,"q":"On a velocity-time graph, a straight horizontal line at 6 m/s is drawn. What does this show about the object?","o":["It is stationary","It moves at a constant speed of 6 m/s","It accelerates at 6 m/s²","It slows down at 6 m/s²"],"a":1,"x":"A horizontal line means the velocity is not changing, so acceleration is zero. The object moves at a steady 6 m/s. The trap is reading the height of the line as acceleration.","w":["A stationary object would be on the time axis at 0 m/s, not at 6 m/s.","","Reads the velocity value as the acceleration. A flat line has zero gradient, so there is no acceleration.","Slowing down needs a line sloping downwards. A flat line has zero gradient, so the velocity is not falling."]}
{"id":"gph-180","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A car starts from rest. Its velocity rises steadily to 10 m/s in 5 s. It then travels at a constant 10 m/s for 10 s. What total distance does it travel?","o":["150 m","100 m","50 m","125 m"],"a":3,"x":"Distance is the area under the graph. Triangle: ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m. Rectangle: 10 × 10 = 100 m. Total = 125 m. The trap is using a full rectangle for the speeding-up part.","w":["Treats the whole 15 s as a rectangle at 10 m/s, ignoring that the car was still speeding up in the first 5 s.","Only counts the constant-speed part (10 × 10) and leaves out the area of the first 5 s.","Only finds 10 × 5 for the first stage and ignores the 10 s at constant speed.",""]}
{"id":"gph-181","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":1,"q":"A velocity-time graph is a straight line from 0 m/s at 0 s to 20 m/s at 8.0 s. What is the acceleration?","o":["2.5 m/s²","0.4 m/s²","160 m/s²","80 m/s²"],"a":0,"x":"Acceleration is the gradient: change in velocity ÷ time = 20 ÷ 8.0 = 2.5 m/s². The trap is inverting the fraction or finding the area instead of the gradient.","w":["","Divided time by velocity (8.0 ÷ 20), the wrong way round.","Multiplied velocity by time instead of dividing.","Found the area under the graph (½ × 8 × 20), which is distance, not acceleration."]}
{"id":"gph-182","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A train slows down uniformly from 30 m/s to 10 m/s over a distance of 400 m. What is the size of its deceleration?","o":["2.0 m/s²","0.50 m/s²","1.0 m/s²","0.050 m/s²"],"a":2,"x":"Use v² − u² = 2as. 10² − 30² = −800, so 2a × 400 = 800 in size and a = 1.0 m/s². The traps are missing the 2 and squaring the speed change instead of each speed.","w":["Left out the 2 in 2as, so 800 ÷ 400 gives double the correct value.","Used (v − u)² = 400 instead of v² − u², which is not the same thing.","","Used v − u = 20, then divided by 400. This does not use the equation with distance."]}
{"id":"gph-183","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":1,"q":"A car travels along a straight, level road at a steady 20 m/s. The engine force is 800 N forwards. What is the total resistive force on the car?","o":["0 N","Less than 800 N but not zero","800 N","Greater than 800 N"],"a":2,"x":"Steady speed in a straight line means zero resultant force (Newton's first law). So the resistive force equals the 800 N engine force. The trap is thinking a moving object needs an unbalanced forward force.","w":["Confuses 'no resultant force' with 'no resistive force'; the car still has air resistance and friction.","If resistive force were smaller, the resultant would be forwards and the car would speed up.","","If resistive force were bigger, the resultant would be backwards and the car would slow down."]}
{"id":"gph-184","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A boat of mass 400 kg has an engine force of 900 N forwards. The water resistance is 300 N backwards. What is the acceleration of the boat?","o":["1.5 m/s²","2.25 m/s²","3.0 m/s²","0.75 m/s²"],"a":0,"x":"Resultant force = 900 − 300 = 600 N. Then a = F ÷ m = 600 ÷ 400 = 1.5 m/s². The trap is using one force instead of the resultant force.","w":["","Used only the engine force (900 ÷ 400), ignoring the water resistance.","Added the forces (1200 ÷ 400) instead of subtracting the opposite force.","Used only the water resistance (300 ÷ 400) instead of the resultant force."]}
{"id":"gph-185","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A resultant force makes a 2 kg trolley accelerate at 3 m/s². The same resultant force acts on a 6 kg trolley. What is its acceleration?","o":["9 m/s²","3 m/s²","18 m/s²","1 m/s²"],"a":3,"x":"The force is 2 × 3 = 6 N. For the 6 kg trolley, a = 6 ÷ 6 = 1 m/s². More mass means less acceleration for the same force. The trap is multiplying instead of dividing.","w":["Multiplied the acceleration by 3 because the mass is three times bigger; more mass gives less acceleration.","Assumed acceleration stays the same because the force is the same, ignoring the change in mass.","Multiplied 3 × 6 instead of finding the force first and dividing by the new mass.",""]}
{"id":"gph-186","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":3,"q":"In a collision, a 1000 kg car pushes on an 8000 kg lorry with 12 000 N. Ignoring other forces, what are the force on the car and the car's acceleration?","o":["12 000 N and 1.5 m/s²","12 000 N and 12 m/s²","1500 N and 1.5 m/s²","96 000 N and 96 m/s²"],"a":1,"x":"Newton's third law: the lorry pushes back on the car with an equal 12 000 N. Then a = 12 000 ÷ 1000 = 12 m/s² for the car. The trap is using the lorry's mass or thinking the forces differ.","w":["Right force, but divided by the lorry's mass (8000 kg) instead of the car's mass.","","Thought the force on the car is smaller because the car is lighter; third law pairs are equal.","Thought the lorry's larger mass makes the force on the car bigger; third law forces are always equal in size."]}
{"id":"gph-187","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":1,"q":"What is the typical range of reaction times for a driver?","o":["2 s to 9 s","0.02 s to 0.09 s","0.2 s to 0.9 s","20 s to 90 s"],"a":2,"x":"Typical human reaction times are about 0.2 s to 0.9 s. Longer reaction times mean a longer thinking distance. Watch the decimal place: the other ranges are out by a factor of ten or more.","w":["This is ten times too big. A 2 s reaction time would be very slow for a driver.","This is ten times too small. People cannot react this quickly.","","This is a hundred times too long, a decimal-place slip; the car would travel hundreds of metres first."]}
{"id":"gph-188","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":2,"q":"A car at 10 m/s stops in a braking distance of 8 m. The braking force is the same each time. What is its braking distance when it travels at 20 m/s?","o":["32 m","16 m","24 m","64 m"],"a":0,"x":"Kinetic energy depends on speed squared. Doubling speed gives four times the kinetic energy. The same force must act over four times the distance: 4 × 8 = 32 m. The trap is thinking braking distance just doubles.","w":["","This assumes braking distance is directly proportional to speed. It is proportional to speed squared.","This adds 16 m to 8 m. Braking distance does not increase by adding a fixed amount.","This multiplies by 8, which is not the factor. Doubling speed gives a factor of 4, not 8."]}
{"id":"gph-189","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":2,"q":"A driver has a reaction time of 0.6 s. The car travels at 20 m/s. Once the brakes are applied, the braking distance is 40 m. What is the total stopping distance?","o":["12 m","52 m","73 m","40 m"],"a":1,"x":"Thinking distance = speed × reaction time = 20 × 0.6 = 12 m. Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance = 12 + 40 = 52 m. The trap is forgetting to add the thinking distance.","w":["This is only the thinking distance. The braking distance must be added.","","This divides speed by time (20 ÷ 0.6) to get thinking distance. Distance is speed × time.","This is only the braking distance. The distance travelled during the reaction time is missing."]}
{"id":"gph-190","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":2,"q":"A car brakes extremely hard from a high speed. Which statement explains why a very large deceleration is dangerous?","o":["The car's kinetic energy is destroyed, so the brakes wear out at once","A large deceleration means a small force, so the car may not stop","Thinking distance increases because the driver is under stress","A very large force is needed, which can overheat the brakes or cause a skid"],"a":3,"x":"By F = ma, a large deceleration needs a large braking force. Friction transfers kinetic energy to thermal energy in the brakes, so they can overheat. The force can also cause a skid or injure people.","w":["Energy is never destroyed. It is transferred to the thermal energy store of the brakes.","F = ma means a large deceleration needs a large force, not a small one.","Stress does not change how braking works. Thinking distance happens before the brakes are applied.",""]}
{"id":"gph-191","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.3","lv":3,"q":"A car of mass 1200 kg travels at 24 m/s. It stops in a braking distance of 48 m. Estimate the constant braking force. Use kinetic energy = ½mv² and work done = force × distance.","o":["14 400 N","600 N","7200 N","345 600 N"],"a":2,"x":"Kinetic energy = ½ × 1200 × 24² = 345 600 J. Work done by the brakes equals this, so force = 345 600 ÷ 48 = 7200 N. The traps are forgetting the ½ and stopping at the energy.","w":["This forgets the ½ in the kinetic energy formula, doubling the answer.","This calculates 1200 × 24 ÷ 48, which is not a correct equation. The kinetic energy needs speed squared.","","This is the kinetic energy in joules, not the force. It must be divided by the braking distance."]}
{"id":"gph-192","s":"physics","t":"4.5.7","lv":3,"q":"A 0.50 kg ball hits a wall at 12 m/s and bounces straight back at 8.0 m/s. It touches the wall for 0.020 s. What is the average force from the wall on the ball?","o":["500 N","100 N","300 N","200 N"],"a":0,"x":"Take the starting direction as positive. Change in momentum = 0.50 × (−8.0 − 12) = −10 kg m/s. Force = 10 ÷ 0.020 = 500 N. The trap is subtracting the speeds as if the ball did not reverse.","w":["","Subtracted 12 − 8 = 4 m/s; the ball reverses direction, so the speeds must be added.","Used only the momentum before impact (0.50 × 12), ignoring the momentum after the bounce.","Used only the momentum after the bounce (0.50 × 8.0), ignoring the momentum before impact."]}
{"id":"gph-193","s":"physics","t":"4.5.7","lv":3,"q":"A skater of mass 60 kg stands still on smooth ice. She throws a 3.0 kg ball forwards at 8.0 m/s. What is her velocity just after the throw?","o":["0.38 m/s backwards","0.050 m/s backwards","0.40 m/s backwards","0.40 m/s forwards"],"a":2,"x":"Total momentum before is zero, so it is zero after. Ball: 3.0 × 8.0 = 24 kg m/s forwards. Skater: 60 × v = 24, so v = 0.40 m/s backwards. The trap is adding the masses together.","w":["Divided by 63 kg (60 + 3.0); the skater alone has mass 60 kg after throwing the ball.","Divided the ball's mass by 60 and forgot to multiply by its speed of 8.0 m/s.","","Right size but wrong direction; the momenta must cancel, so she moves opposite to the ball."]}
{"id":"gph-194","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":1,"q":"A cork floats on a pond. A water wave travels past it. What does the cork do?","o":["It moves away from the source with the wave","It stays completely still","It bobs up and down and stays in about the same place","It drifts along at the same speed as the wave"],"a":2,"x":"Waves transfer energy, not matter. The water and the cork only vibrate about a fixed position as the wave passes. The trap is thinking the material travels along with the wave.","w":["Thinks the wave carries matter with it; waves only transfer energy.","The cork does move, because energy passes through the water and makes it vibrate.","","Thinks matter moves at the wave speed; the cork only oscillates about a fixed position."]}
{"id":"gph-195","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the amplitude of a transverse wave?","o":["The maximum displacement of a point from its rest position","The distance from one crest to the next crest","The distance from the bottom of a trough up to the top of a crest","The number of complete waves passing a point each second"],"a":0,"x":"Amplitude is the maximum displacement from the rest (undisturbed) position. The trap is measuring trough to crest, which is twice the amplitude.","w":["","This is the wavelength, not the amplitude.","Trough to crest is twice the amplitude; amplitude is measured from the rest position.","This is the frequency, not the amplitude."]}
{"id":"gph-196","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A wave machine makes 12 complete waves in 3.0 s. What is the period of the waves?","o":["4.0 s","36 s","0.33 s","0.25 s"],"a":3,"x":"Frequency = 12 ÷ 3.0 = 4.0 Hz. Period = 1 ÷ frequency = 1 ÷ 4.0 = 0.25 s. The trap is giving the frequency, 4.0, as the period.","w":["This is the frequency in Hz; period is 1 ÷ frequency.","Multiplied 12 by 3.0; waves per second needs a division.","Did 1 ÷ 3.0 and ignored the 12 waves.",""]}
{"id":"gph-197","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":2,"q":"A sound wave in air has a frequency of 680 Hz and travels at 340 m/s. What is its wavelength?","o":["2.0 m","0.50 m","231 200 m","1020 m"],"a":1,"x":"wave speed = frequency × wavelength, so wavelength = speed ÷ frequency = 340 ÷ 680 = 0.50 m. The trap is dividing the wrong way round.","w":["Divided frequency by speed (680 ÷ 340); the rearrangement is upside down.","","Multiplied speed by frequency instead of dividing.","Added the speed and frequency; the equation uses division."]}
{"id":"gph-198","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1","lv":3,"q":"On a water wave, the distance from the first crest to the sixth crest in a row is 2.0 m. In 8.0 s, 12 complete waves pass a post. What is the wave speed?","o":["0.50 m/s","3.0 m/s","0.60 m/s","0.25 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"Six crests make 5 wavelengths, so wavelength = 2.0 ÷ 5 = 0.40 m. Frequency = 12 ÷ 8.0 = 1.5 Hz. Speed = 1.5 × 0.40 = 0.60 m/s. The trap is counting crests, not gaps.","w":["Used 6 wavelengths in 2.0 m, giving 0.33 m; six crests only have 5 gaps.","Used 2.0 m as the wavelength, then did 1.5 × 2.0.","","Did 2.0 ÷ 8.0; that is not the wave speed, since 2.0 m is not the distance the wave travelled."]}
{"id":"gph-199","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A light ray hits a flat mirror. The ray makes an angle of 30° with the surface of the mirror. What is the angle of reflection?","o":["30°","120°","60°","90°"],"a":2,"x":"Angles of incidence and reflection are measured from the normal, which is at 90° to the mirror. The incident ray is 30° from the mirror, so it is 60° from the normal. Angle of reflection = 60°.","w":["Here 30° is measured from the mirror surface, but the angle of reflection is measured from the normal.","This is the angle between the incident and reflected rays (60° + 60°), not the angle of reflection.","","The normal is at 90° to the mirror, but the reflected ray does not lie along it, so 90° is wrong."]}
{"id":"gph-200","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":1,"q":"A wave is absorbed by a material. What happens to the energy of the wave?","o":["It is transferred to the material's energy stores","It is reflected back from the surface","It passes through the material unchanged","It is destroyed when the wave stops"],"a":0,"x":"When a wave is absorbed, its energy is transferred to the energy stores of the material, which often warms up. Energy is never destroyed. It is only transferred.","w":["","This describes reflection, not absorption. An absorbed wave does not bounce back.","This describes transmission. An absorbed wave does not carry on through the material.","Energy cannot be destroyed. It is transferred to the material, so thinking it disappears is a slip."]}
{"id":"gph-201","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Parallel rays of light hit a sheet of rough paper and scatter in many directions. What is the reason for this?","o":["The law of reflection does not apply to rough surfaces","The paper absorbs the light and sends it out again","The rays are refracted as they enter the paper","The normal points a different way at each point on the surface"],"a":3,"x":"This is diffuse reflection. Every ray still obeys the law of reflection. But the surface is uneven, so the normal points in different directions and the reflected rays scatter.","w":["The law of reflection applies to every ray, even on rough surfaces. The normals just differ from point to point.","Absorbing and re-emitting is not how reflection works here. The rays are reflected at the surface.","Refraction needs the ray to pass into another material. Scattering from paper is reflection.",""]}
{"id":"gph-203","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.3","lv":3,"q":"Water waves of frequency 2.0 Hz travel at 1.0 m/s in deep water. They move into shallow water, where they travel at 0.6 m/s. What is the wavelength in the shallow water?","o":["0.50 m","0.30 m","0.83 m","1.2 m"],"a":1,"x":"Frequency stays the same at the boundary, because the source sets it. Wavelength = speed ÷ frequency = 0.6 ÷ 2.0 = 0.30 m. The trap is thinking the wavelength stays at its deep-water value.","w":["This is the deep-water wavelength (1.0 ÷ 2.0). Wavelength decreases when the wave slows down.","","This comes from dividing 0.5 by 0.6. The wavelength is speed ÷ frequency, using the new speed.","This multiplies 0.6 by 2.0. The wavelength is speed divided by frequency, not multiplied."]}
{"id":"gph-204","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":3,"q":"After a large earthquake, detectors on the opposite side of the Earth pick up P-waves but no S-waves. Which statement correctly explains this?","o":["S-waves are slower than P-waves, so they have not yet arrived at the detectors.","P-waves are transverse, so they can pass through the liquid outer core.","S-waves are transverse and cannot cross the liquid outer core; P-waves can.","S-waves are absorbed by the Earth's crust, so they cannot travel very far."],"a":2,"x":"S-waves are transverse and cannot pass through liquids. The outer core is liquid, so S-waves are stopped. P-waves are longitudinal and can travel through solids and liquids. The trap is thinking S-waves are just slower.","w":["Slower waves would still arrive later, but S-waves never arrive because the liquid outer core stops them.","P-waves are longitudinal, not transverse, so this mixes up the two types of seismic wave.","","S-waves travel through solid rock easily, so the crust is not the cause; the liquid outer core is."]}
{"id":"gph-205","s":"physics","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A loudspeaker makes a 500 Hz sound in air. Which statement best explains how this sound makes the eardrum vibrate?","o":["Air particles travel from the loudspeaker to the eardrum and push it along.","Compressions and rarefactions push and pull the eardrum 500 times each second.","Sound is transverse, so air moves up and down across the eardrum and shakes it.","The ear amplifies the wave, so the eardrum vibrates more than 500 times a second."],"a":1,"x":"Sound is longitudinal: compressions push the eardrum in and rarefactions let it move out, so it vibrates at the source's 500 Hz. The trap is thinking air particles travel from the speaker; they only vibrate to and fro.","w":["Energy is transferred, but air particles only vibrate about fixed positions and do not travel from the source.","","Sound is longitudinal, so the air vibrates along the direction the wave travels, not across it.","The eardrum vibrates at the same frequency as the source; making it louder does not change the frequency."]}
{"id":"gph-206","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which property is the same for every type of electromagnetic wave travelling through a vacuum?","o":["Wavelength","Speed","Frequency","Energy carried by each wave"],"a":1,"x":"All electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum, about 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Wavelength, frequency and energy all change along the spectrum. The trap is thinking different waves travel at different speeds.","w":["Wavelength is different for each type, from very long radio waves to very short gamma rays.","","Frequency is different for each type; it rises from radio waves to gamma rays.","The energy carried is not the same for every type, so this is not shared."]}
{"id":"gph-207","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A radio wave in a vacuum has frequency $1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz. Electromagnetic waves travel at $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s in a vacuum. What is its wavelength?","o":["$2.0$ m","$0.50$ m","$4.5 \\times 10^{16}$ m","$1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ m"],"a":0,"x":"Use wavelength = wave speed ÷ frequency. $(3.0 \\times 10^{8}) \\div (1.5 \\times 10^{8}) = 2.0$ m. The trap is dividing the wrong way round, which gives 0.50 m.","w":["","This is frequency ÷ speed, so the equation was turned upside down.","This multiplies speed by frequency instead of dividing, so the units and size are wrong.","This subtracts the two numbers instead of dividing speed by frequency."]}
{"id":"gph-208","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Wave X and wave Y are both electromagnetic waves in a vacuum. X has a longer wavelength than Y. Which statement is correct?","o":["X has a higher frequency and the same speed as Y","X has a lower frequency and a lower speed than Y","X has a higher frequency and a higher speed than Y","X has a lower frequency and the same speed as Y"],"a":3,"x":"All electromagnetic waves have the same speed in a vacuum. Since speed = frequency × wavelength, a longer wavelength means a lower frequency. The trap is thinking a longer wave must travel faster.","w":["Longer wavelength means lower frequency, not higher, when the speed is fixed.","The frequency part is right, but both waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum.","Frequency is lower for the longer wavelength, and speed does not change between types in a vacuum.",""]}
{"id":"gph-209","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about all electromagnetic waves is correct?","o":["They are longitudinal waves that need a medium to travel through","They are transverse waves that need particles to travel through","They are longitudinal waves that can travel through a vacuum","They are transverse waves that can travel through a vacuum"],"a":3,"x":"Electromagnetic waves are transverse and transfer energy from a source to an absorber. They need no medium, so they cross space. The trap is mixing them up with sound, which is longitudinal and needs particles.","w":["This describes sound waves; electromagnetic waves are transverse and need no medium.","They are transverse, but they do not need particles; light reaches us from the Sun through space.","They can cross a vacuum, but they are transverse, not longitudinal.",""]}
{"id":"gph-210","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A radio wave has wavelength 600 m. A gamma ray has wavelength $6 \\times 10^{-12}$ m. Both are in a vacuum. Which statement is correct?","o":["The gamma ray has $10^{-14}$ times the frequency, and the same speed","The gamma ray has $10^{10}$ times the frequency, and the same speed","The gamma ray has $10^{14}$ times the frequency, and the same speed","The gamma ray has $10^{14}$ times the frequency and travels $10^{14}$ times faster"],"a":2,"x":"Speed is the same, so frequency is inversely proportional to wavelength. $600 \\div (6 \\times 10^{-12}) = 10^{14}$. The shorter wave has the higher frequency. The trap is thinking a higher frequency means a higher speed.","w":["This turns the ratio upside down; shorter wavelength means higher frequency, not lower.","The powers of ten were mishandled: $10^{2}$ divided by $10^{-12}$ is $10^{14}$, not $10^{10}$.","","The frequency ratio is right, but all electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum."]}
{"id":"gph-211","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":1,"q":"How are radio waves produced?","o":["By changes inside the nucleus of an atom","By heating a metal until it glows","By a steady direct current in a wire","By oscillations of charge in an electrical circuit"],"a":3,"x":"Radio waves are made when charges oscillate in an electrical circuit, for example an alternating current in a transmitter. Changes in the nucleus make gamma rays, not radio waves.","w":["Changes in the nucleus produce gamma rays, so this mixes up the source of gamma rays with radio waves.","A glowing hot metal mainly gives out infrared and visible light, not radio waves.","A steady direct current does not oscillate, so it cannot produce radio waves.",""]}
{"id":"gph-212","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A ray of light bends as it passes from air into glass. What is the reason for the bending?","o":["The speed of the light changes in the glass","The frequency of the light changes in the glass","The glass absorbs some of the light's energy","Some of the light is reflected at the glass surface"],"a":0,"x":"Refraction happens because the wave travels at a different speed in a different substance. The frequency stays the same. The trap is thinking frequency changes.","w":["","Frequency stays the same at a boundary; only the speed and wavelength change.","Absorption reduces the energy of the wave but does not make the ray change direction.","Reflection sends light back into the air, which is a different effect from bending."]}
{"id":"gph-213","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Light from outside passes through a glass window but not a brick wall. A radio inside the house still picks up a radio signal. What does this show?","o":["All electromagnetic waves are absorbed the same way by one substance","Whether a wave is transmitted or absorbed depends on the substance and the wavelength","Brick walls reflect all radio waves but absorb light","Only waves with a higher frequency can pass through solid objects"],"a":1,"x":"Different substances absorb, reflect or transmit different EM waves. Brick blocks light but lets radio waves through, so the effect depends on both the substance and the wavelength.","w":["Brick stops light but lets radio waves through, so one substance treats different waves differently.","","If brick reflected all radio waves, the radio inside would get no signal at all.","Radio waves have a lower frequency than light, yet they pass through the brick while light does not."]}
{"id":"gph-214","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A transmitter has an alternating current of frequency 1.5 MHz in its aerial. It sends out radio waves at $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s. What is the wavelength of the waves?","o":["$4.5 \\times 10^{14}$ m","$0.005$ m","$200$ m","$2000$ m"],"a":2,"x":"1.5 MHz = $1.5 \\times 10^{6}$ Hz. The waves have the same frequency as the current. Wavelength = speed ÷ frequency = $3.0 \\times 10^{8} \\div 1.5 \\times 10^{6} = 200$ m.","w":["This multiplies speed by frequency instead of using wavelength = speed ÷ frequency.","This divides frequency by speed, which is the wrong way round.","","This uses 1.5 × 10⁵ Hz, a slip in converting MHz, since 1 MHz is 10⁶ Hz."]}
{"id":"gph-215","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.2","lv":3,"q":"Light of frequency $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz goes from air into glass, where its speed is $2.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s. What is its wavelength in the glass?","o":["$6.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m","$4.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m","$2.5 \\times 10^{6}$ m","$1.0 \\times 10^{23}$ m"],"a":1,"x":"Frequency stays the same in glass. Only speed changes, so wavelength = $2.0 \\times 10^{8} \\div 5.0 \\times 10^{14} = 4.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m. It is shorter than in air.","w":["This uses the speed in air, $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, instead of the speed in glass.","","This divides frequency by speed, which is the wrong way round.","This multiplies speed by frequency instead of using wavelength = speed ÷ frequency."]}
{"id":"gph-216","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A person spends many hours in strong sunlight without protection. Which hazard of ultraviolet radiation is a risk for them?","o":["Organs deep inside the body are heated from the inside","The skin becomes radioactive and gives out radiation","Premature skin ageing and a higher risk of skin cancer","Bones become weaker and break much more easily"],"a":2,"x":"Ultraviolet can damage skin cells. This leads to premature ageing and a higher risk of skin cancer. Trap: UV does not make anything radioactive, and it does not heat the body's insides.","w":["This describes microwaves heating cells inside the body, not the main hazard of ultraviolet.","Radiation does not make skin radioactive; exposure only damages cells. This confuses irradiation with contamination.","","UV is not linked to weakened bones; this is an invented effect."]}
{"id":"gph-217","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which type of electromagnetic wave is used both to send signals to and from satellites and to heat food in a kitchen appliance?","o":["Radio waves","Microwaves","Infrared","Ultraviolet"],"a":1,"x":"Microwaves pass through the atmosphere to reach satellites. Water in food absorbs them, so food heats up. Trap: radio waves are for TV and radio broadcasting, not cooking.","w":["Radio waves are used for broadcasting and do not heat food; this mixes up two long-wavelength waves.","","Infrared heats food in grills, but it is not used for satellite signals; this only fits half the question.","Ultraviolet is used in energy efficient lamps and sun tanning, not for cooking or satellite signals."]}
{"id":"gph-218","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A radiographer takes X-ray images of patients all day. She stands behind a lead screen each time. Why is this needed for her but not for each patient?","o":["She is exposed many times, so her total exposure could raise her cancer risk","X-rays make people who are near them radioactive","The lead screen stops the X-rays from heating her skin","Patients are less affected because X-rays only harm adults"],"a":0,"x":"X-rays are ionising and can damage DNA, causing cancer. One scan gives a small exposure, but a radiographer's repeated exposure adds up. Trap: X-rays do not make people radioactive.","w":["","X-rays do not make anyone radioactive; this confuses being irradiated with being contaminated.","The danger is ionisation damaging cells, not heating; this mixes up X-rays with infrared or microwaves.","X-rays can harm people of any age; the real difference is how many times each person is exposed."]}
{"id":"gph-219","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Why can X-rays be used to show a broken bone on an image?","o":["Bones reflect X-rays back to the detector, while soft tissue lets them pass","Soft tissue absorbs more X-rays than bone does","Bones give out X-rays when they are broken","Bones absorb more X-rays than soft tissue, so fewer reach the detector behind them"],"a":3,"x":"X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by dense bone. Less X-ray reaches the detector behind bones, so the bones show up and breaks can be seen. Trap: reversing which one absorbs more.","w":["The image forms from X-rays passing through or being absorbed, not reflected back; this confuses X-rays with light.","This reverses the idea; soft tissue absorbs fewer X-rays than dense bone.","Bones do not emit X-rays; this confuses the source with the absorbing material.",""]}
{"id":"gph-220","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"In which option is every pairing of an electromagnetic wave with a use correct?","o":["Radio waves: cooking food; infrared: thermal imaging; X-rays: medical images","Microwaves: satellite signals; ultraviolet: fibre optic communication; gamma rays: treating cancer","Infrared: electrical heaters; microwaves: satellite communications; X-rays: medical imaging","Gamma rays: sun tanning; visible light: fibre optics; radio waves: television"],"a":2,"x":"Infrared heaters, microwave satellite links and X-ray imaging are all correct. In the others, one pairing is wrong, such as radio for cooking or UV for fibre optics. Check each pairing in a row.","w":["Radio waves do not cook food; microwaves do. The other two uses are right, but one error makes the row wrong.","Fibre optic communication uses visible light, not ultraviolet. The other two pairings are right.","","Sun tanning uses ultraviolet, not gamma rays, which are dangerous and ionising. The other two pairings are right."]}
{"id":"gph-221","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Parallel rays of light hit a convex lens, travelling parallel to its axis. What happens to the rays after they pass through the lens?","o":["They spread out, as if they came from the principal focus","They pass straight through without bending","They bend inwards and meet at the principal focus","They reflect back from the lens surface"],"a":2,"x":"A convex lens is a converging lens. It refracts parallel rays inwards so they meet at the principal focus. Spreading out is what a concave lens does, so the trap is mixing up the two lenses.","w":["This is what a concave lens does; a convex lens makes rays come together.","Only a ray through the centre of a thin lens goes straight on, while parallel rays refract.","","The rays refract as they pass into and out of the glass, so reflection is not the main effect."]}
{"id":"gph-222","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A student views an insect that is 3 mm long through a magnifying lens. The image is 1.5 cm long. What is the magnification?","o":["5","0.5","0.2","12"],"a":0,"x":"Magnification = image height ÷ object height. Put both in mm first: 1.5 cm = 15 mm. So 15 ÷ 3 = 5. The trap is forgetting to convert the units.","w":["","This comes from dividing 1.5 by 3 without converting cm to mm.","This is object height ÷ image height, which is the wrong way round.","This subtracts the heights (15 − 3), but magnification is found by dividing."]}
{"id":"gph-223","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":1,"q":"White light shines through a red filter. Which statement explains what the filter does?","o":["It adds red light to the white light","It reflects every colour except red","It absorbs red light and lets the other colours through","It absorbs every colour except red, which is transmitted"],"a":3,"x":"A filter transmits only the wavelengths of its own colour and absorbs the rest. A red filter lets red light through and absorbs the other colours. It does not add light.","w":["A filter cannot make new light; it only removes colours from the light that hits it.","The other colours are absorbed, not reflected, because a filter works by absorbing light.","This is the wrong way round, because a red filter transmits red and absorbs the others.",""]}
{"id":"gph-224","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A convex lens is used as a magnifying glass. The object is closer to the lens than the principal focus. Which describes the image?","o":["Real, inverted and magnified","Virtual, upright and magnified","Real, inverted and smaller than the object","Virtual, upright and smaller than the object"],"a":1,"x":"When the object is inside the focal length, the rays leaving the lens still spread out. The image is virtual, upright and magnified, on the same side as the object. The trap is using the image type for a distant object.","w":["A real, inverted image needs the object beyond the principal focus.","","A smaller, real, inverted image is made when the object is beyond twice the focal length, not inside the focus.","The image is virtual and upright, but a magnifying glass makes it bigger, not smaller."]}
{"id":"gph-225","s":"physics","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A ray from the top of an object travels parallel to the axis of a concave lens and is refracted outwards. Traced back, it meets the ray through the lens centre. Describe the image.","o":["Virtual, upright and smaller than the object","Real, inverted and smaller than the object","Virtual, upright and larger than the object","Real, upright and the same size as the object"],"a":0,"x":"Rays from a concave lens diverge, so they never actually meet. They only appear to meet when traced backwards. The image is virtual, upright and smaller, on the object's side. Real images need rays that really meet.","w":["","A concave lens never makes a real image because the rays spread out and do not meet.","A concave lens always makes a smaller image; a larger virtual image comes from a convex magnifier.","The rays do not actually meet, so the image is not real, and it is smaller than the object."]}
{"id":"gph-226","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":1,"q":"Two identical metal blocks sit in a room at 20°C. One block is at 80°C and the other is at 20°C. Which statement is correct?","o":["Only the 80°C block emits infrared radiation.","The 80°C block emits more infrared radiation each second.","Both blocks emit the same infrared radiation because they are identical.","Only the 20°C block absorbs infrared radiation from the room."],"a":1,"x":"All objects emit infrared radiation. The hotter an object is, the more it emits in a given time. Being identical does not matter, because the temperatures are different.","w":["All objects emit infrared, not just hot ones. The 20°C block also emits.","","Identical size and material do not make emission equal. Emission depends on temperature, and these differ.","All objects absorb infrared. The hot block absorbs some from the room too."]}
{"id":"gph-227","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":1,"q":"A metal bar is heated from 500°C to 1000°C. How does the radiation it emits change?","o":["It emits less radiation, and the peak wavelength is longer.","It emits more radiation, and the peak wavelength is longer.","It emits more radiation, and the peak wavelength stays the same.","It emits more radiation, and the peak wavelength is shorter."],"a":3,"x":"A hotter object emits more radiation each second. The peak of its spread of wavelengths also moves to shorter wavelengths. A common slip is to think the wavelength does not change.","w":["Hotter objects emit more radiation, not less, so this is wrong.","The intensity rise is right, but the peak moves to shorter wavelengths, not longer.","The wavelength distribution changes with temperature. The peak shifts to shorter wavelengths.",""]}
{"id":"gph-228","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":2,"q":"A stone in steady sunshine absorbs more radiation each second than it emits. What happens to the stone?","o":["Its temperature rises until it emits as much as it absorbs, then stays constant.","Its temperature rises at a steady rate for as long as the Sun shines.","Its temperature stays constant because it emits all the energy it absorbs.","Its temperature falls because hotter objects emit more radiation."],"a":0,"x":"If absorption is greater than emission, the stone warms up. As it warms, it emits more. When emission equals absorption, the temperature becomes constant. The trap is thinking it keeps rising forever.","w":["","Warming makes the stone emit more, so the rise slows and stops when emission equals absorption.","Emission is smaller than absorption at first. That imbalance makes the temperature rise.","Absorbing more than it emits means energy builds up, so the stone warms, not cools."]}
{"id":"gph-229","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":2,"q":"Which change would most likely make the Earth's average temperature rise?","o":["Snow covering a larger area of land","More sunlight being reflected by clouds","Melting ice exposing dark ocean that absorbs more sunlight","The Earth emitting more infrared into space than it absorbs"],"a":2,"x":"Dark ocean absorbs more radiation than white ice, which reflects it. Less reflection means more absorbed energy, so the Earth warms. The other options would cool it.","w":["Snow reflects sunlight into space, so less is absorbed and this cools the Earth.","Reflecting sunlight into space means less is absorbed, which tends to cool the Earth.","","Emitting more than absorbing means the Earth loses energy overall, so it cools."]}
{"id":"gph-230","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":3,"q":"The Sun's emission peaks in visible light. The Earth's emission peaks in the infrared. What is the best explanation for this difference?","o":["The Earth is hotter, so its peak wavelength is longer.","The Earth is much cooler, so its peak wavelength is longer.","The Earth only reflects radiation and does not emit any.","The Earth is much cooler, so its peak wavelength is shorter."],"a":1,"x":"The wavelength at which an object emits most strongly depends on its temperature. The Earth is much cooler than the Sun, so its peak is at longer wavelengths, in the infrared. Hotter objects peak at shorter wavelengths.","w":["The Earth is cooler than the Sun, not hotter. Also, hotter objects have shorter peak wavelengths.","","All objects emit radiation, including the Earth. Reflection is a separate process.","Cooler objects have longer peak wavelengths, not shorter. This reverses the relationship."]}
{"id":"gph-236","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A current flows through a long straight wire. Which describes the magnetic field around the wire?","o":["Straight lines running parallel to the wire","Circles around the wire, further apart as you move away","Circles around the wire, closer together as you move away","Straight lines pointing outwards from the wire like spokes"],"a":1,"x":"A straight wire has circular field lines centred on the wire. The field is strongest close to the wire, so the lines are closest together there and spread out with distance. The field does not point along or away from the wire.","w":["The field goes around the wire, not along it, so this confuses the wire's direction with the field's direction.","","The field is strongest near the wire, so the lines are closest there; this reverses the effect of distance.","Magnetic field lines from a current form closed loops around the wire, not spokes pointing outwards."]}
{"id":"gph-237","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A long solenoid carries a current. What is the magnetic field like inside the solenoid, away from its ends?","o":["Zero, because the fields from each turn cancel out","Weak, and getting stronger towards the ends","Strong at the ends only, and zero in the middle","Strong, and the same strength and direction everywhere"],"a":3,"x":"Inside a long solenoid the field is strong and uniform, so the lines are parallel and evenly spaced. Outside, the field looks like that of a bar magnet. The trap is thinking the turns cancel.","w":["The fields from each turn add together inside the coil rather than cancelling.","The field inside is strong and uniform, not weak and changing along the coil.","The poles are at the ends, but the field is strong all through the inside, including the middle.",""]}
{"id":"gph-238","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does winding a wire into a coil make its magnetic field stronger than the same wire held straight?","o":["The fields from each turn add together inside the coil","Coiling the wire increases the current flowing in it","Coiling the wire turns it into a permanent magnet","Coiling the wire makes it shorter, so the field gets stronger"],"a":0,"x":"Each loop of wire has its own circular field. Inside the coil these fields point the same way and add up, giving a strong field. Coiling does not change the current or make the wire a permanent magnet.","w":["","The current is set by the cell and the wire's resistance, and coiling does not change either.","The coil is only magnetic while current flows, so this confuses electromagnets with permanent magnets.","Coiling does not change the wire's length; the strengthening comes from the turns adding their fields together."]}
{"id":"gph-239","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A plotting compass sits on the axis of a solenoid, just beyond one end. A current flows and the needle settles. The cell is then connected the other way round. What happens to the needle?","o":["It stays where it is, because the current is still flowing","It spins round and round without stopping","It turns round to point the opposite way","It turns by about 90° and stops"],"a":2,"x":"Reversing the current reverses the direction of the solenoid's field. The compass needle lines up with the field, so it turns through about 180°. The strength of the field stays the same.","w":["The field direction has reversed, so thinking only the presence of current matters is the slip.","The new field is steady, so the needle settles in one direction rather than spinning.","","The field is reversed, not rotated by 90°, so the needle flips through about 180°."]}
{"id":"gph-240","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A scrapyard crane uses an electromagnet with a soft iron core. The core is not made of steel. Why is soft iron the better choice?","o":["Soft iron is a better electrical conductor than steel","Soft iron magnetises easily and loses it when the current stops","Soft iron stays magnetised, so it holds the scrap with no current","Soft iron is a stronger permanent magnet than steel"],"a":1,"x":"Soft iron becomes magnetised when current flows, strengthening the field. It loses its magnetism when the current is switched off, so the crane can drop the scrap. Steel would stay magnetised, so the crane could not release the load.","w":["Conduction is not the reason, because the current is carried by the coil wire, not the core.","","Staying magnetised is the problem, because the crane must switch the field off to drop the scrap.","Soft iron is not a permanent magnet; it is only strongly magnetic while the current flows."]}
{"id":"gph-241","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A loudspeaker makes a note of frequency 400 Hz. A coil joined to the cone sits in the field of a fixed permanent magnet. Which statement explains how the sound is made?","o":["A steady direct current pushes the cone out and back 400 times a second.","A 400 Hz a.c. in the coil keeps reversing the force, so the cone vibrates.","The permanent magnet's poles swap 400 times a second, moving the coil and cone.","The coil spins 400 times a second on a split-ring commutator, shaking the cone."],"a":1,"x":"The coil carries alternating current. By the motor effect, the force on it reverses each time the current reverses. The coil and cone vibrate at the frequency of the current. The permanent magnet stays fixed.","w":["A steady direct current gives a steady force in one direction, so the cone would not vibrate. This mixes up d.c. and a.c.","","A permanent magnet cannot swap its poles. It is the current in the coil that changes direction.","This describes a d.c. motor. A loudspeaker coil vibrates to and fro and does not spin or use a commutator."]}
{"id":"gph-242","s":"physics","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A wire 8.0 cm long is at right angles to a magnetic field of flux density 0.30 T. The force on the wire is 0.12 N. What is the current in the wire?","o":["0.050 A","1.5 A","5.0 A","0.40 A"],"a":2,"x":"Rearrange F = BIl to I = F ÷ (B × l). Change the length to metres first: 8.0 cm = 0.080 m. I = 0.12 ÷ (0.30 × 0.080) = 5.0 A. Using 8.0 cm directly is the trap.","w":["Used the length as 8.0 instead of 0.080 m, so forgot to convert centimetres to metres.","Divided force by length only and left out the magnetic flux density B.","","Divided force by B only and left out the length l."]}
{"id":"gph-243","s":"physics","t":"4.7.3","lv":3,"q":"The coil in a simple a.c. generator is turned twice as fast. The magnet and the coil are not changed. What happens to the peak potential difference and the frequency of the output?","o":["Peak pd doubles; frequency stays the same","Peak pd stays the same; frequency doubles","Peak pd doubles; frequency doubles","Peak pd halves; frequency doubles"],"a":2,"x":"Turning faster means the coil cuts the magnetic field lines faster, so the induced pd is larger. One turn also takes half the time, so the frequency doubles. The trap is thinking only one of them changes.","w":["Forgot that each turn takes half the time, so the frequency doubles too.","Forgot that cutting field lines faster induces a bigger pd, so the peak rises.","","Got the pd backwards; cutting field lines faster makes the induced pd bigger, not smaller."]}
{"id":"gph-244","s":"physics","t":"4.7.3","lv":3,"q":"A step-up transformer changes 25 000 V to 400 000 V. Its secondary coil has 3200 turns. Assume it is ideal. How many turns does the primary coil have?","o":["51 200","200","2 000","16"],"a":1,"x":"Vp/Vs = np/ns, so np = 3200 × 25 000 ÷ 400 000 = 200. A step-up transformer has fewer turns on the primary. The trap is turning the ratio upside down.","w":["Inverted the ratio to get 3200 × 16, but a step-up transformer has fewer primary turns.","","Power-of-ten slip in the calculation; 3200 ÷ 16 is 200, not 2000.","Gave the voltage ratio 400 000 ÷ 25 000 as the turns, forgetting to use the 3200 turns."]}
{"id":"gph-245","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a dwarf planet in our Solar System?","o":["Titan","Halley's comet","Pluto","Mars"],"a":2,"x":"Pluto is a dwarf planet. Titan is a moon of Saturn, Halley's comet is a comet, and Mars is one of the eight planets. The trap is thinking any small round world is a dwarf planet.","w":["Titan is a moon orbiting Saturn, and moons are not dwarf planets even when they are large.","Halley's comet is a small body of ice and dust, which is a different kind of object from a dwarf planet.","","Mars is one of the eight planets, so it is not a dwarf planet."]}
{"id":"gph-246","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the Sun is correct?","o":["It is one of hundreds of billions of stars in the Milky Way","It is the only star in the Milky Way","It is a planet that orbits the centre of the Milky Way","It is at the centre of the Milky Way galaxy"],"a":0,"x":"The Sun is just one star among hundreds of billions in the Milky Way galaxy. It is at the centre of our Solar System, not of the galaxy. The trap is mixing up the Solar System with the galaxy.","w":["","The Milky Way contains hundreds of billions of stars, so the Sun is not the only one.","The Sun is a star that gives out its own light, not a planet that only reflects light.","The Sun is at the centre of the Solar System, not the galaxy, which mixes up the Solar System and the Milky Way."]}
{"id":"gph-247","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":2,"q":"As our Sun formed, a protostar became hot enough at its centre for a new process to start. What happened?","o":["Uranium nuclei split and released energy","Hydrogen gas burned with oxygen and gave out heat","The planets fell in and made the star hotter","Hydrogen nuclei joined to make helium and released energy"],"a":3,"x":"When the centre of the protostar was hot enough, nuclear fusion began. Hydrogen nuclei join to form helium and release energy. The trap is confusing fusion (joining) with fission (splitting) or with burning.","w":["Splitting uranium nuclei is fission, but stars release energy by joining light nuclei in fusion.","Burning is a chemical reaction needing oxygen, but the Sun's energy comes from nuclear fusion.","The planets stayed in orbit and did not fall in, and the star's energy comes from fusion.",""]}
{"id":"gph-249","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A cloud of dust and gas collapses under gravity. Before fusion starts, why does the temperature at its centre rise?","o":["Fusion is already releasing energy in the cloud","Nearby stars shine on the cloud and heat the centre","Particles fall inwards, speed up and collide more often","Newly forming planets rub on the gas and cause friction"],"a":2,"x":"Gravity pulls the particles towards the centre. They speed up and collide more often, so the temperature rises. This happens before fusion. The trap is thinking fusion is the cause of the first heating.","w":["Fusion only starts once the centre is already very hot, so it cannot cause the first heating.","Light from other stars is far too weak; the heating comes from the collapse of the cloud itself.","","Friction with forming planets does not heat the centre; the heating comes from particles falling inwards under gravity."]}
{"id":"gph-250","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A star is in the main sequence stage and stays the same size for billions of years. What is the reason it is stable?","o":["Gravity stops pulling inwards once fusion starts","Inward gravity is balanced by outward pressure from fusion","Fusion energy is balanced by the star's magnetic field","Inward gravity is balanced by the star's spinning"],"a":1,"x":"In a main sequence star, gravity pulling inwards is balanced by the outward pressure from the energy released by fusion. Gravity never switches off. Forgetting that gravity is still acting is the trap.","w":["Gravity never stops acting on a star. It keeps pulling inwards, and fusion pressure balances it.","","The magnetic field is not what balances the star. Outward fusion pressure balances inward gravity.","Spin is not the balancing effect. The outward pressure comes from energy released by fusion, not from rotation."]}
{"id":"gph-251","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What happens in the core of a main sequence star such as the Sun, where energy is released by nuclear fusion?","o":["Helium nuclei split to make hydrogen nuclei","Hydrogen atoms burn with oxygen to make water","Uranium nuclei split and release energy","Hydrogen nuclei join together to make helium nuclei"],"a":3,"x":"In a main sequence star, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium nuclei and release energy. Fusion means joining nuclei. Confusing it with fission, or with chemical burning, is the trap.","w":["This reverses the process. Fusion joins small nuclei into larger ones, so helium is made from hydrogen.","Stars do not burn fuel in a chemical reaction. The energy comes from nuclear fusion, not combustion.","Splitting uranium is nuclear fission, used in power stations. Stars release energy by fusion.",""]}
{"id":"gph-252","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A star has a mass much greater than the Sun. Which statement about its life cycle is correct?","o":["It may end up as a black hole after a supernova","It ends as a white dwarf after a supernova","It becomes a red giant, then fades into a black dwarf","It has no main sequence stage because it uses its fuel too quickly"],"a":0,"x":"A star much bigger than the Sun becomes a red supergiant, explodes as a supernova, and leaves a neutron star or a black hole. White dwarfs are the end of Sun-sized stars. Mixing up the two routes is the trap.","w":["","White dwarfs form from Sun-sized stars, which do not have a supernova. Bigger stars leave neutron stars or black holes.","The red giant to black dwarf route is for Sun-sized stars. Massive stars become red supergiants and explode.","Every star has a main sequence stage, whatever its mass. Big stars just spend less time in it."]}
{"id":"gph-253","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A cloud of dust and gas collapses to form a protostar. What makes the temperature rise enough for fusion to start?","o":["Hydrogen in the cloud burns with oxygen","Fusion is already happening in the cloud before it collapses","Gravity pulls the particles together, so they move faster and collide more","Light from nearby stars heats the cloud until it explodes"],"a":2,"x":"Gravity pulls the cloud's particles inwards, so they speed up and the temperature rises. When it is hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fuse and a main sequence star starts. Fusion is the result of the heating, not its cause.","w":["Stars are not heated by chemical burning. Heating comes from gravity squashing the gas, and later fusion.","Fusion only starts once the protostar is hot and dense enough. It is the result of the heating, not its cause.","","Nearby starlight is not the cause, and the cloud does not explode. Gravity pulling the cloud together heats it."]}
{"id":"gph-254","s":"physics","t":"4.8.1.2","lv":3,"q":"Gold is found on Earth. Which is the best explanation for how its atoms were made?","o":["Fusion in the Sun's core made gold, which later formed the Earth","A supernova made it; it later joined the cloud that formed the Solar System","Fusion in a red giant makes all the elements, including gold, as the star swells","Gold formed as the Earth cooled down from hot hydrogen gas"],"a":1,"x":"Fusion in stars only makes elements up to iron. Elements heavier than iron, such as gold, form in a supernova. The explosion scatters them, and they later join a new cloud that forms stars and planets.","w":["The Sun's core makes helium, not gold. Fusion in stars only goes up to iron, so gold is made elsewhere.","","Fusion in a red giant stops at iron. Elements heavier than iron need the conditions in a supernova.","Cooling cannot create new elements. Atoms of different elements are made by nuclear reactions in stars."]}
{"id":"gph-260","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"A line in the spectrum of a star in a distant galaxy is at a longer wavelength than the same line measured in a lab. What does this show about the galaxy?","o":["It is moving towards us","It is moving away from us","It is not moving at all","It is hotter than our Sun"],"a":1,"x":"A longer observed wavelength is red-shift. Red-shift happens when a source moves away from the observer. The trap is thinking a longer wavelength means the galaxy is approaching.","w":["Confuses red-shift with blue-shift; a galaxy moving towards us would show a shorter wavelength.","","If the galaxy were not moving, the line would match the lab wavelength, with no shift.","Red-shift is caused by motion, not temperature; the wavelength change says nothing about how hot it is."]}
{"id":"gph-261","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes the Big Bang theory?","o":["The Universe has always been the same size and has never changed","A huge cloud of gas collapsed inwards to make the Universe","Matter exploded outwards into empty space that was already there","The Universe began from a tiny, hot, dense region and is still expanding"],"a":3,"x":"The Big Bang theory says the Universe began from a very small, extremely hot and dense region and has been expanding ever since. The trap is picturing an explosion into empty space that already existed.","w":["This describes an unchanging Universe, which does not fit the evidence of red-shift and expansion.","Gets the direction wrong; the Big Bang is an expansion from a small region, not a collapse.","Treats it as an ordinary explosion; the theory says space itself began and expanded.",""]}
{"id":"gph-262","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Why does the red-shift of light from almost all galaxies support the Big Bang theory?","o":["The galaxies are moving apart, so they must have been closer together in the past","The galaxies are moving towards each other, so the Universe will collapse","The galaxies are all the same distance away, so the Universe is not changing","The galaxies are burning out, so their light has turned red"],"a":0,"x":"Red-shift shows galaxies moving away from us and from each other. Going back in time, they were closer together, which fits a start from a very small region. Red-shift is not about stars cooling.","w":["","Confuses red-shift with blue-shift; red-shift shows galaxies moving apart, not towards each other.","Red-shift increases with distance, so galaxies are at different distances and the Universe is changing.","Red-shift is a change in wavelength caused by motion, not light turning red as stars burn out."]}
{"id":"gph-263","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":3,"q":"A spectral line has wavelength 656 nm in a lab. From galaxy A it is measured as 660 nm. From galaxy B it is measured as 676 nm. Which conclusion is correct?","o":["Both are moving away from us, and A is moving faster than B","A is moving towards us and B is moving away from us","Both are moving away from us, and B is moving faster than A","Both are moving towards us, and B is moving faster than A"],"a":2,"x":"Both wavelengths are longer than 656 nm, so both galaxies are red-shifted and moving away. B's shift (20 nm) is bigger than A's (4 nm), so B is moving faster. The trap is reading the smaller shift as faster.","w":["Reverses the link; the larger shift belongs to B, so B is moving faster, not A.","A's wavelength is longer than 656 nm, so it is red-shifted; a small shift is still a shift away.","","Longer wavelength means red-shift and moving away; moving towards us would give a shorter wavelength."]}
{"id":"gph-264","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Since 1998, observations of supernovae suggest that distant galaxies are moving away from us faster and faster. What do scientists suggest this means?","o":["The expansion of the Universe is slowing down because of gravity","The expansion of the Universe is speeding up, and dark energy may explain this","The Universe has stopped expanding and is now steady","The red-shift measurements must be wrong because galaxies cannot speed up"],"a":1,"x":"Galaxies receding ever faster means the expansion is speeding up. Scientists suggest dark energy as a possible cause. Gravity alone would slow the expansion, which is the trap.","w":["Gravity would slow expansion, but the observations show galaxies receding faster, not slower.","","Galaxies moving away faster and faster means the expansion has not stopped.","Rejects the evidence; the supernova observations are accepted and used to propose dark energy."]}
{"id":"gph-fig-001","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a cyclist's journey. What is the cyclist's speed in the last part of the journey, when the line rises again after the flat part?","o":["0.33 m/s","3.0 m/s","1.4 m/s","2.0 m/s"],"a":1,"x":"The last part runs from 50 s to 70 s. Distance rises from 40 m to 100 m. Speed is the gradient: 60 ÷ 20 = 3.0 m/s.","w":["Gradient upside down: found time ÷ distance (20 ÷ 60) instead of distance ÷ time.","","Used the whole journey: total distance ÷ total time (100 ÷ 70), not the last part only.","Read the first part of the journey (40 m in 20 s), not the last part."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,100],"xstep":10,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"distance / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[20,40],[50,40],[70,100]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-002","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows two people, A and B, walking along the same straight path. B walks towards A's starting point. How much greater is B's speed than A's speed?","o":["4.0 m/s","6.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","1.0 m/s"],"a":2,"x":"A's gradient: 80 m in 40 s = 2.0 m/s. B's line falls 120 m in 30 s, so B's speed is 4.0 m/s. The difference is 4.0 − 2.0 = 2.0 m/s.","w":["Gave B's speed only and did not subtract A's speed.","Added the two speeds instead of finding the difference.","","Used 40 s for B's time (120 ÷ 40 = 3.0 m/s, then 3.0 − 2.0), but B's line reaches zero at 30 s."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,40],"y":[0,120],"xstep":10,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"distance from A's start / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[40,80]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,120],[30,0]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-010","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows force against extension for a spring. The line is straight up to point P. How much energy is stored in the spring when it is stretched to P? Give your answer in joules.","o":["0.72 J","36 J","0.36 J","72 J"],"a":2,"x":"Read P: force 12 N, extension 6 cm = 0.06 m. The line is straight, so energy = area under the graph = ½ × F × e = ½ × 12 × 0.06 = 0.36 J.","w":["Used F × e and forgot the half. Energy stored is the area of a triangle under the line, not a rectangle.","Used the extension in cm (6) without converting to metres. This gives 36, which is not in joules.","","Forgot the half and kept the extension in cm. Both slips together give 72."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,14],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"extension / cm","ylabel":"force / N","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[6,12],[7,13],[8,13.6]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[6,12],"label":"P"}]}}
{"id":"gph-fig-006","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how the activity of a radioactive source changes with time. Points P and Q are marked on the curve. Use them to find the half-life of the source.","o":["10 minutes","20 minutes","40 minutes","60 minutes"],"a":1,"x":"Read P: 400 Bq at 20 minutes. Read Q: 100 Bq at 60 minutes. Activity goes 400 → 200 → 100, which is two halvings. They take 40 minutes, so one half-life is 40 ÷ 2 = 20 minutes.","w":["Divides the 40 minutes by 4, as if the fall from 400 to 100 were four halvings instead of two.","","Takes the whole time from P to Q as one half-life, but the activity halves twice in that time.","Reads the time at Q instead of the time taken for the activity to halve."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,800],"xstep":10,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"time / minutes","ylabel":"activity / Bq","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,800.0],[0.5,786.256479],[1.0,772.749063],[1.5,759.473697],[2.0,746.426393],[2.5,733.603235],[3.0,721.00037],[3.5,708.614015],[4.0,696.440451],[4.5,684.476021],[5.0,672.717132],[5.5,661.160255],[6.0,649.801917],[6.5,638.638709],[7.0,627.667278],[7.5,616.88433],[8.0,606.286627],[8.5,595.870985],[9.0,585.634278],[9.5,575.573432],[10.0,565.685425],[10.5,555.967288],[11.0,546.416103],[11.5,537.029001],[12.0,527.803164],[12.5,518.735822],[13.0,509.824251],[13.5,501.065775],[14.0,492.457765],[14.5,483.997636],[15.0,475.682846],[15.5,467.510899],[16.0,459.479342],[16.5,451.585762],[17.0,443.827789],[17.5,436.203093],[18.0,428.709385],[18.5,421.344414],[19.0,414.10597],[19.5,406.991877],[20.0,400.0],[20.5,393.128239],[21.0,386.374532],[21.5,379.736848],[22.0,373.213197],[22.5,366.801617],[23.0,360.500185],[23.5,354.307008],[24.0,348.220225],[24.5,342.23801],[25.0,336.358566],[25.5,330.580127],[26.0,324.900959],[26.5,319.319355],[27.0,313.833639],[27.5,308.442165],[28.0,303.143313],[28.5,297.935493],[29.0,292.817139],[29.5,287.786716],[30.0,282.842712],[30.5,277.983644],[31.0,273.208051],[31.5,268.514501