{"id":"gph-fig-035","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows Jo and Sam travelling along the same straight path from the same start. Sam sets off later than Jo. How long after Sam sets off does Sam catch up with Jo?","o":["80 s","200 s","60 s","40 s"],"a":3,"x":"Sam catches Jo where the lines cross, at 80 s on the time axis. Sam set off at 40 s. Time after Sam sets off = 80 − 40 = 40 s.","w":["Read the crossing time but forgot Sam started 40 s after Jo. This is the time since Jo set off.","Read the distance at the crossing point from the wrong axis. 200 is metres, not seconds.","Used the time between Sam's start and Sam's end of the line (100 − 40), not the crossing point.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,120],"y":[0,300],"xstep":20,"ystep":50,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"distance from start / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[120,300]],"label":"Jo","c":0},{"pts":[[40,0],[100,300]],"label":"Sam","c":1}],"points":[],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-047","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":2,"q":"A Leslie cube has four faces at the same temperature. Use the graph to compare the best and the poorest emitters. How much more infrared does the best give out each second?","o":["70 times as much","8 times as much","7 times as much","2 times as much"],"a":1,"x":"The best emitter is matt black at 80 units. The poorest is shiny silver at 10 units. Divide: 80 ÷ 10 = 8, so the best emitter gives out 8 times as much infrared.","w":["Subtracts 80 − 10 to get 70, then treats the difference as if it were a ratio.","","Uses the matt white bar (70) with the poorest bar (10) instead of the best bar.","Compares the best bar with shiny black (40), not the poorest emitter, shiny silver."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Matt black","Matt white","Shiny black","Shiny silver"],"vals":[80,70,40,10],"y":[0,90],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"surface of cube","ylabel":"infrared detected / units"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-048","s":"physics","t":"4.6.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows two black bodies, A and B, with the peak of each curve marked. Which object is hotter, and at what wavelength does it emit most strongly?","o":["Object B, at 4 µm","Object B, at 3 µm","Object A, at 3 µm","Object A, at 8 µm"],"a":2,"x":"A hotter object emits more radiation at every wavelength, so its curve is higher. That is A. Its peak is at 3 µm on the x-axis. Hotter objects peak at shorter wavelengths.","w":["Thinks the curve peaking at the longer wavelength is the hotter one. Hotter objects peak at shorter wavelengths.","Picks B, then reads the peak wavelength of curve A instead of curve B.","","Picks the right object but reads the peak height (8) from the y-axis, not the wavelength from the x-axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,9],"y":[0,10],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"wavelength / µm","ylabel":"intensity / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0.05625,0.0],[0.1125,0.0],[0.16875,0.0],[0.225,0.0],[0.28125,0.0],[0.3375,0.0],[0.39375,0.0],[0.45,0.0],[0.50625,1e-06],[0.5625,1.6e-05],[0.61875,0.000107],[0.675,0.000515],[0.73125,0.001885],[0.7875,0.005575],[0.84375,0.013933],[0.9,0.030414],[0.95625,0.059462],[1.0125,0.106158],[1.06875,0.175717],[1.125,0.272943],[1.18125,0.40174],[1.2375,0.564747],[1.29375,0.76315],[1.35,0.996632],[1.40625,1.263478],[1.4625,1.560755],[1.51875,1.884573],[1.575,2.230348],[1.63125,2.593079],[1.6875,2.967592],[1.74375,3.348749],[1.8,3.731626],[1.85625,4.111636],[1.9125,4.484626],[1.96875,4.846934],[2.025,5.195413],[2.08125,5.527439],[2.1375,5.840898],[2.19375,6.134156],[2.25,6.406025],[2.30625,6.655719],[2.3625,6.882813],[2.41875,7.087193],[2.475,7.269016],[2.53125,7.428663],[2.5875,7.566703],[2.64375,7.683855],[2.7,7.780957],[2.75625,7.858936],[2.8125,7.918781],[2.86875,7.961526],[2.925,7.988226],[2.98125,7.999943],[3.0375,7.997737],[3.09375,7.982648],[3.15,7.955693],[3.20625,7.917857],[3.2625,7.870087],[3.31875,7.813292],[3.375,7.748335],[3.43125,7.676035],[3.4875,