{"id":"gph-fig-049","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how much a spectral line is shifted for four galaxies. In the lab the line is at 434 nm. What wavelength is measured from the furthest galaxy?","o":["8 nm","426 nm","442 nm","436 nm"],"a":2,"x":"The furthest galaxy has the tallest bar, which is 8 nm. Red-shift means the wavelength gets longer, so add the shift to the lab value: 434 + 8 = 442 nm.","w":["Gives the size of the shift, not the wavelength that is measured.","Takes the shift away, as if the light were blue-shifted. Red-shift makes the wavelength longer.","","Reads the shortest bar, which is the nearest galaxy, not the furthest."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["50","100","150","200"],"vals":[2,4,6,8],"y":[0,10],"ystep":2,"xlabel":"distance / million light-years","ylabel":"increase in wavelength / nm"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-050","s":"physics","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how fast galaxies move away from us at different distances. How much faster is galaxy Q moving away than galaxy P?","o":["4000 km/s","6000 km/s","2000 km/s","200 km/s"],"a":0,"x":"Read the speeds from the vertical axis. Q is at 6000 km/s and P is at 2000 km/s. The difference is 6000 − 2000 = 4000 km/s.","w":["","Reads only the speed of Q. The question asks how much faster Q is than P, so P's speed must be taken away.","Reads only the speed of P and does not find the difference.","Takes the two distances (300 − 100) from the horizontal axis. The question is about speed."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,400],"y":[0,8000],"xstep":100,"ystep":2000,"xlabel":"distance / million light-years","ylabel":"speed of recession / km/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[400,8000]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[100,2000],"label":"P"},{"at":[300,6000],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-018","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Resistors A and B are connected in parallel to a 4.0 V cell. Use the graph to find the total current from the cell.","o":["0.20 A","0.15 A","0.30 A","0.10 A"],"a":2,"x":"In parallel, each resistor has the full 4.0 V. At 4.0 V the graph gives 0.20 A for A and 0.10 A for B. Branch currents add: 0.20 + 0.10 = 0.30 A.","w":["Read only resistor A at 4.0 V and forgot the current in B.","Averaged the two currents. In parallel the branch currents add up to the total.","","Read only resistor B at 4.0 V and forgot the current in A."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,0.5],"xstep":2,"ystep":0.1,"xlabel":"potential difference / V","ylabel":"current / A","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,0.5]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[10,0.25]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-019","s":"physics","t":"4.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A cell, two lamps and a resistor R are in series. The graph shows the potential difference across the cell and each lamp. The current is 0.50 A. What is the resistance of R?","o":["16 Ω","24 Ω","2.0 Ω","8.0 Ω"],"a":3,"x":"In series the p.d. across the components adds up to the cell's p.d. R gets 12 − 2 − 6 = 4 V. Resistance = V ÷ I = 4 ÷ 0.50 = 8.0 Ω.","w":["Used the total p.d. across the two lamps (8 V) instead of the p.d. across R.","Used the cell's 12 V across R. The lamps share that p.d. with R in series.","Multiplied 4 V by 0.50 A instead of dividing. That gives power, not resistance.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Cell","Lamp 1","Lamp 2"],"vals":[12,2,6],"y":[0,12],"ystep":2,"xlabel":"component","ylabel":"potential difference / V"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-028","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how the activity of a source changes with time. How long does the activity take to fall to one eighth of its starting value?","o":["10 s","30 s","40 s","100 s"],"a":1,"x":"The starting activity is 800 Bq. One eighth of this is 100 Bq. The curve reaches 100 Bq at 30 s. That is three half-lives of 10 s each.","w":["Read the time for one half-life (activity halves to 400 Bq) instead of three halvings.","","Read the time where the activity is 50 Bq, which is one sixteenth, not one eighth.","Used the activity value of 100 Bq as if it were a time on the time axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,50],"y":[0,800],"xstep":10,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"activity / Bq","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,800.0],[0.3125,782.85765],[0.625,766.082625],[0.9375,749.667054],[1.25,733.603235],[1.5625,717.88363],[1.875,702.500864],[2.1875,687.447719],[2.5,672.717132],[2.8125,658.302191],[3.125,644.196133],[3.4375,630.392338],[3.75,616.88433],[4.0625,603.665771],[4.375,590.730458],[4.6875,578.072323],[5.0,565.685425],[5.3125,553.563953],[5.625,541.702219],[5.9375,530.094657],[6.25,518.735822],[6.5625,507.620383],[6.875,496.743125],[7.1875,486.098944],[7.5,475.682846],[7.8125,465.489944],[8.125,455.515454],[8.4375,445.754697],[8.75,436.203093],[9.0625,426.85616],[9.375,417.709513],[9.6875,408.758859],[10.0,400.0],[10.3125,391.428825],[10.625,383.041312],[10.9375,374.833527],[11.25,366.801617],[11.5625,358.941815],[11.875,351.250432],[12.1875,343.72386],[12.5,336.358566],[12.8125,329.151096],[13.125,322.098066],[13.4375,315.196169],[13.75,308.442165],[14.0625,301.832886],[14.375,295.365229],[14.6875,289.036161],[15.0,282.842712],[15.3125,276.781976],[15.625,270.851109],[15.9375,265.047329],[16.25,259.367911],[16.5625,253.810191],[16.875,248.371562],[17.1875,243.049472],[17.5,237.841423],[17.8125,232.744972],[18.125,227.757727],[18.4375,222.877349],[18.75,218.101547],[19.0625,213.42808],[19.375,208.854756],[19.6875,204.37943],[20.0,200.0],[20.3125,195.714412],[20.625,191.520656],[20.9375,187.416763],[21.25,183.400809],[21.5625,179.470908],[21.875,175.625216],[22.1875,171.86193],[22.5,168.179283],[22.8125,164.575548],[23.125,161.049033],[23.4375,157.598085],[23.75,154.221083],[24.0625,150.916443],[24.375,147.682615],[24.6875,144.518081],[25.0,141.421356],[25.3125,138.390988],[25.625,135.425555],[25.9375,132.523664],[26.25,129.683955],[26.5625,126.905096],[26.875,124.185781],[27.1875,121.524736],[27.5,118.920712],[27.8125,116.372486],[28.125,113.878863],[28.4375,111.438674],[28.75,109.050773],[29.0625,106.71404],[29.375,104.427378],[29.6875,102.189715],[30.0,100.0],[30.3125,97.857206],[30.625,95.760328],[30.9375,93.708382],[31.25,91.700404],[31.5625,89.735454],[31.875,87.812608],[32.1875,85.930965],[32.5,84.089642],[32.8125,82.287774],[33.125,80.524517],[33.4375,78.799042],[33.75,77.110541],[34.0625,75.458221],[34.375,73.841307],[34.6875,72.25904],[35.0,70.710678],[35.3125,69.195494],[35.625,67.712777],[35.9375,66.261832],[36.25,64.841978],[36.5625,63.452548],[36.875,62.092891],[37.1875,60.762368],[37.5,59.460356],[37.8125,58.186243],[38.125,56.939432],[38.4375,55.719337],[38.75,54.525387],[39.0625,53.35702],[39.375,52.213689],[39.6875,51.094857],[40.0,50.0],[40.3125,48.928603],[40.625,47.880164],[40.9375,46.854191],[41.25,45.850202],[41.5625,44.867727],[41.875,43.906304],[42.1875,42.965482],[42.5,42.044821],[42.8125,41.143887],[43.125,40.262258],[43.4375,39.399521],[43.75,38.555271],[44.0625,37.729111],[44.375,36.920654],[44.6875,36.12952],[45.0,35.355339],[45.3125,34.597747],[45.625,33.856389],[45.9375,33.130916],[46.25,32.420989],[46.5625,31.726274],[46.875,31.046445],[47.1875,30.381184],[47.5,29.730178],[47.8125,29.093121],[48.125,28.469716],[48.4375,27.859669],[48.75,27.262693],[49.0625,26.67851],[49.375,26.106845],[49.6875,25.547429],[50.0,25.0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-029","s":"physics","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The bar chart shows the number of undecayed nuclei in a sample as time passes. How many nuclei have decayed by 90 minutes?","o":["100 thousand","300 thousand","700 thousand","400 thousand"],"a":2,"x":"At 0 minutes there are 800 thousand nuclei. At 90 minutes, 100 thousand are left. Decayed nuclei = 800 − 100 = 700 thousand.","w":["Gave the number still undecayed at 90 minutes, not the number that have decayed.","Subtracted the bar at 90 minutes from the bar at 30 minutes, so only part of the decay was counted.","","Found the number that decayed in the first 30 minutes only (800 − 400), not by 90 minutes."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["0","30","60","90","120"],"vals":[800,400,200,100,50],"y":[0,800],"ystep":100,"xlabel":"time / minutes","ylabel":"undecayed nuclei / thousands"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-013","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows CO₂ released per kWh of electricity. A town switches from oil to gas for its electricity. By how much does CO₂ per kWh fall?","o":["400 g/kWh","200 g/kWh","600 g/kWh","1000 g/kWh"],"a":1,"x":"Read the oil bar: 600 g/kWh. Read the gas bar: 400 g/kWh. The fall is 600 − 400 = 200 g/kWh.","w":["Reads only the gas bar and forgets to subtract it from the oil bar.","","Reads only the oil bar and does not find the difference.","Adds the oil and gas bars instead of finding the difference."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Coal","Oil","Gas","Nuclear","Wind"],"vals":[800,600,400,0,0],"y":[0,1000],"ystep":200,"xlabel":"energy resource","ylabel":"CO₂ released / g per kWh"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-014","s":"physics","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows demand and the output of a solar farm over one day. At 16:00, how much power must other power stations supply to meet demand?","o":["20 MW","80 MW","60 MW","100 MW"],"a":2,"x":"At 16:00 the demand line reads 80 MW. The solar farm line reads 20 MW. Other stations must supply the gap: 80 − 20 = 60 MW.","w":["Reads only the solar farm line and ignores the demand.","Reads only the demand line and forgets the solar farm supplies some power.","","Adds the demand and solar values instead of finding the gap."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,24],"y":[0,100],"xstep":4,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time of day / hour","ylabel":"power / MW","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[4,20],[8,60],[12,60],[16,80],[20,80],[24,20]],"label":"Demand","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[4,0],[8,20],[12,60],[16,20],[20,0],[24,0]],"label":"Solar farm","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-026","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph is for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature. The gas is compressed from P to Q. By how much does its pressure increase?","o":["60 kPa","20 kPa","10 kPa","100 kPa"],"a":1,"x":"Read P: 40 kPa at 30 cm³. Read Q: 60 kPa at 20 cm³. The increase is 60 − 40 = 20 kPa. In a smaller volume, particles hit the walls more often, so pressure rises.","w":["Reads the pressure at Q only and does not subtract the pressure at P.","","Uses the change in volume (10 cm³) as if it were the change in pressure, so reads the wrong axis.","Adds the two pressures (40 + 60) instead of finding the difference."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[0,120],"xstep":10,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"volume / cm³","ylabel":"pressure / kPa","lines":[{"pts":[[10,120],[12,100],[15,80],[20,60],[24,50],[30,40],[40,30],[50,24],[60,20]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[30,40],"label":"P"},{"at":[20,60],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-027","s":"physics","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph is for a fixed mass of gas in a sealed rigid container. The gas at A is cooled until its temperature in kelvin is halved. Use the graph to find the new pressure.","o":["120 kPa","30 kPa","60 kPa","80 kPa"],"a":2,"x":"Read A: 120 kPa at 300 K. Halved temperature is 150 K. The line is straight through the origin, so pressure is proportional to kelvin temperature. At 150 K the graph reads 60 kPa.","w":["Thinks fixed volume means the pressure cannot change, but cooling slows the particles and lowers pressure.","Halves the temperature and then halves the pressure again, so it is halved twice.","","Takes 100 K off the temperature (to 200 K) instead of halving it, then reads the pressure there."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,400],"y":[0,160],"xstep":50,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"temperature / K","ylabel":"pressure / kPa","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[100,40],[200,80],[300,120],[400,160]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[300,120],"label":"A"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-022","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A solid is heated at a steady rate. Use the graph to decide at which labelled point the particles are gaining potential energy but their kinetic energy is not increasing.","o":["Point P","Point Q","Point R","None of the points"],"a":1,"x":"Point Q is on the flat part of the graph. The temperature is constant, so the particles' kinetic energy is not rising. The energy supplied is changing the state (melting), so potential energy increases.","w":["P is on a rising part. There the temperature rises, so the particles' kinetic energy increases as the solid warms.","","R is on a rising part. There the liquid is warming up, so its particles' kinetic energy increases.","Energy is still supplied on a flat line. It changes the state instead of the temperature."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,16],"y":[0,120],"xstep":2,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[4,60],[10,60],[14,100]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[2,40],"label":"P"},{"at":[8,60],"label":"Q"},{"at":[12,80],"label":"R"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-023","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A 0.25 kg sample is heated by a 100 W heater. All of the heater's energy goes into the sample. Use the graph to find the specific latent heat of fusion of the substance.","o":["120 000 J/kg","160 000 J/kg","30 000 J/kg","7500 J/kg"],"a":0,"x":"The flat part runs from 100 s to 400 s, so it lasts 300 s. Energy = 100 × 300 = 30 000 J. L = E ÷ m = 30 000 ÷ 0.25 = 120 000 J/kg.","w":["","Used the time at the end of the flat part, 400 s, instead of how long it lasts, 300 s.","Found the energy but forgot to divide by the mass.","Multiplied the energy by the mass instead of dividing by it."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,600],"y":[0,80],"xstep":100,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,10],[100,40],[400,40],[500,60]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-024","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Each material is made into a 2.0 kg block. Use the chart to find the energy needed to raise the temperature of block C by 20 °C.","o":["20 000 J","80 000 J","40 000 J","2000 J"],"a":2,"x":"The bar for C shows a specific heat capacity of 1000 J/kg °C. Energy = m × c × Δθ = 2.0 × 1000 × 20 = 40 000 J.","w":["Left out the mass. Used 1000 × 20 only.","Read the bar for A (2000 J/kg °C) instead of the bar for C.","","Multiplied only mass and specific heat capacity, and forgot the temperature change."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["A","B","C","D"],"vals":[2000,500,1000,4000],"y":[0,4500],"ystep":500,"xlabel":"material","ylabel":"specific heat capacity / J/kg °C"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-025","s":"physics","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A 2.0 kg block is heated. The graph shows its temperature against the energy supplied. Use the graph to find the specific heat capacity of the block.","o":["1 J/kg °C","2000 J/kg °C","4000 J/kg °C","1000 J/kg °C"],"a":3,"x":"From A to P the temperature rises by 40 °C while 80 kJ (80 000 J) is supplied. c = E ÷ (m × Δθ) = 80 000 ÷ (2.0 × 40) = 1000 J/kg °C.","w":["Left the energy in kJ. 80 kJ must be changed to 80 000 J before dividing.","Divided the energy by the temperature change only, and forgot to divide by the mass.","Multiplied the energy by the mass instead of dividing by it.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,70],"xstep":10,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"energy supplied / kJ","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[80,60]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[0,20],"label":"A"},{"at":[80,60],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-020","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The heater is switched on for 2.0 minutes. Use the graph to work out the energy the heater transfers.","o":["6000 J","360 J","360 000 J","240 000 J"],"a":2,"x":"Read the heater's power from the graph: 3.0 kW = 3000 W. Time = 2.0 min = 120 s. E = Pt = 3000 × 120 = 360 000 J.","w":["Used 2 minutes instead of 120 s, so worked out 3000 × 2 = 6000 J.","Used 3 instead of 3000 for the power, so the kilowatts were not changed to watts.","","Read the kettle's bar instead of the heater's bar, so used 2 kW instead of 3 kW."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Kettle","Toaster","Heater","Microwave"],"vals":[2,1,3,1.5],"y":[0,3.5],"ystep":0.5,"xlabel":"appliance","ylabel":"power rating / kW"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-021","s":"physics","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The same power is sent along the cables. Transformers raise the p.d. from the value at P to the value at Q. Use the graph to find by what factor the power lost in the cables changes.","o":["It becomes 4 times smaller","It becomes 2 times smaller","It becomes 12 times smaller","It becomes 8 times smaller"],"a":0,"x":"Read P: 16 MW lost at the lower p.d. Read Q: 4 MW lost at the higher p.d. 16 ÷ 4 = 4. So the loss is 4 times smaller. A higher p.d. means a lower current.","w":["","Thought the loss falls in the same ratio as the p.d. rises, so doubling p.d. only halves the loss.","Subtracted the two losses (16 − 4 = 12) and used the difference as the factor.","Read the loss at Q as 2 MW instead of 4 MW, then divided 16 by 2."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[100,400],"y":[0,16],"xstep":50,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"potential difference / kV","ylabel":"power lost in cables / MW","lines":[{"pts":[[100.0,16.0],[101.875,15.416463],[103.75,14.864276],[105.625,14.341235],[107.5,13.845322],[109.375,13.374694],[111.25,12.927661],[113.125,12.502671],[115.0,12.098299],[116.875,11.713232],[118.75,11.34626],[120.625,10.996268],[122.5,10.662224],[124.375,10.343173],[126.25,10.038232],[128.125,9.746579],[130.0,9.467456],[131.875,9.200153],[133.75,8.944013],[135.625,8.698422],[137.5,8.46281],[139.375,8.236643],[141.25,8.019422],[143.125,7.810682],[145.0,7.609988],[146.875,7.416931],[148.75,7.231128],[150.625,7.05222],[152.5,6.879871],[154.375,6.713764],[156.25,6.5536],[158.125,6.3991],[160.0,6.25],[161.875,6.106051],[163.75,5.967018],[165.625,5.832681],[167.5,5.702829],[169.375,5.577266],[171.25,5.455805],[173.125,5.338268],[175.0,5.22449],[176.875,5.11431],[178.75,5.00758],[180.625,4.904156],[182.5,4.803903],[184.375,4.706693],[186.25,4.612405],[188.125,4.520921],[190.0,4.432133],[191.875,4.345935],[193.75,4.262227],[195.625,4.180914],[197.5,4.101907],[199.375,4.025118],[201.25,3.950465],[203.125,3.87787],[205.0,3.807258],[206.875,3.73855