{"id":"gph-fig-015","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows current against potential difference for a filament lamp. Use points P and Q to decide how the resistance of the lamp changes from P to Q.","o":["Falls from 0.125 Ω to 0.0625 Ω","Rises from 8 Ω to 16 Ω","Rises from 2 Ω to 16 Ω","Stays the same because current keeps rising"],"a":1,"x":"Use R = V ÷ I at each point. At P: 4.0 V ÷ 0.50 A = 8 Ω. At Q: 16 V ÷ 1.0 A = 16 Ω. The resistance rises because the filament gets hotter as the current increases.","w":["Divided current by potential difference (I ÷ V), which turns the equation upside down.","","Multiplied V by I at each point instead of dividing, which gives power, not resistance.","Thought a rising line means constant resistance. Only a straight line through the origin means that."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,16],"y":[0,1.25],"xstep":2,"ystep":0.25,"xlabel":"potential difference / V","ylabel":"current / A","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.1,0.079057],[0.2,0.111803],[0.3,0.136931],[0.4,0.158114],[0.5,0.176777],[0.6,0.193649],[0.7,0.209165],[0.8,0.223607],[0.9,0.237171],[1.0,0.25],[1.1,0.262202],[1.2,0.273861],[1.3,0.285044],[1.4,0.295804],[1.5,0.306186],[1.6,0.316228],[1.7,0.32596],[1.8,0.33541],[1.9,0.344601],[2.0,0.353553],[2.1,0.362284],[2.2,0.37081],[2.3,0.379144],[2.4,0.387298],[2.5,0.395285],[2.6,0.403113],[2.7,0.410792],[2.8,0.41833],[2.9,0.425735],[3.0,0.433013],[3.1,0.44017],[3.2,0.447214],[3.3,0.454148],[3.4,0.460977],[3.5,0.467707],[3.6,0.474342],[3.7,0.480885],[3.8,0.48734],[3.9,0.49371],[4.0,0.5],[4.1,0.506211],[4.2,0.512348],[4.3,0.518411],[4.4,0.524404],[4.5,0.53033],[4.6,0.53619],[4.7,0.541987],[4.8,0.547723],[4.9,0.553399],[5.0,0.559017],[5.1,0.564579],[5.2,0.570088],[5.3,0.575543],[5.4,0.580948],[5.5,0.586302],[5.6,0.591608],[5.7,0.596867],[5.8,0.60208],[5.9,0.607248],[6.0,0.612372],[6.1,0.617454],[6.2,0.622495],[6.3,0.627495],[6.4,0.632456],[6.5,0.637377],[6.6,0.642262],[6.7,0.647109],[6.8,0.65192],[6.9,0.656696],[7.0,0.661438],[7.1,0.666146],[7.2,0.67082],[7.3,0.675463],[7.4,0.680074],[7.5,0.684653],[7.6,0.689202],[7.7,0.693722],[7.8,0.698212],[7.9,0.702673],[8.0,0.707107],[8.1,0.711512],[8.2,0.715891],[8.3,0.720243],[8.4,0.724569],[8.5,0.728869],[8.6,0.733144],[8.7,0.737394],[8.8,0.74162],[8.9,0.745822],[9.0,0.75],[9.1,0.754155],[9.2,0.758288],[9.3,0.762398],[9.4,0.766485],[9.5,0.770552],[9.6,0.774597],[9.7,0.778621],[9.8,0.782624],[9.9,0.786607],[10.0,0.790569],[10.1,0.794512],[10.2,0.798436],[10.3,0.80234],[10.4,0.806226],[10.5,0.810093],[10.6,0.813941],[10.7,0.817771],[10.8,0.821584],[10.9,0.825379],[11.0,0.829156],[11.1,0.832917],[11.2,0.83666],[11.3,0.840387],[11.4,0.844097],[11.5,0.847791],[11.6,0.851469],[11.7,0.855132],[11.8,0.858778],[11.9,0.862409],[12.0,0.866025],[12.1,0.869626],[12.2,0.873212],[12.3,0.876784],[12.4,0.880341],[12.5,0.883883],[12.6,0.887412],[12.7,0.890926],[12.8,0.894427],[12.9,0.897914],[13.0,0.901388],[13.1,0.904848],[13.2,0.908295],[13.3,0.911729],[13.4,0.91515],[13.5,0.918559],[13.6,0.921954],[13.7,0.925338],[13.8,0.928709],[13.9,0.932068],[14.0,0.935414],[14.1,0.938749],[14.2,0.942072],[14.3,0.945384],[14.4,0.948683],[14.5,0.951972],[14.6,0.955249],[14.7,0.958514],[14.8,0.961769],[14.9,0.965013],[15.0,0.968246],[15.1,0.971468],[15.2,0.974679],[15.3,0.97788],[15.4,0.981071],[15.5,0.984251],[15.6,0.987421],[15.7,0.990581],[15.8,0.99373],[15.9,0.99687],[16.0,1.0]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[4,0.5],"label":"P"},{"at":[16,1],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-016","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows current against potential difference for resistors A and B at constant temperature. They are connected in series to a cell and the current is 0.2 A. Find the potential difference of the cell.","o":["2 V","4 V","6 V","8 V"],"a":3,"x":"In series the current is the same in both. Go across from 0.2 A: line A gives 2 V and line B gives 6 V. The cell's potential difference is the sum: 2 + 6 = 8 V.","w":["Read the potential difference across resistor A only, so B was left out.","Took the mean of 2 V and 6 V. The potential differences should be added.","Read the potential difference across resistor B only, so A was left out.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,1.2],"xstep":2,"ystep":0.2,"xlabel":"potential difference / V","ylabel":"current / A","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[12,1.2]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[12,0.4]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-017","s":"physics","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how the resistance of a thermistor changes with temperature. The thermistor is at 40 °C and is connected across a 6.0 V cell. Use the graph to find the current in it.","o":["0.020 A","50 A","1800 A","0.15 A"],"a":0,"x":"At 40 °C the graph gives a resistance of 300 Ω. Current = potential difference ÷ resistance = 6.0 ÷ 300 = 0.020 A.","w":["","Divided resistance by potential difference (300 ÷ 6), so the equation was upside down.","Multiplied 6.0 V by 300 Ω instead of dividing.","Used the temperature, 40, as the resistance instead of reading the resistance from the graph."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,800],"xstep":10,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"resistance / Ω","lines":[{"pts":[[0,800],[10,600],[20,460],[30,360],[40,300],[50,250],[60,210],[70,180],[80,160]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-036","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A train's journey is shown on the graph. Find the acceleration of the train in the final sloping section of the graph.","o":["3.0 m/s²","−4.0 m/s²","−3.0 m/s²","−0.33 m/s²"],"a":2,"x":"In the last section the velocity falls from 16 m/s to 4 m/s in 4 s. Acceleration = (4 − 16) ÷ 4 = −3.0 m/s². The minus sign shows the train is slowing down.","w":["Ignores that the line slopes downwards, so the sign is lost. Slowing down is a negative acceleration.","Uses 16 as the change in velocity. The train does not stop, so the change is 4 − 16 = −12 m/s.","","Turns the gradient upside down by dividing time by change in velocity, instead of change in velocity by time."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,16],"y":[0,20],"xstep":2,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[4,16],[10,16],[14,4]],"label":"train","c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-037","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A skateboarder starts from rest and speeds up as the graph shows. The acceleration stays the same. Use v² = u² + 2as to find the speed after 54 m.","o":["18 m/s","324 m/s","12 m/s","6.0 m/s"],"a":0,"x":"Gradient = 12 ÷ 4 = 3.0 m/s². Then v² = 0 + 2 × 3.0 × 54 = 324. Take the square root: v = 18 m/s.","w":["","Finds v² = 324 but forgets to take the square root.","Reads the top velocity on the graph, which is the speed after 4 s, not after 54 m.","Inverts the gradient to get 0.33 m/s², then uses it in the equation."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,5],"y":[0,14],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[4,12]],"label":"skater","c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-038","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.1.5","lv":3,"q":"The curve shows how a car's velocity changes. A tangent is drawn at point P. Use the tangent to find the car's acceleration at P.","o":["2.0 m/s²","2.4 m/s²","0.25 m/s²","4.0 m/s²"],"a":3,"x":"Acceleration at P is the gradient of the tangent. It passes through (2 s, 0 m/s) and (5 s, 12 m/s). Gradient = (12 − 0) ÷ (5 − 2) = 4.0 m/s².","w":["Divides the velocity at P (8 m/s) by the time (4 s). That is the gradient of a line from the origin, not the tangent.","Divides 12 by 5 and ignores that the tangent starts at 2 s, not at 0 s.","Turns the gradient upside down: time change ÷ velocity change.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,18],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.0375,0.000703],[0.075,0.002812],[0.1125,0.006328],[0.15,0.01125],[0.1875,0.017578],[0.225,0.025313],[0.2625,0.034453],[0.3,0.045],[0.3375,0.056953],[0.375,0.070312],[0.4125,0.085078],[0.45,0.10125],[0.4875,0.118828],[0.525,0.137813],[0.5625,0.158203],[0.6,0.18],[0.6375,0.203203],[0.675,0.227813],[0.7125,0.253828],[0.75,0.28125],[0.7875,0.310078],[0.825,0.340312],[0.8625,0.371953],[0.9,0.405],[0.9375,0.439453],[0.975,0.475312],[1.0125,0.512578],[1.05,0.55125],[1.0875,0.591328],[1.125,0.632812],[1.1625,0.675703],[1.2,0.72],[1.2375,0.765703],[1.275,0.812812],[1.3125,0.861328],[1.35,0.91125],[1.3875,0.962578],[1.425,1.015313],[1.4625,1.069453],[1.5,1.125],[1.5375,1.181953],[1.575,1.240312],[1.6125,1.300078],[1.65,1.36125],[1.6875,1.423828],[1.725,1.487813],[1.7625,1.553203],[1.8,1.62],[1.8375,1.688203],[1.875,1.757812],[1.9125,1.828828],[1.95,1.90125],[1.9875,1.975078],[2.025,2.050312],[2.0625,2.126953],[2.1,2.205],[2.1375,2.284453],[2.175,2.365312],[2.2125,2.447578],[2.25,2.53125],[2.2875,2.616328],[2.325,2.702813],[2.3625,2.790703],[2.4,2.88],[2.4375,2.970703],[2.475,3.062813],[2.5125,3.156328],[2.55,3.25125],[2.5875,3.347578],[2.625,3.445312],[2.6625,3.544453],[2.7,3.645],[2.7375,3.746953],[2.775,3.850312],[2.8125,3.955078],[2.85,4.06125],[2.8875,4.168828],[2.925,4.277812],[2.9625,4.388203],[3.0,4.5],[3.0375,4.613203],[3.075,4.727813],[3.1125,4.843828],[3.15,4.96125],[3.1875,5.080078],[3.225,5.200312],[3.2625,5.321953],[3.3,5.445],[3.3375,5.569453],[3.375,5.695312],[3.4125,5.822578],[3.45,5.95125],[3.4875,6.081328],[3.525,6.212813],[3.5625,6.345703],[3.6,6.48],[3.6375,6.615703],[3.675,6.752812],[3.7125,6.891328],[3.75,7.03125],[3.7875,7.172578],[3.825,7.315313],[3.8625,7.459453],[3.9,7.605],[3.9375,7.751953],[3.975,7.900313],[4.0125,8.050078],[4.05,8.20125],[4.0875,8.353828],[4.125,8.507812],[4.1625,8.663203],[4.2,8.82],[4.2375,8.978203],[4.275,9.137813],[4.3125,9.298828],[4.35,9.46125],[4.3875,9.625078],[4.425,9.790312],[4.4625,9.956953],[4.5,10.125],[4.5375,10.294453],[4.575,10.465313],[4.6125,10.637578],[4.65,10.81125],[4.6875,10.986328],[4.725,11.162812],[4.7625,11.340703],[4.8,11.52],[4.8375,11.700703],[4.875,11.882812],[4.9125,12.066328],[4.95,12.25125],[4.9875,12.437578],[5.025,12.625313],[5.0625,12.814453],[5.1,13.005],[5.1375,13.196953],[5.175,13.390312],[5.2125,13.585078],[5.25,13.78125],[5.2875,13.978828],[5.325,14.177813],[5.3625,14.378203],[5.4,14.58],[5.4375,14.783203],[5.475,14.987812],[5.5125,15.193828],[5.55,15.40125],[5.5875,15.610078],[5.625,15.820312],[5.6625,16.031953],[5.7,16.245],[5.7375,16.459453],[5.775,16.675313],[5.8125,16.892578],[5.85,17.11125],[5.8875,17.331328],[5.925,17.552812],[5.9625,17.775703],[6.0,18.0]],"c":0,"label":"car"},{"pts":[[2,0],[5,12]],"dash":true,"label":"tangent","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[4,8],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-030","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows force against extension for a spring that obeys Hooke's law. Use the graph to find the spring constant of the spring.","o":["1.5 N/m","96 N/m","150 N/m","0.0067 N/m"],"a":2,"x":"Take the gradient: 12 N ÷ 8 cm = 1.5 N/cm. Convert 8 cm to 0.08 m: $k = 12 \\div 0.08 = 150$ N/m. The spring constant must be in N/m.","w":["Found the gradient 1.5 but left the extension in cm, so the unit is N/cm, not N/m.","Multiplied the force and extension read from the graph (12 × 8) instead of dividing.","","Divided extension by force (0.08 ÷ 12), so the gradient is upside down."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,12],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"extension / cm","ylabel":"force / N","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[8,12]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-031","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":1,"q":"The graph shows force against extension for a spring. What is the largest force for which the spring still obeys Hooke's law, so that force is directly proportional to extension?","o":["12 N","6 N","15 N","14 N"],"a":0,"x":"Force is directly proportional to extension only while the line is straight. The line stops being straight at an extension of 6 cm. The force there is 12 N. This is the limit of proportionality.","w":["","Read the extension (6 cm) off the horizontal axis instead of the force off the vertical axis.","Used the highest force on the graph, which is on the curved part, past the limit of proportionality.","Used the first point on the curved part, which is already past the limit of proportionality."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,16],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"extension / cm","ylabel":"force / N","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[2,4],[4,8],[6,12],[8,14],[10,15]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-032","s":"physics","t":"4.5.3","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows force against extension for springs A and B, both within their limit of proportionality. Both are stretched by the same extension. How many times more energy is stored in A than in B?","o":["0.4","6.25","0.16","2.5"],"a":3,"x":"At the same extension, $E = \\frac{1}{2}ke^2$, so the energy ratio equals the ratio of the spring constants. A: 10 ÷ 0.10 = 100 N/m. B: 4 ÷ 0.10 = 40 N/m. 100 ÷ 40 = 2.5.","w":["Divided B's spring constant by A's, so the ratio is upside down.","Squared the ratio of spring constants. The extension is the same, so only $k$ changes.","Squared the ratio and also turned it upside down.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,0.1],"y":[0,10],"xstep":0.02,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"extension / m","ylabel":"force / N","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[0.1,10]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[0.1,4]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-011","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A ball starts with 50 J in its gravitational potential store and falls. The graph shows the energy in two stores at one moment. How much energy has gone to the surroundings so far?","o":["20 J","35 J","5 J","45 J"],"a":2,"x":"Read the bars: gravitational potential 30 J and kinetic 15 J. Together that is 45 J. Energy is conserved, so the rest went to the surroundings: 50 − 45 = 5 J.","w":["You took 50 − 30 and ignored the kinetic bar. That energy is still in a store, not wasted.","You took 50 − 15 and ignored the gravitational potential bar. That energy is still in a store.","","You added the two bars. That is the energy still in the stores, not the energy that has gone to the surroundings."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Gravitational potential","Kinetic"],"vals":[30,15],"y":[0,40],"ystep":5,"xlabel":"energy store","ylabel":"energy in store / J"}}
{"id":"gph-fig-012","s":"physics","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Two heaters, A and B, are switched on together. The graph shows the energy each one transfers over time. How much greater is the power of A than the power of B?","o":["20 W","10 W","30 W","600 W"],"a":1,"x":"Power = energy ÷ time. At 60 s, A has transferred 1200 J, so 1200 ÷ 60 = 20 W. B has transferred 600 J, so 600 ÷ 60 = 10 W. The difference is 20 − 10 = 10 W.","w":["You found the power of A only and did not subtract the power of B.","","You added the two powers instead of finding the difference.","You subtracted the energies (1200 − 600) but did not divide by the time, so this is not a power."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[0,1200],"xstep":10,"ystep":200,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"energy transferred / J","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[60,1200]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[60,600]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-039","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how the acceleration of a trolley changes with the resultant force on it. Use the graph to find the mass of the trolley.","o":["0.5 kg","4 kg","2 kg","8 kg"],"a":2,"x":"At P, force is 8 N and acceleration is 4 m/s². Gradient = 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5, which is 1 ÷ mass. So mass = 1 ÷ 0.5 = 2 kg. Check: a = F ÷ m = 8 ÷ 2 = 4 m/s².","w":["Used the gradient as the mass. On this graph the gradient is 1 ÷ mass, because acceleration is on the y-axis.","Read the acceleration at P (4 m/s²) and called it the mass. Acceleration is not mass.","","Read the force at P (8 N) and called it the mass. Force is not mass."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,6],"xstep":2,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"resultant force / N","ylabel":"acceleration / m/s²","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.075,0.0375],[0.15,0.075],[0.225,0.1125],[0.3,0.15],[0.375,0.1875],[0.45,0.225],[0.525,0.2625],[0.6,0.3],[0.675,0.3375],[0.75,0.375],[0.825,0.4125],[0.9,0.45],[0.975,0.4875],[1.05,0.525],[1.125,0.5625],[1.2,0.6],[1.275,0.6375],[1.35,0.675],[1.425,0.7125],[1.5,0.75],[1.575,0.7875],[1.65,0.825],[1.725,0.8625],[1.8,0.9],[1.875,0.9375],[1.95,0.975],[2.025,1.0125],[2.1,1.05],[2.175,1.0875],[2.25,1.125],[2.325,1.1625],[2.4,1.2],[2.475,1.2375],[2.55,1.275],[2.625,1.3125],[2.7,1.35],[2.775,1.3875],[2.85,1.425],[2.925,1.4625],[3.0,1.5],[3.075,1.5375],[3.15,1.575],[3.225,1.6125],[3.3,1.65],[3.375,1.6875],[3.45,1.725],[3.525,1.7625],[3.6,1.8],[3.675,1.8375],[3.75,1.875],[3.825,1.9125],[3.9,1.95],[3.975,1.9875],[4.05,2.025],[4.125,2.0625],[4.2,2.1],[4.275,2.1375],[4.35,2.175],[4.425,2.2125],[4.5,2.25],[4.575,2.2875],[4.65,2.325],[4.725,2.3625],[4.8,2.4],[4.875,2.4375],[4.95,2.475],[5.025,2.5125],[5.1,2.55],[5.175,2.5875],[5.25,2.625],[5.325,2.6625],[5.4,2.7],[5.475,2.7375],[5.55,2.775],[5.625,2.8125],[5.7,2.85],[5.775,2.8875],[5.85,2.925],[5.925,2.9625],[6.0,3.0],[6.075,3.0375],[6.15,3.075],[6.225,3.1125],[6.3,3.15],[6.375,3.1875],[6.45,3.225],[6.525,3.2625],[6.6,3.3],[6.675,3.3375],[6.75,3.375],[6.825,3.4125],[6.9,3.45],[6.975,3.4875],[7.05,3.525],[7.125,3.5625],[7.2,3.6],[7.275,3.6375],[7.35,3.675],[7.425,3.7125],[7.5,3.75],[7.575,3.7875],[7.65,3.825],[7.725,3.8625],[7.8,3.9],[7.875,3.9375],[7.95,3.975],[8.025,4.0125],[8.1,4.05],[8.175,4.0875],[8.25,4.125],[8.325,4.1625],[8.4,4.2],[8.475,4.2375],[8.55,4.275],[8.625,4.3125],[8.7,4.35],[8.775,4.3875],[8.85,4.425],[8.925,4.4625],[9.0,4.5],[9.075,4.5375],[9.15,4.575],[9.225,4.6125],[9.3,4.65],[9.375,4.6875],[9.45,4.725],[9.525,4.7625],[9.6,4.8],[9.675,4.8375],[9.75,4.875],[9.825,4.9125],[9.9,4.95],[9.975,4.9875],[10.05,5.025],[10.125,5.0625],[10.2,5.1],[10.275,5.1375],[10.35,5.175],[10.425,5.2125],[10.5,5.25],[10.575,5.2875],[10.65,5.325],[10.725,5.3625],[10.8,5.4],[10.875,5.4375],[10.95,5.475],[11.025,5.5125],[11.1,5.55],[11.175,5.5875],[11.25,5.625],[11.325,5.6625],[11.4,5.7],[11.475,5.7375],[11.55,5.775],[11.625,5.8125],[11.7,5.85],[11.775,5.8875],[11.85,5.925],[11.925,5.9625],[12.0,6.0]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[8,4],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gph-fig-040","s":"physics","t":"4.5.6.2","lv":2,"q":"A car has a mass of 800 kg. The graph shows its velocity during a journey. What is the resultant force on the car in the last section of the journey?","o":["6400 N forwards","1600 N backwards","1600 N forwards","3200 N backwards"],"a":1,"x":"In the last section the velocity falls from 8 m/s to 0 m/s in 4 s. Gradient = −8 ÷ 4 = −2 m/s². F = ma = 800 × 2 = 1600 N. The acceleration is negative, so the force acts backwards.","w":["Used the speed (8 m/s) as the acceleration, and ignored that the car is slowing down.","","Found the size correctly but ignored the negative gradient. The car is slowing, so the force is backwards.","Used the time (4 s) as the acceleration instead of finding the gradient."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,14],"y":[0,10],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[4,8],[10,8],[14,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
